-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/story-lead/progress/001-story-lead.progress.jsonl:8:{\\\"storyRunId\\\":\\\"05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone-story-run-001\\\",\\\"sequence\\\":8,\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-06-12T23:31:13.215Z\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"story-lead-self-note-recorded\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Story-lead recorded a durable self-note for a future planner turn.\\\",\\\"data\\\":{\\\"note\\\":\\\"If verifier passes unkeyed/default behavior but keyed run evidence is still absent, do not accept; request ruling or block on the missing keyed proof required by the story DoD.\\\",\\\"actionSequence\\\":7,\\\"actionType\\\":\\\"run-verify\\\",\\\"turn\\\":2}}\\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:1:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"system\\\",\\\"subtype\\\":\\\"init\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\",\\\"tools\\\":[\\\"Task\\\",\\\"AskUserQuestion\\\",\\\"Bash\\\",\\\"CronCreate\\\",\\\"CronDelete\\\",\\\"CronList\\\",\\\"DesignSync\\\",\\\"Edit\\\",\\\"EnterPlanMode\\\",\\\"EnterWorktree\\\",\\\"ExitPlanMode\\\",\\\"ExitWorktree\\\",\\\"Monitor\\\",\\\"NotebookEdit\\\",\\\"PushNotification\\\",\\\"Read\\\",\\\"RemoteTrigger\\\",\\\"ScheduleWakeup\\\",\\\"Skill\\\",\\\"TaskCreate\\\",\\\"TaskGet\\\",\\\"TaskList\\\",\\\"TaskOutput\\\",\\\"TaskStop\\\",\\\"TaskUpdate\\\",\\\"ToolSearch\\\",\\\"WebFetch\\\",\\\"WebSearch\\\",\\\"Workflow\\\",\\\"Write\\\"],\\\"mcp_servers\\\":[],\\\"model\\\":\\\"claude-fable-5\\\",\\\"permissionMode\\\":\\\"bypassPermissions\\\",\\\"slash_commands\\\":[\\\"deep-research\\\",\\\"design-sync\\\",\\\"update-config\\\",\\\"verify\\\",\\\"debug\\\",\\\"code-review\\\",\\\"simplify\\\",\\\"batch\\\",\\\"fewer-permission-prompts\\\",\\\"loop\\\",\\\"schedule\\\",\\\"claude-api\\\",\\\"run\\\",\\\"run-skill-generator\\\",\\\"clear\\\",\\\"compact\\\",\\\"context\\\",\\\"heapdump\\\",\\\"init\\\",\\\"reload-skills\\\",\\\"review\\\",\\\"security-review\\\",\\\"usage-credits\\\",\\\"extra-usage\\\",\\\"usage\\\",\\\"insights\\\",\\\"goal\\\",\\\"team-onboarding\\\"],\\\"apiKeySource\\\":\\\"none\\\",\\\"claude_code_version\\\":\\\"2.1.176\\\",\\\"output_style\\\":\\\"default\\\",\\\"agents\\\":[\\\"claude\\\",\\\"Explore\\\",\\\"general-purpose\\\",\\\"Plan\\\",\\\"statusline-setup\\\"],\\\"skills\\\":[\\\"deep-research\\\",\\\"design-sync\\\",\\\"update-config\\\",\\\"verify\\\",\\\"debug\\\",\\\"code-review\\\",\\\"simplify\\\",\\\"batch\\\",\\\"fewer-permission-prompts\\\",\\\"loop\\\",\\\"schedule\\\",\\\"claude-api\\\",\\\"run\\\",\\\"run-skill-generator\\\"],\\\"plugins\\\":[],\\\"analytics_disabled\\\":false,\\\"product_feedback_disabled\\\":false,\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"c0b47ffb-5c98-42ae-862b-60dbc506d3b4\\\",\\\"memory_paths\\\":{\\\"auto\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/.claude/projects/-Users-leemoore-code-pi-long-horizon-liminal-context/memory/\\\"},\\\"fast_mode_state\\\":\\\"off\\\"}\\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:20:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"toolu_01LBS5nZHWD4DpjnGddkxSFn\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"1\\\\t# Story 5: Real-Inference Suite and Capstone\\\\n2\\\\t\\\\n3\\\\t### Summary\\\\n4\\\\t<!-- Jira: Summary field -->\\\\n5\\\\t\\\\n6\\\\tAdd the opt-in OpenRouter-backed suite with visible ran/not-ran accounting and the real-adapter lifecycle capstone.\\\\n7\\\\t\\\\n8\\\\t### Description\\\\n9\\\\t<!-- Jira: Description field -->\\\\n10\\\\t\\\\n11\\\\t**User Profile:** The operator needs proof that the inference seam works against a real model while CI remains network-free by default.\\\\n12\\\\t\\\\n13\\\\t**Objective:** Verify all seven derivation kinds and the Epic 04 lifecycle sequence through a real endpoint when keyed, with explicit not-ran accounting when unkeyed.\\\\n14\\\\t\\\\n15\\\\t**Scope In:** Test-owned OpenRouter `ModelCall`, suite guard, ran/not-ran output, seven real round-trips, shared seam-conformance assertions, and lifecycle capstone structural checks.\\\\n16\\\\t\\\\n17\\\\t**Scope Out:** Prompt quality evaluation, model/prompt dial-in, PI extension wiring, and the full integrated harness.\\\\n18\\\\t\\\\n19\\\\t**Dependencies:** Stories 2-4 provide the seam, adapter, prompts, provenance, and failure classification.\\\\n20\\\\t\\\\n21\\\\t### Acceptance Criteria\\\\n22\\\\t<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\\\\n23\\\\t\\\\n24\\\\t- **AC-4.1**: The real-inference suite is opt-in on key presence and its outcome is always visible: ran (with the model assignments used) or not-ran (with reason) in the suite output. Absence of the key can never produce a silent pass. The suite's model-call function lives in test code, implements the AC-1.2 contract, and reaches a real endpoint.\\\\n25\\\\t- **AC-4.2**: The capstone: the Epic 04 lifecycle sequence with the real adapter completes with every derivation kind landing `ready` at least once — non-empty content, no deterministic-marker strings, provenance naming the real model — and the deterministic leg's checkpoint-coherence assertions hold structurally (cleared-then-ready around mutations; second compact reflects post-edit content).\\\\n26\\\\t\\\\n27\\\\t**Test Conditions**\\\\n28\\\\t\\\\n29\\\\t- **TC-4.1** (AC-4.1): `inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n30\\\\t  - with `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`, each of the seven kinds round-trips real inference once\\\\n31\\\\t  - each result lands `ready`, has non-empty content, and contains no deterministic marker pattern\\\\n32\\\\t  - with the key absent in a controlled leg, the suite emits a not-ran record with reason\\\\n33\\\\t  - the not-ran record is distinguishable from a pass\\\\n34\\\\t  - the suite-level guard emits exactly one ran/not-ran line into run output\\\\n35\\\\t- **TC-4.2** (AC-4.2): `inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n36\\\\t  - run Epic 04's lifecycle sequence against the real adapter: intake, drain, compact, pull, inspect, edit, rebuild, drain, compact, materialize\\\\n37\\\\t  - every form kind appears `ready` at least once\\\\n38\\\\t  - no deterministic marker pattern appears anywhere\\\\n39\\\\t  - provenance names the real model\\\\n40\\\\t  - mutation-cleared forms regenerate with content different from pre-edit content\\\\n41\\\\t  - second compact's view reflects post-edit content\\\\n42\\\\t  - health is coherent at each checkpoint\\\\n43\\\\t- **TC-4.3** (AC-1.2, AC-4.1): `inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n44\\\\t  - `assertModelCallContract(openRouterCall)` runs against the real host function\\\\n45\\\\t  - `assertRoutingThroughSdk(openRouterCall, realAssignments)` runs the same routing helper used by Story 2's fake-host seam test, unchanged\\\\n46\\\\t\\\\n47\\\\t### Technical Design\\\\n48\\\\t<!-- Jira: Technical Notes or sub-section of Description -->\\\\n49\\\\t\\\\n50\\\\t#### Architecture Context\\\\n51\\\\t\\\\n52\\\\tThis story proves the same `ModelCall` seam against a real endpoint without making CI depend on network credentials. The OpenRouter function is test-owned host code, not production LHC transport code, and exists to prove that the injected-function contract is sufficient.\\\\n53\\\\t\\\\n54\\\\tThe capstone reuses the Epic 04 lifecycle sequence with real-adapter structural assertions: ready forms, non-marker content, real-model provenance, mutation regeneration, and coherent checkpoints.\\\\n55\\\\t\\\\n56\\\\t#### Build Strategy\\\\n57\\\\t\\\\n58\\\\tStrategy: full-staged-risk\\\\n59\\\\t\\\\n60\\\\tReason:\\\\n61\\\\t- The story has an external endpoint, auth-gated execution, visible accounting, and a capstone path spanning intake, drain, compact, mutation, rebuild, and materialization.\\\\n62\\\\t- The not-ran path is as important as the keyed path because silent pass is the failure mode.\\\\n63\\\\t\\\\n64\\\\tRisk Reminders:\\\\n65\\\\t- CI-default run must make zero network calls.\\\\n66\\\\t- The suite guard must report one visible ran/not-ran fact.\\\\n67\\\\t- Keyed run evidence must be recorded in story completion notes.\\\\n68\\\\t\\\\n69\\\\t#### Implementation Targets\\\\n70\\\\t\\\\n71\\\\t| Area | Files / Modules |\\\\n72\\\\t|---|---|\\\\n73\\\\t| Real host fixture | `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts` |\\\\n74\\\\t| Shared seam helpers | `test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts` |\\\\n75\\\\t| Real suite | `test/inference-real.test.ts` |\\\\n76\\\\t| Verify accounting | `packages/lhc/package.json` scripts |\\\\n77\\\\t| Lifecycle fixtures | existing Epic 04 lifecycle test helpers |\\\\n78\\\\t\\\\n79\\\\t#### Design References\\\\n80\\\\t\\\\n81\\\\t- [epic.md §Flow 4](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:134), lines 134-149\\\\n82\\\\t- [epic.md §Non-Functional Requirements](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:240), lines 240-246\\\\n83\\\\t- [tech-design.md §Design Decisions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:32), line 32\\\\n84\\\\t- [tech-design.md §Flow 4](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:169), lines 169-173\\\\n85\\\\t- [tech-design.md §Runtime Prerequisites](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:293), lines 293-301\\\\n86\\\\t- [tech-design.md §Testing Strategy](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:303), lines 303-309\\\\n87\\\\t- [test-plan.md §Fixture Contracts](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:34), lines 34-36\\\\n88\\\\t- [test-plan.md §TC-4.1 / TC-4.2 / TC-4.3](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:91), lines 91-104\\\\n89\\\\t- [test-plan.md §Story-completion accounting](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:166), line 166\\\\n90\\\\t- [coverage.md §Story Shape Review](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/stories/coverage.md:76), line 76\\\\n91\\\\t\\\\n92\\\\t#### Test Mapping\\\\n93\\\\t\\\\n94\\\\t| TC | Test File / Check | Test Description |\\\\n95\\\\t|---|---|---|\\\\n96\\\\t| TC-4.1 | `test/inference-real.test.ts` | keyed seven-kind real round-trips; unkeyed not-ran record; exactly one suite-level ran/not-ran output line |\\\\n97\\\\t| TC-4.2 | `test/inference-real.test.ts` | Epic 04 lifecycle sequence under real adapter with structural checkpoint assertions |\\\\n98\\\\t| TC-4.3 | `test/inference-real.test.ts` | OpenRouter host passes shared `ModelCall` contract and SDK routing helpers |\\\\n99\\\\t\\\\n100\\\\t#### Architecture-Risk Tests\\\\n101\\\\t\\\\n102\\\\t| Risk | Test File / Check | Test Description | Why AC/TC Mapping Alone Would Miss It |\\\\n103\\\\t|---|---|---|---|\\\\n104\\\\t| Silent real-suite skip | `test/inference-real.test.ts` unkeyed guard leg | Not-ran record has reason and cannot look like pass | A skipped keyed suite could leave default CI green without proving anything |\\\\n105\\\\t| Fixture does not represent host boundary | `test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts` helpers | Same contract/routing helpers run against fake and real host functions | A direct OpenRouter call could pass while violating the SDK boundary shape |\\\\n106\\\\t| Capstone only checks one derivation | lifecycle structural assertions | Every form kind reaches ready at least once with real provenance | A single ready result would not prove the whole derivation catalog works |\\\\n107\\\\t\\\\n108\\\\t#### Technical Notes\\\\n109\\\\t\\\\n110\\\\tRelevant contract:\\\\n111\\\\t\\\\n112\\\\t```ts\\\\n113\\\\texport type ModelCall = (input: {\\\\n114\\\\t  provider: string;\\\\n115\\\\t  model: string;\\\\n116\\\\t  messages: { role: \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\"; content: string }[];\\\\n117\\\\t}) => Promise<\\\\n118\\\\t  | { ok: true; text: string }\\\\n119\\\\t  | { ok: false; kind: ModelCallFailureKind; message: string }\\\\n120\\\\t>;\\\\n121\\\\t```\\\\n122\\\\t\\\\n123\\\\tRuntime prerequisites:\\\\n124\\\\t\\\\n125\\\\t| Prerequisite | Where | Verification |\\\\n126\\\\t|---|---|---|\\\\n127\\\\t| `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY` | opt-in suite only | suite reports ran or not-ran |\\\\n128\\\\t| `LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL` | opt-in suite optional | fixture default used when unset |\\\\n129\\\\t| native `fetch` | test fixture | Node runtime already provides it |\\\\n130\\\\t\\\\n131\\\\tReal-suite fixtures:\\\\n132\\\\t\\\\n133\\\\t| Fixture | Responsibility |\\\\n134\\\\t|---|---|\\\\n135\\\\t| `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts` | `createOpenRouterCall(key, defaultModel)` implements `ModelCall` over OpenRouter using plain `fetch` |\\\\n136\\\\t| `resolveRealSuiteEnv()` | returns `{ key, model }` or `{ notRan: reason }` |\\\\n137\\\\t| `test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts` | shared `assertModelCallContract` and `assertRoutingThroughSdk` helpers |\\\\n138\\\\t\\\\n139\\\\tOpenRouter failure mapping:\\\\n140\\\\t\\\\n141\\\\t| Source | Failure kind |\\\\n142\\\\t|---|---|\\\\n143\\\\t| HTTP 401/403 | `auth` |\\\\n144\\\\t| HTTP 429 | `rate_limit` |\\\\n145\\\\t| HTTP 400 | `invalid_request` |\\\\n146\\\\t| network error | `network` |\\\\n147\\\\t| other non-success | `other` |\\\\n148\\\\t\\\\n149\\\\tThe CI-default suite makes zero network calls. The keyed run is recorded in story completion notes with date, model, and pass state.\\\\n150\\\\t\\\\n151\\\\t#### Runtime Contract Assumptions\\\\n152\\\\t\\\\n153\\\\t- `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY` controls keyed execution.\\\\n154\\\\t- `LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL` may select the cheap model; fixture default applies when unset.\\\\n155\\\\t- OpenRouter provider/model interpretation is host-side and still returns the AC-1.2 `ModelCallResult` shape.\\\\n156\\\\t\\\\n157\\\\t#### Anti-Shim Requirements\\\\n158\\\\t\\\\n159\\\\t- Do not use deterministic provider output in the real-suite ready assertions.\\\\n160\\\\t- Do not hide missing key with test skip semantics indistinguishable from pass.\\\\n161\\\\t- Do not mock `fetch` in the keyed leg; the point is real endpoint proof.\\\\n162\\\\t- Do not assert output quality; assert structure, non-marker content, provenance, and checkpoint coherence.\\\\n163\\\\t\\\\n164\\\\t#### Production Path Proof\\\\n165\\\\t\\\\n166\\\\t- Entrypoint: `test/inference-real.test.ts` constructs SDK with a real host `ModelCall`.\\\\n167\\\\t- Registration/default path: env-gated suite in default runner reports ran/not-ran; keyed execution reaches OpenRouter through plain `fetch`.\\\\n168\\\\t- Evidence: keyed seven-kind round-trips, shared seam-conformance helpers, and real-adapter lifecycle capstone.\\\\n169\\\\t\\\\n170\\\\t#### Verification\\\\n171\\\\t\\\\n172\\\\t- Targeted: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm exec vitest run test/inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n173\\\\t- Story gate: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm run red-verify && pnpm exec vitest run test/inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n174\\\\t- Epic gate: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm run verify-all`\\\\n175\\\\t\\\\n176\\\\t#### Spec Deviations\\\\n177\\\\t\\\\n178\\\\tNone.\\\\n179\\\\t\\\\n180\\\\t### Definition of Done\\\\n181\\\\t<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n182\\\\t\\\\n183\\\\t- OpenRouter-backed test `ModelCall` fixture exists and conforms to AC-1.2.\\\\n184\\\\t- Suite guard emits exactly one visible ran/not-ran record and unkeyed absence cannot look like a pass.\\\\n185\\\\t- Keyed seven-kind real round-trip passes with non-empty, non-marker ready forms and real-model provenance.\\\\n186\\\\t- Real-adapter lifecycle capstone passes structural checkpoint assertions.\\\\n187\\\\t- TC-4.1, TC-4.2, and TC-4.3 are green in the keyed local run; unkeyed not-ran accounting is green in default CI.\\\\n188\\\\t- Story completion notes record keyed run date, model, and pass state.\\\\n189\\\\t\\\"}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"de11cb76-781d-428d-a345-04e7ab08e7ca\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-06-12T23:20:40.828Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"file\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/stories/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone.md\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"# Story 5: Real-Inference Suite and Capstone\\\\n\\\\n### Summary\\\\n<!-- Jira: Summary field -->\\\\n\\\\nAdd the opt-in OpenRouter-backed suite with visible ran/not-ran accounting and the real-adapter lifecycle capstone.\\\\n\\\\n### Description\\\\n<!-- Jira: Description field -->\\\\n\\\\n**User Profile:** The operator needs proof that the inference seam works against a real model while CI remains network-free by default.\\\\n\\\\n**Objective:** Verify all seven derivation kinds and the Epic 04 lifecycle sequence through a real endpoint when keyed, with explicit not-ran accounting when unkeyed.\\\\n\\\\n**Scope In:** Test-owned OpenRouter `ModelCall`, suite guard, ran/not-ran output, seven real round-trips, shared seam-conformance assertions, and lifecycle capstone structural checks.\\\\n\\\\n**Scope Out:** Prompt quality evaluation, model/prompt dial-in, PI extension wiring, and the full integrated harness.\\\\n\\\\n**Dependencies:** Stories 2-4 provide the seam, adapter, prompts, provenance, and failure classification.\\\\n\\\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\\\n<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\\\\n\\\\n- **AC-4.1**: The real-inference suite is opt-in on key presence and its outcome is always visible: ran (with the model assignments used) or not-ran (with reason) in the suite output. Absence of the key can never produce a silent pass. The suite's model-call function lives in test code, implements the AC-1.2 contract, and reaches a real endpoint.\\\\n- **AC-4.2**: The capstone: the Epic 04 lifecycle sequence with the real adapter completes with every derivation kind landing `ready` at least once — non-empty content, no deterministic-marker strings, provenance naming the real model — and the deterministic leg's checkpoint-coherence assertions hold structurally (cleared-then-ready around mutations; second compact reflects post-edit content).\\\\n\\\\n**Test Conditions**\\\\n\\\\n- **TC-4.1** (AC-4.1): `inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n  - with `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`, each of the seven kinds round-trips real inference once\\\\n  - each result lands `ready`, has non-empty content, and contains no deterministic marker pattern\\\\n  - with the key absent in a controlled leg, the suite emits a not-ran record with reason\\\\n  - the not-ran record is distinguishable from a pass\\\\n  - the suite-level guard emits exactly one ran/not-ran line into run output\\\\n- **TC-4.2** (AC-4.2): `inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n  - run Epic 04's lifecycle sequence against the real adapter: intake, drain, compact, pull, inspect, edit, rebuild, drain, compact, materialize\\\\n  - every form kind appears `ready` at least once\\\\n  - no deterministic marker pattern appears anywhere\\\\n  - provenance names the real model\\\\n  - mutation-cleared forms regenerate with content different from pre-edit content\\\\n  - second compact's view reflects post-edit content\\\\n  - health is coherent at each checkpoint\\\\n- **TC-4.3** (AC-1.2, AC-4.1): `inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n  - `assertModelCallContract(openRouterCall)` runs against the real host function\\\\n  - `assertRoutingThroughSdk(openRouterCall, realAssignments)` runs the same routing helper used by Story 2's fake-host seam test, unchanged\\\\n\\\\n### Technical Design\\\\n<!-- Jira: Technical Notes or sub-section of Description -->\\\\n\\\\n#### Architecture Context\\\\n\\\\nThis story proves the same `ModelCall` seam against a real endpoint without making CI depend on network credentials. The OpenRouter function is test-owned host code, not production LHC transport code, and exists to prove that the injected-function contract is sufficient.\\\\n\\\\nThe capstone reuses the Epic 04 lifecycle sequence with real-adapter structural assertions: ready forms, non-marker content, real-model provenance, mutation regeneration, and coherent checkpoints.\\\\n\\\\n#### Build Strategy\\\\n\\\\nStrategy: full-staged-risk\\\\n\\\\nReason:\\\\n- The story has an external endpoint, auth-gated execution, visible accounting, and a capstone path spanning intake, drain, compact, mutation, rebuild, and materialization.\\\\n- The not-ran path is as important as the keyed path because silent pass is the failure mode.\\\\n\\\\nRisk Reminders:\\\\n- CI-default run must make zero network calls.\\\\n- The suite guard must report one visible ran/not-ran fact.\\\\n- Keyed run evidence must be recorded in story completion notes.\\\\n\\\\n#### Implementation Targets\\\\n\\\\n| Area | Files / Modules |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| Real host fixture | `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts` |\\\\n| Shared seam helpers | `test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts` |\\\\n| Real suite | `test/inference-real.test.ts` |\\\\n| Verify accounting | `packages/lhc/package.json` scripts |\\\\n| Lifecycle fixtures | existing Epic 04 lifecycle test helpers |\\\\n\\\\n#### Design References\\\\n\\\\n- [epic.md §Flow 4](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:134), lines 134-149\\\\n- [epic.md §Non-Functional Requirements](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/epic.md:240), lines 240-246\\\\n- [tech-design.md §Design Decisions](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:32), line 32\\\\n- [tech-design.md §Flow 4](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:169), lines 169-173\\\\n- [tech-design.md §Runtime Prerequisites](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:293), lines 293-301\\\\n- [tech-design.md §Testing Strategy](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md:303), lines 303-309\\\\n- [test-plan.md §Fixture Contracts](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:34), lines 34-36\\\\n- [test-plan.md §TC-4.1 / TC-4.2 / TC-4.3](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:91), lines 91-104\\\\n- [test-plan.md §Story-completion accounting](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md:166), line 166\\\\n- [coverage.md §Story Shape Review](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/stories/coverage.md:76), line 76\\\\n\\\\n#### Test Mapping\\\\n\\\\n| TC | Test File / Check | Test Description |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| TC-4.1 | `test/inference-real.test.ts` | keyed seven-kind real round-trips; unkeyed not-ran record; exactly one suite-level ran/not-ran output line |\\\\n| TC-4.2 | `test/inference-real.test.ts` | Epic 04 lifecycle sequence under real adapter with structural checkpoint assertions |\\\\n| TC-4.3 | `test/inference-real.test.ts` | OpenRouter host passes shared `ModelCall` contract and SDK routing helpers |\\\\n\\\\n#### Architecture-Risk Tests\\\\n\\\\n| Risk | Test File / Check | Test Description | Why AC/TC Mapping Alone Would Miss It |\\\\n|---|---|---|---|\\\\n| Silent real-suite skip | `test/inference-real.test.ts` unkeyed guard leg | Not-ran record has reason and cannot look like pass | A skipped keyed suite could leave default CI green without proving anything |\\\\n| Fixture does not represent host boundary | `test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts` helpers | Same contract/routing helpers run against fake and real host functions | A direct OpenRouter call could pass while violating the SDK boundary shape |\\\\n| Capstone only checks one derivation | lifecycle structural assertions | Every form kind reaches ready at least once with real provenance | A single ready result would not prove the whole derivation catalog works |\\\\n\\\\n#### Technical Notes\\\\n\\\\nRelevant contract:\\\\n\\\\n```ts\\\\nexport type ModelCall = (input: {\\\\n  provider: string;\\\\n  model: string;\\\\n  messages: { role: \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\"; content: string }[];\\\\n}) => Promise<\\\\n  | { ok: true; text: string }\\\\n  | { ok: false; kind: ModelCallFailureKind; message: string }\\\\n>;\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nRuntime prerequisites:\\\\n\\\\n| Prerequisite | Where | Verification |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY` | opt-in suite only | suite reports ran or not-ran |\\\\n| `LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL` | opt-in suite optional | fixture default used when unset |\\\\n| native `fetch` | test fixture | Node runtime already provides it |\\\\n\\\\nReal-suite fixtures:\\\\n\\\\n| Fixture | Responsibility |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts` | `createOpenRouterCall(key, defaultModel)` implements `ModelCall` over OpenRouter using plain `fetch` |\\\\n| `resolveRealSuiteEnv()` | returns `{ key, model }` or `{ notRan: reason }` |\\\\n| `test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts` | shared `assertModelCallContract` and `assertRoutingThroughSdk` helpers |\\\\n\\\\nOpenRouter failure mapping:\\\\n\\\\n| Source | Failure kind |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| HTTP 401/403 | `auth` |\\\\n| HTTP 429 | `rate_limit` |\\\\n| HTTP 400 | `invalid_request` |\\\\n| network error | `network` |\\\\n| other non-success | `other` |\\\\n\\\\nThe CI-default suite makes zero network calls. The keyed run is recorded in story completion notes with date, model, and pass state.\\\\n\\\\n#### Runtime Contract Assumptions\\\\n\\\\n- `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY` controls keyed execution.\\\\n- `LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL` may select the cheap model; fixture default applies when unset.\\\\n- OpenRouter provider/model interpretation is host-side and still returns the AC-1.2 `ModelCallResult` shape.\\\\n\\\\n#### Anti-Shim Requirements\\\\n\\\\n- Do not use deterministic provider output in the real-suite ready assertions.\\\\n- Do not hide missing key with test skip semantics indistinguishable from pass.\\\\n- Do not mock `fetch` in the keyed leg; the point is real endpoint proof.\\\\n- Do not assert output quality; assert structure, non-marker content, provenance, and checkpoint coherence.\\\\n\\\\n#### Production Path Proof\\\\n\\\\n- Entrypoint: `test/inference-real.test.ts` constructs SDK with a real host `ModelCall`.\\\\n- Registration/default path: env-gated suite in default runner reports ran/not-ran; keyed execution reaches OpenRouter through plain `fetch`.\\\\n- Evidence: keyed seven-kind round-trips, shared seam-conformance helpers, and real-adapter lifecycle capstone.\\\\n\\\\n#### Verification\\\\n\\\\n- Targeted: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm exec vitest run test/inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n- Story gate: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm run red-verify && pnpm exec vitest run test/inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n- Epic gate: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm run verify-all`\\\\n\\\\n#### Spec Deviations\\\\n\\\\nNone.\\\\n\\\\n### Definition of Done\\\\n<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\n\\\\n- OpenRouter-backed test `ModelCall` fixture exists and conforms to AC-1.2.\\\\n- Suite guard emits exactly one visible ran/not-ran record and unkeyed absence cannot look like a pass.\\\\n- Keyed seven-kind real round-trip passes with non-empty, non-marker ready forms and real-model provenance.\\\\n- Real-adapter lifecycle capstone passes structural checkpoint assertions.\\\\n- TC-4.1, TC-4.2, and TC-4.3 are green in the keyed local run; unkeyed not-ran accounting is green in default CI.\\\\n- Story completion notes record keyed run date, model, and pass state.\\\\n\\\",\\\"numLines\\\":189,\\\"startLine\\\":1,\\\"totalLines\\\":189}}}\\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:26:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"toolu_018rZ1s1NYyy8kGrNn7Kfhp9\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"1\\\\t# Epic 05: Derivation Inference — Tech Design\\\\n2\\\\t\\\\n3\\\\t**Epic:** `epic.md` (22 ACs / 15 TCs across 6 flows)\\\\n4\\\\t**Test Plan:** `test-plan.md` (full TC→test mapping, Red/Green detail)\\\\n5\\\\t**Tech Arch:** `../01-tech-arch.md`\\\\n6\\\\t**Consumed contracts:** Epic 02 provider seam and queue machinery (as built, deviations closed), Epic 03 visibility boundary (Flow 5 patches its contract), Epic 04 lifecycle exercise (as built; deviation state — e.g. overview boundary from `pull.meta` — is the consumed truth)\\\\n7\\\\t\\\\n8\\\\t## Context\\\\n9\\\\t\\\\n10\\\\tFour epics built a derivation pipeline that has never made a model call. The seam was designed for this moment: `DerivationProvider` is an interface with seven semantic operations, the deterministic provider implements it with marker strings, and every handler, queue row, retry path, and state transition in `domains/` is provider-agnostic. This epic builds the second implementation of that interface — the real one — plus the injection path that lets a host supply model access without LHC ever touching a credential.\\\\n11\\\\t\\\\n12\\\\tThe design splits inference into two layers with different owners. The **semantic layer** is LHC's and lives in a new `src/inference/` area: seven prompt templates, output shaping, per-kind model assignment, failure classification, provenance stamping. The **transport layer** is the host's and crosses the boundary as exactly one function: `(provider, model, messages) → text or structured failure`. The host's function is the only code that knows what `\\\\\\\"anthropic\\\\\\\"` means; LHC treats provider and model strings as opaque routing keys carried from config to call. This is the seam decision recorded in `post-epic-4-work.md`, and it is why the PI extension's eventual contribution is ~15 lines while the seven prompts stay tunable inside LHC without touching any host.\\\\n13\\\\t\\\\n14\\\\tTwo riders land in the same epic because they consolidate decisions the inference work exposes. The CLI retirement deletes the one consumer of the named-provider registry and env-resolution path, which means provider arrival collapses to a single mechanism — injection at `createSdk` — exactly as the new `inference` config option arrives. Doing these together means the construction-validation story is written once against the final shape rather than patched twice. The boundary-advance patch is independent of inference entirely; it rides because it is small, fully validated in the POC (commits `1cf2dc45`, `6a9aa7a4`, `f12a850d` are the reference implementation), and it shares this pack's spec cycle rather than deserving its own.\\\\n15\\\\t\\\\n16\\\\tOne thing this design deliberately does not do: chase output quality. The prompts ship structurally sound and explicitly pre-dial-in; the dial-in working period that follows the integrated-harness pairing produces the tuned prompts and model choices, and its findings backfill this pack before acceptance. Every TC in this epic asserts structure, routing, classification, and provenance — never whether a summary is good.\\\\n17\\\\t\\\\n18\\\\t## Design Decisions\\\\n19\\\\t\\\\n20\\\\t- **DD-1: One transport function, not seven.** The host injects a single `ModelCall`; per-kind knowledge (which provider/model/prompt) lives in LHC config. Injecting seven functions would move LHC's derivation catalog into every host's wiring and make an eighth kind a breaking host change. The function takes the provider string per call, so multi-lane hosts route inside one function.\\\\n21\\\\t- **DD-2: The adapter is a `DerivationProvider`.** No seam movement. `createInferenceProvider(inference, config)` returns the same interface the deterministic provider implements; `createSdk` and everything below it see a provider, full stop. Handlers, queue, drain, states: zero changes for Flows 1–4 (except the one-line provenance copy, DD-4).\\\\n22\\\\t- **DD-3: Classification maps onto the existing `ProviderResult`.** Epic 02's failure shape is already `{ ok: false, retryable, reason }`. The classification table is a pure function `ModelCallFailureKind → { retryable, reason }` where `reason` is the kind string itself — stable, machine-readable, and the queue machinery consumes `retryable` exactly as it always has. No queue changes, no new states.\\\\n23\\\\t- **DD-4: Provenance rides the `ProviderResult` and is copied, never authored.** The ok branch gains `provenance?: { provider, model, prompt }`. The adapter stamps it (it alone knows the assignment); handlers copy it into `DerivedFormMetadata.provenance` alongside the existing mechanical stamps. The deterministic provider simply never sets it. This keeps the \\\\\\\"metadata is mechanically stamped\\\\\\\" invariant: provenance comes from config-known strings, not model output.\\\\n24\\\\t- **DD-5: Construction is `provider` XOR `inference`.** `createSdk` accepts exactly one. The `inference` path validates the assignment map (all seven `FormKind`s present, every prompt name known) and builds the adapter; the `provider` path is unchanged and keeps the deterministic provider as the test default. Both/neither is a `TypeError` at construction, matching the existing config-mistakes-throw contract.\\\\n25\\\\t- **DD-6: The adapter owns a per-call timeout.** Epic tech-design question 2, resolved: a hung host function must not stall a drain, and the host shouldn't have to remember a duty LHC can enforce. `Promise.race` against `inference.timeoutMs` (default 60 000); a timeout classifies as `timeout` (retryable). A host that resolves later resolves into nothing.\\\\n26\\\\t- **DD-7: Input bounding is an adapter seam with a generous default.** Question 3, resolved: `summarizeToolResult` content over `inference.maxInputChars` (default 200 000) is truncated head-and-tail with a marker before prompt rendering. The bound exists so a pathological tool result cannot blow a small-context model; the value is a dial-in tunable, the seam is design.\\\\n27\\\\t- **DD-8: Prompts are modules in a name-keyed registry.** Question 1, resolved: `src/inference/prompts/` holds one module per template exporting `{ name, render(input) → messages }`; `prompts/index.ts` assembles the registry. Config selects by name; dial-in swaps by adding a module and editing config — no handler, adapter, or host changes. Versioning is in the name (`smoothing-v1`).\\\\n28\\\\t- **DD-9: The turn-end trigger is a gate in the pipeline, not a new seam.** Question 4, resolved: `pipeline.ts` already registers the advance `onCommit`, ordered before the queue poke, throw-isolated, both host modes. The change is registering it only when the batch's walk saw a `turn_end` event. Ordering against the queue poke is untouched — same registration site, same pinned order. The advance still runs at flush strictly after intake's COMMIT, in its own short transaction.\\\\n29\\\\t- **DD-10: Whole-turn eviction via `turn_id` grouping; turnless results degrade to singleton groups.** The zone walk groups tool results by `turn_id` (the message column), oldest group first by the group's lowest `source_event_order`. A `turn_id IS NULL` tool result (turnless intake is legal) is its own group — whole-turn semantics degenerate to whole-message for messages that belong to no turn, which preserves the epic's \\\\\\\"no turn is ever partially flipped\\\\\\\" without inventing membership.\\\\n30\\\\t- **DD-11: Floor retirement is a config-surface change, not a migration.** `VisibilityBudgets` becomes `{ maxTokens, targetTokens }` (64 000 / 32 000 defaults); `floorTokens` is deleted from the type, resolution, and validation. No schema change — `view_boundary` is untouched; the decision function changes shape, the storage doesn't. The protected floor's *job* survives structurally: the newest closed turn group is never evicted, and the open turn is invisible to a check that only runs at turn close.\\\\n31\\\\t- **DD-12: CLI deletion removes the registry, keeps the deterministic provider.** `src/providers/registry.ts` (named resolution, `LHC_PROVIDER`) dies with its only consumer. `src/providers/deterministic.ts` stays — it is the CI-default test provider and the byte-stable fixture substrate. The twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` suites delete; `verify-all` drops the `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate and gains the opt-in inference suite (which self-reports ran/not-ran).\\\\n32\\\\t- **DD-13: The real-inference suite is a second host of the same contract.** The OpenRouter-backed `ModelCall` lives in `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts`, implements the AC-1.2 contract over plain `fetch`, and runs Flow 1's construction/routing assertions parameterized (question 5, resolved: shared assertion helpers parameterized over the function, not duplicated tests). Key arrives via `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`; absence produces an explicit not-ran record, never a silent pass.\\\\n33\\\\t\\\\n34\\\\t## Top-Tier Surfaces\\\\n35\\\\t\\\\n36\\\\t| Surface | Source | This Epic's Role |\\\\n37\\\\t|---------|--------|------------------|\\\\n38\\\\t| SDK assembly (`src/sdk.ts`) | Tech arch | Gains the `inference` construction path (DD-5); loses CLI-only exports |\\\\n39\\\\t| `shared/` | Tech arch | `derivation.ts` gains provenance on `ProviderResult`/metadata; `view.ts` loses `floorTokens` |\\\\n40\\\\t| `inference/` (new, peer of `providers/`) | This epic | The semantic layer: adapter, classification, prompts. Not a domain — it implements the provider seam, owns no thread state, queues no work |\\\\n41\\\\t| `domains/thread-view` | Tech arch | `boundary.ts` decision reshape (DD-10); seam/registration untouched except the gate (DD-9) |\\\\n42\\\\t| `domains/intake-stream` | Tech arch | One-line gate on the advance registration (DD-9) |\\\\n43\\\\t| `domains/messages`, `domains/turns` | Tech arch | One-line provenance copy in each handler's form write (DD-4) |\\\\n44\\\\t| `cli/`, `providers/registry` | Tech arch (retiring) | Deleted (DD-12) |\\\\n45\\\\t\\\\n46\\\\t`inference/` must not import `domains/`; it depends only on `shared/` types. The boundaries check gains that rule.\\\\n47\\\\t\\\\n48\\\\t## Module Architecture\\\\n49\\\\t\\\\n50\\\\t```\\\\n51\\\\tsrc/\\\\n52\\\\t├── inference/                          # NEW: the semantic layer (DD-1..DD-8)\\\\n53\\\\t│   ├── types.ts                        # NEW: ModelCall, ModelCallResult, ModelCallFailureKind,\\\\n54\\\\t│   │                                   #      InferenceConfig, ModelAssignment, ResolvedInferenceConfig\\\\n55\\\\t│   ├── classify.ts                     # NEW: classification table + exception containment (DD-3)\\\\n56\\\\t│   ├── adapter.ts                      # NEW: createInferenceProvider — DerivationProvider impl (DD-2)\\\\n57\\\\t│   └── prompts/\\\\n58\\\\t│       ├── index.ts                    # NEW: PROMPT_REGISTRY (name → PromptTemplate) (DD-8)\\\\n59\\\\t│       ├── smoothing-v1.ts             # NEW: ported from POC, settled\\\\n60\\\\t│       ├── tool-call-v1.ts             # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n61\\\\t│       ├── tool-result-v1.ts           # NEW: pre-dial-in (input bounding lives here-adjacent, DD-7)\\\\n62\\\\t│       ├── turn-compose-v1.ts          # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n63\\\\t│       ├── lower-band-v1.ts            # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n64\\\\t│       ├── chunk-detailed-v1.ts        # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n65\\\\t│       └── chunk-brief-v1.ts           # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n66\\\\t├── sdk.ts                              # MODIFIED: provider XOR inference; assignment validation;\\\\n67\\\\t│                                       #           export cleanup (registry exports removed)\\\\n68\\\\t├── shared/\\\\n69\\\\t│   ├── derivation.ts                   # MODIFIED: ProviderResult ok-branch + DerivedFormMetadata\\\\n70\\\\t│   │                                   #           gain provenance? (DD-4)\\\\n71\\\\t│   └── view.ts                         # MODIFIED: VisibilityBudgets → { maxTokens, targetTokens },\\\\n72\\\\t│                                       #           defaults 64000/32000 (DD-11)\\\\n73\\\\t├── domains/\\\\n74\\\\t│   ├── intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts   # MODIFIED: advance registration gated on turn_end (DD-9)\\\\n75\\\\t│   ├── thread-view/internal/boundary.ts     # MODIFIED: turn-grouped zone, peek-ahead decision (DD-10)\\\\n76\\\\t│   ├── messages/internal/…handlers          # MODIFIED: copy result.provenance → metadata (DD-4)\\\\n77\\\\t│   └── turns/internal/…handlers             # MODIFIED: same one-line copy (DD-4)\\\\n78\\\\t├── providers/\\\\n79\\\\t│   ├── deterministic.ts                # EXISTS: unchanged — CI-default test provider\\\\n80\\\\t│   └── registry.ts                     # DELETED (DD-12)\\\\n81\\\\t└── cli/                                # DELETED entirely (DD-12)\\\\n82\\\\t```\\\\n83\\\\t\\\\n84\\\\t## Module Responsibility Matrix\\\\n85\\\\t\\\\n86\\\\t| Module | Status | Responsibility | Dependencies | ACs |\\\\n87\\\\t|--------|--------|----------------|--------------|-----|\\\\n88\\\\t| `inference/types.ts` | NEW | The boundary vocabulary: `ModelCall` contract, failure kinds, config shapes | none (pure types) | AC-1.2 |\\\\n89\\\\t| `inference/classify.ts` | NEW | `classifyFailure(kind)` table; `safeCall` wrapper (timeout, thrown-exception containment) | `types` | AC-3.1, AC-3.3, AC-2.4 (empty-output check lives in adapter but classifies here) |\\\\n90\\\\t| `inference/adapter.ts` | NEW | The seven operations: render prompt → bounded input → `safeCall` → shape → `ProviderResult` with provenance | `types`, `classify`, `prompts`, `shared/derivation` | AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.4, AC-2.5, AC-1.4 |\\\\n91\\\\t| `inference/prompts/*` | NEW | Named templates: `render(input) → messages`; registry | `shared/derivation` (input types) | AC-2.2, AC-2.3 |\\\\n92\\\\t| `sdk.ts` | MODIFIED | XOR validation, assignment validation (all seven kinds, known prompts), adapter construction; SDK-only export surface | `inference/*`, existing | AC-1.1, AC-1.3, AC-6.1 |\\\\n93\\\\t| `shared/derivation.ts` | MODIFIED | Provenance fields on result/metadata | — | AC-2.5 |\\\\n94\\\\t| `domains/*/handlers` | MODIFIED | Copy `provenance` into form metadata on success | — | AC-2.5 |\\\\n95\\\\t| `shared/view.ts` | MODIFIED | Two-field budgets, 64k/32k defaults, `max > target` validation | — | AC-5.4 |\\\\n96\\\\t| `intake-stream/pipeline.ts` | MODIFIED | Register advance only when batch committed a `turn_end` | — | AC-5.1 |\\\\n97\\\\t| `thread-view/boundary.ts` | MODIFIED | Turn-grouped zone read; peek-ahead whole-group decision; newest-group protection | — | AC-5.1–5.3, AC-5.5 |\\\\n98\\\\t| `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts` | NEW (test) | The real `ModelCall` host: fetch to OpenRouter, contract-conformant failures, key resolution | — | AC-4.1 |\\\\n99\\\\t| deletions (`cli/`, `registry.ts`, process suites) | DELETED | — | — | AC-6.1–6.3 |\\\\n100\\\\t\\\\n101\\\\tQueue machinery, drain, states, repair report: explicitly **untouched** (DD-3). Flow 3's behavior lands entirely in `classify.ts` + the adapter; the queue consumes `retryable` as it has since Epic 02.\\\\n102\\\\t\\\\n103\\\\t## Flow-by-Flow Design\\\\n104\\\\t\\\\n105\\\\t### Flow 1: Inference Wiring and Model Assignment (AC-1.1–1.4)\\\\n106\\\\t\\\\n107\\\\tConstruction is where every mistake should die. `createSdk` gains the XOR check first (before any other validation, so the error names the rule, not a downstream symptom), then — on the `inference` path — assignment validation: iterate the seven `FormKind`s, require each present with non-empty `provider`/`model` strings and a `prompt` name found in `PROMPT_REGISTRY`; reject unknown kind keys. The resolved config then feeds `createInferenceProvider`, whose return value drops into the existing `resolved.provider` slot — everything downstream of construction is Epic 02 code.\\\\n108\\\\t\\\\n109\\\\t```mermaid\\\\n110\\\\tsequenceDiagram\\\\n111\\\\t    participant Host\\\\n112\\\\t    participant SDK as createSdk\\\\n113\\\\t    participant ADP as inference/adapter\\\\n114\\\\t    participant Q as drain/handler (Epic 02, unchanged)\\\\n115\\\\t\\\\n116\\\\t    Note over Host,SDK: AC-1.1, AC-1.3: XOR + assignment validation\\\\n117\\\\t    Host->>SDK: createSdk({ inference: { call, assignments } })\\\\n118\\\\t    SDK->>SDK: validate XOR, all 7 kinds, known prompts (throws naming violation)\\\\n119\\\\t    SDK->>ADP: createInferenceProvider(resolvedInference, config)\\\\n120\\\\t    ADP-->>SDK: DerivationProvider\\\\n121\\\\t    Note over Q,ADP: AC-1.4: per-call routing at drain time\\\\n122\\\\t    Q->>ADP: provider.smoothPrompt({ text })\\\\n123\\\\t    ADP->>Host: call({ provider, model, messages }) — the kind's assignment\\\\n124\\\\t    Host-->>ADP: { ok: true, text }\\\\n125\\\\t    ADP-->>Q: { ok: true, text, provenance }\\\\n126\\\\t```\\\\n127\\\\t\\\\n128\\\\tThe routing fact worth stating precisely: the adapter closes over the assignment map at construction; each operation looks up its own kind's entry at call time. There is no per-thread or per-item routing state — two drained items of different kinds calling different lanes is just two closure lookups.\\\\n129\\\\t\\\\n130\\\\t### Flow 2: The Adapter and the Seven Prompts (AC-2.1–2.5)\\\\n131\\\\t\\\\n132\\\\tEach adapter operation is the same five-step pipeline: (1) bound the input where DD-7 applies, (2) `render` the kind's prompt template into a messages array, (3) `safeCall` the host function with the kind's assignment, (4) reject empty/whitespace text as `empty_output`, (5) return `{ ok: true, text, provenance }`. The operations differ only in which template renders and which input fields embed — the pipeline is one private function parameterized by kind.\\\\n133\\\\t\\\\n134\\\\tA prompt template's contract, worked example (`tool-result-v1`):\\\\n135\\\\t\\\\n136\\\\t```ts\\\\n137\\\\t// prompts/tool-result-v1.ts\\\\n138\\\\texport const toolResultV1: PromptTemplate<{ toolName: string; content: string }> = {\\\\n139\\\\t  name: \\\\\\\"tool-result-v1\\\\\\\",\\\\n140\\\\t  render: (i) => [\\\\n141\\\\t    { role: \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\"You summarize tool output for an engineering record. Preserve concrete facts: paths, identifiers, counts, error text. State the outcome plainly. No commentary, no speculation, 150 words maximum.\\\\\\\" },\\\\n142\\\\t    { role: \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\", content: `Tool: ${i.toolName}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOutput:\\\\\\\\n${i.content}` },\\\\n143\\\\t  ],\\\\n144\\\\t};\\\\n145\\\\t```\\\\n146\\\\t\\\\n147\\\\tThe rendered array is the entire call — single-turn, no tools, no streaming (Assumption 2). The smoothing template ports the POC's settled prompt text verbatim; the other six are written to its standard (concrete instruction, fact-preservation rule, length bound) and carry a `// PRE-DIAL-IN` marker comment. The chunk-brief template's input is already receipt-stripped by the Epic 02 handler before the provider is called — the adapter inherits that structural guarantee and the test plan re-asserts it through the adapter (TC-2.2).\\\\n148\\\\t\\\\n149\\\\tWhat the adapter must never do: parse the response for outcomes, receipts, or any mechanical fact. Those stamps happen in handlers from the record, exactly as under the deterministic provider (AC-2.2). The adapter's only additions to the result are the text and the config-known provenance strings.\\\\n150\\\\t\\\\n151\\\\t### Flow 3: Failure Classification (AC-3.1–3.3)\\\\n152\\\\t\\\\n153\\\\t`classify.ts` is two small exports. The table:\\\\n154\\\\t\\\\n155\\\\t```ts\\\\n156\\\\texport const FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION: Record<ModelCallFailureKind, { retryable: boolean }> = {\\\\n157\\\\t  rate_limit: { retryable: true },\\\\n158\\\\t  timeout: { retryable: true },\\\\n159\\\\t  network: { retryable: true },\\\\n160\\\\t  empty_output: { retryable: true },\\\\n161\\\\t  other: { retryable: true },\\\\n162\\\\t  auth: { retryable: false },\\\\n163\\\\t  invalid_request: { retryable: false },\\\\n164\\\\t};\\\\n165\\\\t```\\\\n166\\\\t\\\\n167\\\\tAnd `safeCall(call, input, timeoutMs)`: wraps the host function in try/catch (thrown → `{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\", message }`) and a timeout race (→ `{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\", message }`). The adapter maps the structured failure to Epic 02's `ProviderResult`: `{ ok: false, retryable: FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION[kind].retryable, reason: kind }`. From there the existing machinery does everything — backoff on retryable, immediate `failed` on terminal, exhaustion copying attempts/last-error into form metadata. The misconfigured-lane story (config names an unauthed provider) needs no code: the host function returns `auth`, classification makes it terminal, the health report shows `failed` forms with reason `auth` per assigned kind.\\\\n168\\\\t\\\\n169\\\\t### Flow 4: Real-Inference Verification (AC-4.1–4.2)\\\\n170\\\\t\\\\n171\\\\tThe suite (`test/inference-real.test.ts`) keys on `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`. Present: build the OpenRouter `ModelCall` fixture, construct an SDK with all seven kinds assigned to the configured cheap model (`LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL`, defaulted in the fixture), and run the legs. Absent: emit one structured not-ran record (a test that asserts-and-reports the skip reason loudly — visible in output as `NOT-RAN: real-inference (LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY unset)`, never a green checkmark indistinguishable from a pass). The accounting rule is implemented as a suite-level guard, not per-test `skip`s, so the run/not-run state is one fact reported once.\\\\n172\\\\t\\\\n173\\\\tThe capstone reuses Epic 04's lifecycle sequence with the assertion set swapped: where the deterministic leg asserted byte-exact replay and marker content, this leg asserts structure — every `FormKind` reaches `ready` at least once with non-empty text containing no deterministic marker pattern (`/^(smoothed|toolcall|toolresult|rendering|projection|detailed|brief)\\\\\\\\(/`), provenance naming the real model, and the checkpoint-coherence ladder (mutation clears → pending; drain → ready with *different* content; second compact's view reflects post-edit content).\\\\n174\\\\t\\\\n175\\\\t### Flow 5: Turn-End Boundary Advance (AC-5.1–5.5)\\\\n176\\\\t\\\\n177\\\\tTwo modules change. In `pipeline.ts`, the walk already knows whether the batch contained a `turn_end` (it processes the event kinds); the advance registration at line ~150 gains that condition. Everything about the registration — first-before-poke order, throw isolation, both host modes, flush-after-COMMIT — is untouched.\\\\n178\\\\t\\\\n179\\\\tIn `boundary.ts`, the zone read gains `turn_id` and the decision regroups:\\\\n180\\\\t\\\\n181\\\\t```\\\\n182\\\\treadZoneToolResults: + turn_id per row (same WHERE, same order)\\\\n183\\\\tgroupZone: consecutive rows by turn_id → groups, each { turnId, sourceEventOrders, tokenSum }\\\\n184\\\\t           (turn_id NULL ⇒ singleton group, DD-10)\\\\n185\\\\tadvanceDecision(groups, { maxTokens, targetTokens }):\\\\n186\\\\t  total ≤ max → null\\\\n187\\\\t  walk groups oldest-first, excluding the newest group always:\\\\n188\\\\t    evict group only if (remaining − group.tokenSum) ≥ target   ← peek-ahead, AC-5.3\\\\n189\\\\t    newPosition = group's highest sourceEventOrder\\\\n190\\\\t  return newPosition (lands in [target, target + one group))\\\\n191\\\\t```\\\\n192\\\\t\\\\n193\\\\tWorked example (golden G2 in the test plan): groups of 30k/25k/20k/15k tokens (oldest→newest), max 64k, target 32k. Total 90k > max. Evict 30k → 60k remaining, ≥ 32k ✓. Evict 25k → 35k remaining, ≥ 32k ✓. Peek 20k: 35k − 20k = 15k < 32k → stop. Boundary lands after the 25k group; zone = 35k, in [32k, 32k+20k). The 15k newest group was never a candidate.\\\\n194\\\\t\\\\n195\\\\tThe shared-query invariant survives: `visibilityZoneTokens` (status's sum) and the decision's walk read the same WHERE-clause population, so status and advance can never disagree about the zone — the property the existing code makes structural stays structural.\\\\n196\\\\t\\\\n197\\\\t`VisibilityBudgets` loses `floorTokens` everywhere it appears: the type, `resolveViewConfig`'s defaults and validation (which gains `maxTokens > targetTokens`), and the status read's reporting shape is unchanged (`zoneTokens`/`maxTokens` already). Existing Epic 03 tests asserting floor behavior are superseded — the test plan's amendment ledger names each one (the spec-sanctioned test-change path, as Epic 02's F-3 established).\\\\n198\\\\t\\\\n199\\\\t### Flow 6: CLI Retirement (AC-6.1–6.3)\\\\n200\\\\t\\\\n201\\\\tDeletion plus proof. Delete `src/cli/`, the `src/cli.ts` bin entrypoint (a separate file outside the directory — it imports `./cli/index.js` and breaks the build if left), the `bin` manifest entry, and the `dev:cli` script; delete `src/providers/registry.ts` and its two `sdk.ts` re-exports; delete the twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` files; rewrite `verify`/`verify-all` (the `SKIP: cli-process` echo and `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate go; `verify-all` becomes red-verify + full vitest including the self-accounting inference suite). The proof is three-legged: a public-API surface snapshot test (import the package entry, assert the export-name set against a checked-in expected list — catches both leftover CLI exports and accidental removals), a source-level grep test for `LHC_PROVIDER`/registry references, and the unchanged-SDK-coverage check (the full remaining suite green proves no behavior test rode out with the process suites — process suites asserted *parity*, never unique behavior, which is why deletion is safe).\\\\n202\\\\t\\\\n203\\\\tThe Epic 04 deviation note is written into this pack's deviation table at story completion: spawned `inspect health` parity was backfilled before Epic 05, and this story deletes the spawned-process parity surface instead of carrying it forward.\\\\n204\\\\t\\\\n205\\\\t## Interface Definitions\\\\n206\\\\t\\\\n207\\\\t```ts\\\\n208\\\\t// ── inference/types.ts ──────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n209\\\\t\\\\n210\\\\t/** The one function a host supplies. Single-turn completion; provider/model\\\\n211\\\\t *  are opaque routing keys the host's implementation interprets. (AC-1.2) */\\\\n212\\\\texport type ModelCall = (input: ModelCallInput) => Promise<ModelCallResult>;\\\\n213\\\\t\\\\n214\\\\texport interface ModelCallInput {\\\\n215\\\\t  provider: string;\\\\n216\\\\t  model: string;\\\\n217\\\\t  messages: { role: \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\"; content: string }[];\\\\n218\\\\t}\\\\n219\\\\t\\\\n220\\\\texport type ModelCallResult =\\\\n221\\\\t  | { ok: true; text: string }\\\\n222\\\\t  | { ok: false; kind: ModelCallFailureKind; message: string };\\\\n223\\\\t\\\\n224\\\\t/** `empty_output` is adapter-generated (AC-2.4); hosts never return it.\\\\n225\\\\t *  Thrown exceptions classify as `other` (AC-3.3). */\\\\n226\\\\texport type ModelCallFailureKind =\\\\n227\\\\t  | \\\\\\\"rate_limit\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"network\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\"\\\\n228\\\\t  | \\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"invalid_request\\\\\\\";\\\\n229\\\\t\\\\n230\\\\texport interface ModelAssignment {\\\\n231\\\\t  provider: string;\\\\n232\\\\t  model: string;\\\\n233\\\\t  prompt: string; // must name a PROMPT_REGISTRY entry (AC-1.3)\\\\n234\\\\t}\\\\n235\\\\t\\\\n236\\\\t/** SdkConfig.inference — the alternative to SdkConfig.provider (DD-5). */\\\\n237\\\\texport interface InferenceConfig {\\\\n238\\\\t  call: ModelCall;\\\\n239\\\\t  assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>; // all seven, validated complete\\\\n240\\\\t  timeoutMs?: number;      // default 60_000 (DD-6)\\\\n241\\\\t  maxInputChars?: number;  // default 200_000 (DD-7)\\\\n242\\\\t}\\\\n243\\\\t\\\\n244\\\\t// ── inference/prompts/index.ts ──────────────────────────────────\\\\n245\\\\t\\\\n246\\\\texport interface PromptTemplate<I = unknown> {\\\\n247\\\\t  name: string;\\\\n248\\\\t  render(input: I): ModelCallInput[\\\\\\\"messages\\\\\\\"];\\\\n249\\\\t}\\\\n250\\\\texport const PROMPT_REGISTRY: Record<string, PromptTemplate<never>>;\\\\n251\\\\t\\\\n252\\\\t// ── inference/classify.ts ───────────────────────────────────────\\\\n253\\\\t\\\\n254\\\\texport const FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION: Record<ModelCallFailureKind, { retryable: boolean }>;\\\\n255\\\\t/** try/catch + timeout race around the host function (AC-3.3, DD-6). */\\\\n256\\\\texport function safeCall(\\\\n257\\\\t  call: ModelCall, input: ModelCallInput, timeoutMs: number,\\\\n258\\\\t): Promise<ModelCallResult>;\\\\n259\\\\t\\\\n260\\\\t// ── inference/adapter.ts ────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n261\\\\t\\\\n262\\\\t/** The real provider. Same interface the deterministic provider implements;\\\\n263\\\\t *  createSdk slots it into resolved.provider (DD-2). */\\\\n264\\\\texport function createInferenceProvider(config: ResolvedInferenceConfig): DerivationProvider;\\\\n265\\\\t\\\\n266\\\\t// ── shared/derivation.ts (additions only) ───────────────────────\\\\n267\\\\t\\\\n268\\\\texport interface ProviderProvenance { provider: string; model: string; prompt: string }\\\\n269\\\\t// ProviderResult ok-branch: { ok: true; text: string; provenance?: ProviderProvenance }\\\\n270\\\\t// DerivedFormMetadata: + provenance?: ProviderProvenance   (DD-4; AC-2.5)\\\\n271\\\\t\\\\n272\\\\t// ── shared/view.ts (change) ─────────────────────────────────────\\\\n273\\\\t\\\\n274\\\\texport interface VisibilityBudgets { maxTokens: number; targetTokens: number }\\\\n275\\\\t// defaults 64_000 / 32_000; resolveViewConfig validates maxTokens > targetTokens (AC-5.4)\\\\n276\\\\t\\\\n277\\\\t// ── sdk.ts (change) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n278\\\\t\\\\n279\\\\texport interface SdkConfig {\\\\n280\\\\t  provider?: DerivationProvider;   // XOR with inference (AC-1.1)\\\\n281\\\\t  inference?: InferenceConfig;\\\\n282\\\\t  // …existing fields unchanged\\\\n283\\\\t}\\\\n284\\\\t// removed exports: resolveNamedProvider, registeredProviderNames (AC-6.1)\\\\n285\\\\t```\\\\n286\\\\t\\\\n287\\\\tSkeleton stubs follow the house pattern: `inference/` functions are machine-readable-contract code, so stubs return structured failures (`{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\", message: \\\\\\\"not implemented\\\\\\\" }`) rather than throwing; `createInferenceProvider` may throw `NotImplementedError` at construction since construction errors throw by contract.\\\\n288\\\\t\\\\n289\\\\t## Error Contract\\\\n290\\\\t\\\\n291\\\\tConstruction errors are `TypeError`s naming the violated rule (existing contract): `\\\\\\\"createSdk config: exactly one of provider or inference\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"inference.assignments missing kind chunk_summary_brief\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"inference.assignments.smoothed_prompt.prompt names unknown template 'smoothing-v2'\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"view.visibility.maxTokens must be > targetTokens\\\\\\\"`. Operating failures never throw: they are `ProviderResult` failures with `reason` = the failure kind, flowing into form state via the unchanged Epic 02 paths (`failed` with reason `provider_failure` at exhaustion, terminal kinds immediately).\\\\n292\\\\t\\\\n293\\\\t## Runtime Prerequisites\\\\n294\\\\t\\\\n295\\\\t| Prerequisite | Where | How to Verify |\\\\n296\\\\t|---|---|---|\\\\n297\\\\t| Node ≥ 22 (`node:sqlite`, native `fetch`) | Local + CI | `node --version` (existing) |\\\\n298\\\\t| `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY` | Opt-in suite only | Suite self-reports ran/not-ran; never required by CI default |\\\\n299\\\\t| `LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL` | Opt-in suite, optional | Fixture default (cheap model) used when unset |\\\\n300\\\\t\\\\n301\\\\tNo new dependencies. The OpenRouter fixture uses plain `fetch` in test code (epic NFR).\\\\n302\\\\t\\\\n303\\\\t## Testing Strategy\\\\n304\\\\t\\\\n305\\\\tThe mock boundary moves with this epic, and naming it precisely is the design's most important testing statement: **the external boundary is now the `ModelCall` function.** Flows 1–3 test against scripted fake functions (canned successes, scripted failure sequences, recording wrappers) — these are boundary mocks, not internal mocks, because the function *is* where LHC's code ends. The `DerivationProvider` interface is no longer the mock seam for this epic's tests; it's internal wiring under test (the adapter is the unit). Epic 02–04 suites keep their provider-level doubles untouched — their subject was never the adapter.\\\\n306\\\\t\\\\n307\\\\tEverything else follows house rules: real temp SQLite for anything touching thread state (boundary tests, capstone), no internal module mocks, deterministic provider remains the CI default for all existing suites. The opt-in suite is the only network-touching code in the repo and is labeled in `verify-all` accounting as `ran` or `not-ran (reason)` — the four-tier verify scripts keep their names; composition changes are in Flow 6.\\\\n308\\\\t\\\\n309\\\\tTest segmentation after the process suites delete: one vitest tier (default, no network) plus the env-gated inference suite in the same runner. No separate integration runner — the capstone is a vitest test that happens to make real calls when keyed.\\\\n310\\\\t\\\\n311\\\\t## Work Breakdown\\\\n312\\\\t\\\\n313\\\\tChunk order mirrors the epic's story order; the test plan carries per-chunk Red/Green tables.\\\\n314\\\\t\\\\n315\\\\t| Chunk | Scope | ACs | Key risk |\\\\n316\\\\t|-------|-------|-----|----------|\\\\n317\\\\t| 0: Foundation | `inference/types.ts`, provenance type additions, fake-call fixtures (`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`: recording/scripted builders), prompt-template type + registry skeleton | — | Fixture validity: scripted fakes must satisfy the AC-1.2 contract shape |\\\\n318\\\\t| 1: CLI retirement | Flow 6 deletions + proofs + verify-script rewrite | 6.1–6.3 | Deleting a behavior test disguised as a process test (mitigation: surface snapshot + full-suite green before/after comparison) |\\\\n319\\\\t| 2: Seam + assignments | XOR, validation, adapter construction wiring | 1.1–1.4 | Validation drift from `FormKind` (mitigation: iterate the exported union, never a literal list) |\\\\n320\\\\t| 3: Adapter + prompts | Seven operations, templates, bounding, empty-output, provenance | 2.1–2.5 | Provenance authored from output instead of config (it's three config strings — keep it that way) |\\\\n321\\\\t| 4: Classification | Table, `safeCall`, handler provenance copies | 3.1–3.3 | Re-implementing retry logic the queue already owns (the adapter returns `retryable`; nothing more) |\\\\n322\\\\t| 5: Real suite + capstone | OpenRouter fixture, accounting guard, seven round-trips, capstone leg | 4.1–4.2 | Silent skip (the accounting guard is the deliverable; review it first) |\\\\n323\\\\t| 6: Boundary advance | Gate, grouping, peek-ahead, config change, Epic 03 test amendments | 5.1–5.5 | Floor-test amnesia — superseded tests must be amended per the ledger, not deleted wholesale |\\\\n324\\\\t\\\\n325\\\\tDependencies: 0 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5; 1 and 6 are independent of the pipeline chunks (1 first by story order; 6 anytime).\\\\n326\\\\t\\\\n327\\\\t## Deferred Items\\\\n328\\\\t\\\\n329\\\\t| Item | Related | Reason | Where |\\\\n330\\\\t|------|---------|--------|-------|\\\\n331\\\\t| Tuned prompts + model choices | AC-2.3 | Dial-in working period; needs harness corpora | Backfills this pack pre-acceptance |\\\\n332\\\\t| Config generalization (token-band routing, fallback chains) | Flow 1 | Vocabulary derived from seven dialed-in answers | Post-dial-in design |\\\\n333\\\\t| Mid-turn safety ceiling | Flow 5 | POC removed it; no usage evidence of need | Revisit on evidence |\\\\n334\\\\t| Full integrated harness (capture ext, corpora, converter, journeys) | Flow 4 | Paired working period post-epic | `post-epic-4-work.md` |\\\\n335\\\\t| PI extension transport + config values | Flow 1 | Extension PRD | `pi-ext-prd-notes.md` |\\\\n336\\\\t\\\"}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"9b33975e-11b1-498f-8628-daa665e421dc\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-06-12T23:20:42.061Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"file\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"# Epic 05: Derivation Inference — Tech Design\\\\n\\\\n**Epic:** `epic.md` (22 ACs / 15 TCs across 6 flows)\\\\n**Test Plan:** `test-plan.md` (full TC→test mapping, Red/Green detail)\\\\n**Tech Arch:** `../01-tech-arch.md`\\\\n**Consumed contracts:** Epic 02 provider seam and queue machinery (as built, deviations closed), Epic 03 visibility boundary (Flow 5 patches its contract), Epic 04 lifecycle exercise (as built; deviation state — e.g. overview boundary from `pull.meta` — is the consumed truth)\\\\n\\\\n## Context\\\\n\\\\nFour epics built a derivation pipeline that has never made a model call. The seam was designed for this moment: `DerivationProvider` is an interface with seven semantic operations, the deterministic provider implements it with marker strings, and every handler, queue row, retry path, and state transition in `domains/` is provider-agnostic. This epic builds the second implementation of that interface — the real one — plus the injection path that lets a host supply model access without LHC ever touching a credential.\\\\n\\\\nThe design splits inference into two layers with different owners. The **semantic layer** is LHC's and lives in a new `src/inference/` area: seven prompt templates, output shaping, per-kind model assignment, failure classification, provenance stamping. The **transport layer** is the host's and crosses the boundary as exactly one function: `(provider, model, messages) → text or structured failure`. The host's function is the only code that knows what `\\\\\\\"anthropic\\\\\\\"` means; LHC treats provider and model strings as opaque routing keys carried from config to call. This is the seam decision recorded in `post-epic-4-work.md`, and it is why the PI extension's eventual contribution is ~15 lines while the seven prompts stay tunable inside LHC without touching any host.\\\\n\\\\nTwo riders land in the same epic because they consolidate decisions the inference work exposes. The CLI retirement deletes the one consumer of the named-provider registry and env-resolution path, which means provider arrival collapses to a single mechanism — injection at `createSdk` — exactly as the new `inference` config option arrives. Doing these together means the construction-validation story is written once against the final shape rather than patched twice. The boundary-advance patch is independent of inference entirely; it rides because it is small, fully validated in the POC (commits `1cf2dc45`, `6a9aa7a4`, `f12a850d` are the reference implementation), and it shares this pack's spec cycle rather than deserving its own.\\\\n\\\\nOne thing this design deliberately does not do: chase output quality. The prompts ship structurally sound and explicitly pre-dial-in; the dial-in working period that follows the integrated-harness pairing produces the tuned prompts and model choices, and its findings backfill this pack before acceptance. Every TC in this epic asserts structure, routing, classification, and provenance — never whether a summary is good.\\\\n\\\\n## Design Decisions\\\\n\\\\n- **DD-1: One transport function, not seven.** The host injects a single `ModelCall`; per-kind knowledge (which provider/model/prompt) lives in LHC config. Injecting seven functions would move LHC's derivation catalog into every host's wiring and make an eighth kind a breaking host change. The function takes the provider string per call, so multi-lane hosts route inside one function.\\\\n- **DD-2: The adapter is a `DerivationProvider`.** No seam movement. `createInferenceProvider(inference, config)` returns the same interface the deterministic provider implements; `createSdk` and everything below it see a provider, full stop. Handlers, queue, drain, states: zero changes for Flows 1–4 (except the one-line provenance copy, DD-4).\\\\n- **DD-3: Classification maps onto the existing `ProviderResult`.** Epic 02's failure shape is already `{ ok: false, retryable, reason }`. The classification table is a pure function `ModelCallFailureKind → { retryable, reason }` where `reason` is the kind string itself — stable, machine-readable, and the queue machinery consumes `retryable` exactly as it always has. No queue changes, no new states.\\\\n- **DD-4: Provenance rides the `ProviderResult` and is copied, never authored.** The ok branch gains `provenance?: { provider, model, prompt }`. The adapter stamps it (it alone knows the assignment); handlers copy it into `DerivedFormMetadata.provenance` alongside the existing mechanical stamps. The deterministic provider simply never sets it. This keeps the \\\\\\\"metadata is mechanically stamped\\\\\\\" invariant: provenance comes from config-known strings, not model output.\\\\n- **DD-5: Construction is `provider` XOR `inference`.** `createSdk` accepts exactly one. The `inference` path validates the assignment map (all seven `FormKind`s present, every prompt name known) and builds the adapter; the `provider` path is unchanged and keeps the deterministic provider as the test default. Both/neither is a `TypeError` at construction, matching the existing config-mistakes-throw contract.\\\\n- **DD-6: The adapter owns a per-call timeout.** Epic tech-design question 2, resolved: a hung host function must not stall a drain, and the host shouldn't have to remember a duty LHC can enforce. `Promise.race` against `inference.timeoutMs` (default 60 000); a timeout classifies as `timeout` (retryable). A host that resolves later resolves into nothing.\\\\n- **DD-7: Input bounding is an adapter seam with a generous default.** Question 3, resolved: `summarizeToolResult` content over `inference.maxInputChars` (default 200 000) is truncated head-and-tail with a marker before prompt rendering. The bound exists so a pathological tool result cannot blow a small-context model; the value is a dial-in tunable, the seam is design.\\\\n- **DD-8: Prompts are modules in a name-keyed registry.** Question 1, resolved: `src/inference/prompts/` holds one module per template exporting `{ name, render(input) → messages }`; `prompts/index.ts` assembles the registry. Config selects by name; dial-in swaps by adding a module and editing config — no handler, adapter, or host changes. Versioning is in the name (`smoothing-v1`).\\\\n- **DD-9: The turn-end trigger is a gate in the pipeline, not a new seam.** Question 4, resolved: `pipeline.ts` already registers the advance `onCommit`, ordered before the queue poke, throw-isolated, both host modes. The change is registering it only when the batch's walk saw a `turn_end` event. Ordering against the queue poke is untouched — same registration site, same pinned order. The advance still runs at flush strictly after intake's COMMIT, in its own short transaction.\\\\n- **DD-10: Whole-turn eviction via `turn_id` grouping; turnless results degrade to singleton groups.** The zone walk groups tool results by `turn_id` (the message column), oldest group first by the group's lowest `source_event_order`. A `turn_id IS NULL` tool result (turnless intake is legal) is its own group — whole-turn semantics degenerate to whole-message for messages that belong to no turn, which preserves the epic's \\\\\\\"no turn is ever partially flipped\\\\\\\" without inventing membership.\\\\n- **DD-11: Floor retirement is a config-surface change, not a migration.** `VisibilityBudgets` becomes `{ maxTokens, targetTokens }` (64 000 / 32 000 defaults); `floorTokens` is deleted from the type, resolution, and validation. No schema change — `view_boundary` is untouched; the decision function changes shape, the storage doesn't. The protected floor's *job* survives structurally: the newest closed turn group is never evicted, and the open turn is invisible to a check that only runs at turn close.\\\\n- **DD-12: CLI deletion removes the registry, keeps the deterministic provider.** `src/providers/registry.ts` (named resolution, `LHC_PROVIDER`) dies with its only consumer. `src/providers/deterministic.ts` stays — it is the CI-default test provider and the byte-stable fixture substrate. The twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` suites delete; `verify-all` drops the `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate and gains the opt-in inference suite (which self-reports ran/not-ran).\\\\n- **DD-13: The real-inference suite is a second host of the same contract.** The OpenRouter-backed `ModelCall` lives in `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts`, implements the AC-1.2 contract over plain `fetch`, and runs Flow 1's construction/routing assertions parameterized (question 5, resolved: shared assertion helpers parameterized over the function, not duplicated tests). Key arrives via `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`; absence produces an explicit not-ran record, never a silent pass.\\\\n\\\\n## Top-Tier Surfaces\\\\n\\\\n| Surface | Source | This Epic's Role |\\\\n|---------|--------|------------------|\\\\n| SDK assembly (`src/sdk.ts`) | Tech arch | Gains the `inference` construction path (DD-5); loses CLI-only exports |\\\\n| `shared/` | Tech arch | `derivation.ts` gains provenance on `ProviderResult`/metadata; `view.ts` loses `floorTokens` |\\\\n| `inference/` (new, peer of `providers/`) | This epic | The semantic layer: adapter, classification, prompts. Not a domain — it implements the provider seam, owns no thread state, queues no work |\\\\n| `domains/thread-view` | Tech arch | `boundary.ts` decision reshape (DD-10); seam/registration untouched except the gate (DD-9) |\\\\n| `domains/intake-stream` | Tech arch | One-line gate on the advance registration (DD-9) |\\\\n| `domains/messages`, `domains/turns` | Tech arch | One-line provenance copy in each handler's form write (DD-4) |\\\\n| `cli/`, `providers/registry` | Tech arch (retiring) | Deleted (DD-12) |\\\\n\\\\n`inference/` must not import `domains/`; it depends only on `shared/` types. The boundaries check gains that rule.\\\\n\\\\n## Module Architecture\\\\n\\\\n```\\\\nsrc/\\\\n├── inference/                          # NEW: the semantic layer (DD-1..DD-8)\\\\n│   ├── types.ts                        # NEW: ModelCall, ModelCallResult, ModelCallFailureKind,\\\\n│   │                                   #      InferenceConfig, ModelAssignment, ResolvedInferenceConfig\\\\n│   ├── classify.ts                     # NEW: classification table + exception containment (DD-3)\\\\n│   ├── adapter.ts                      # NEW: createInferenceProvider — DerivationProvider impl (DD-2)\\\\n│   └── prompts/\\\\n│       ├── index.ts                    # NEW: PROMPT_REGISTRY (name → PromptTemplate) (DD-8)\\\\n│       ├── smoothing-v1.ts             # NEW: ported from POC, settled\\\\n│       ├── tool-call-v1.ts             # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n│       ├── tool-result-v1.ts           # NEW: pre-dial-in (input bounding lives here-adjacent, DD-7)\\\\n│       ├── turn-compose-v1.ts          # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n│       ├── lower-band-v1.ts            # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n│       ├── chunk-detailed-v1.ts        # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n│       └── chunk-brief-v1.ts           # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\n├── sdk.ts                              # MODIFIED: provider XOR inference; assignment validation;\\\\n│                                       #           export cleanup (registry exports removed)\\\\n├── shared/\\\\n│   ├── derivation.ts                   # MODIFIED: ProviderResult ok-branch + DerivedFormMetadata\\\\n│   │                                   #           gain provenance? (DD-4)\\\\n│   └── view.ts                         # MODIFIED: VisibilityBudgets → { maxTokens, targetTokens },\\\\n│                                       #           defaults 64000/32000 (DD-11)\\\\n├── domains/\\\\n│   ├── intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts   # MODIFIED: advance registration gated on turn_end (DD-9)\\\\n│   ├── thread-view/internal/boundary.ts     # MODIFIED: turn-grouped zone, peek-ahead decision (DD-10)\\\\n│   ├── messages/internal/…handlers          # MODIFIED: copy result.provenance → metadata (DD-4)\\\\n│   └── turns/internal/…handlers             # MODIFIED: same one-line copy (DD-4)\\\\n├── providers/\\\\n│   ├── deterministic.ts                # EXISTS: unchanged — CI-default test provider\\\\n│   └── registry.ts                     # DELETED (DD-12)\\\\n└── cli/                                # DELETED entirely (DD-12)\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n## Module Responsibility Matrix\\\\n\\\\n| Module | Status | Responsibility | Dependencies | ACs |\\\\n|--------|--------|----------------|--------------|-----|\\\\n| `inference/types.ts` | NEW | The boundary vocabulary: `ModelCall` contract, failure kinds, config shapes | none (pure types) | AC-1.2 |\\\\n| `inference/classify.ts` | NEW | `classifyFailure(kind)` table; `safeCall` wrapper (timeout, thrown-exception containment) | `types` | AC-3.1, AC-3.3, AC-2.4 (empty-output check lives in adapter but classifies here) |\\\\n| `inference/adapter.ts` | NEW | The seven operations: render prompt → bounded input → `safeCall` → shape → `ProviderResult` with provenance | `types`, `classify`, `prompts`, `shared/derivation` | AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.4, AC-2.5, AC-1.4 |\\\\n| `inference/prompts/*` | NEW | Named templates: `render(input) → messages`; registry | `shared/derivation` (input types) | AC-2.2, AC-2.3 |\\\\n| `sdk.ts` | MODIFIED | XOR validation, assignment validation (all seven kinds, known prompts), adapter construction; SDK-only export surface | `inference/*`, existing | AC-1.1, AC-1.3, AC-6.1 |\\\\n| `shared/derivation.ts` | MODIFIED | Provenance fields on result/metadata | — | AC-2.5 |\\\\n| `domains/*/handlers` | MODIFIED | Copy `provenance` into form metadata on success | — | AC-2.5 |\\\\n| `shared/view.ts` | MODIFIED | Two-field budgets, 64k/32k defaults, `max > target` validation | — | AC-5.4 |\\\\n| `intake-stream/pipeline.ts` | MODIFIED | Register advance only when batch committed a `turn_end` | — | AC-5.1 |\\\\n| `thread-view/boundary.ts` | MODIFIED | Turn-grouped zone read; peek-ahead whole-group decision; newest-group protection | — | AC-5.1–5.3, AC-5.5 |\\\\n| `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts` | NEW (test) | The real `ModelCall` host: fetch to OpenRouter, contract-conformant failures, key resolution | — | AC-4.1 |\\\\n| deletions (`cli/`, `registry.ts`, process suites) | DELETED | — | — | AC-6.1–6.3 |\\\\n\\\\nQueue machinery, drain, states, repair report: explicitly **untouched** (DD-3). Flow 3's behavior lands entirely in `classify.ts` + the adapter; the queue consumes `retryable` as it has since Epic 02.\\\\n\\\\n## Flow-by-Flow Design\\\\n\\\\n### Flow 1: Inference Wiring and Model Assignment (AC-1.1–1.4)\\\\n\\\\nConstruction is where every mistake should die. `createSdk` gains the XOR check first (before any other validation, so the error names the rule, not a downstream symptom), then — on the `inference` path — assignment validation: iterate the seven `FormKind`s, require each present with non-empty `provider`/`model` strings and a `prompt` name found in `PROMPT_REGISTRY`; reject unknown kind keys. The resolved config then feeds `createInferenceProvider`, whose return value drops into the existing `resolved.provider` slot — everything downstream of construction is Epic 02 code.\\\\n\\\\n```mermaid\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\n    participant Host\\\\n    participant SDK as createSdk\\\\n    participant ADP as inference/adapter\\\\n    participant Q as drain/handler (Epic 02, unchanged)\\\\n\\\\n    Note over Host,SDK: AC-1.1, AC-1.3: XOR + assignment validation\\\\n    Host->>SDK: createSdk({ inference: { call, assignments } })\\\\n    SDK->>SDK: validate XOR, all 7 kinds, known prompts (throws naming violation)\\\\n    SDK->>ADP: createInferenceProvider(resolvedInference, config)\\\\n    ADP-->>SDK: DerivationProvider\\\\n    Note over Q,ADP: AC-1.4: per-call routing at drain time\\\\n    Q->>ADP: provider.smoothPrompt({ text })\\\\n    ADP->>Host: call({ provider, model, messages }) — the kind's assignment\\\\n    Host-->>ADP: { ok: true, text }\\\\n    ADP-->>Q: { ok: true, text, provenance }\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nThe routing fact worth stating precisely: the adapter closes over the assignment map at construction; each operation looks up its own kind's entry at call time. There is no per-thread or per-item routing state — two drained items of different kinds calling different lanes is just two closure lookups.\\\\n\\\\n### Flow 2: The Adapter and the Seven Prompts (AC-2.1–2.5)\\\\n\\\\nEach adapter operation is the same five-step pipeline: (1) bound the input where DD-7 applies, (2) `render` the kind's prompt template into a messages array, (3) `safeCall` the host function with the kind's assignment, (4) reject empty/whitespace text as `empty_output`, (5) return `{ ok: true, text, provenance }`. The operations differ only in which template renders and which input fields embed — the pipeline is one private function parameterized by kind.\\\\n\\\\nA prompt template's contract, worked example (`tool-result-v1`):\\\\n\\\\n```ts\\\\n// prompts/tool-result-v1.ts\\\\nexport const toolResultV1: PromptTemplate<{ toolName: string; content: string }> = {\\\\n  name: \\\\\\\"tool-result-v1\\\\\\\",\\\\n  render: (i) => [\\\\n    { role: \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\"You summarize tool output for an engineering record. Preserve concrete facts: paths, identifiers, counts, error text. State the outcome plainly. No commentary, no speculation, 150 words maximum.\\\\\\\" },\\\\n    { role: \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\", content: `Tool: ${i.toolName}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOutput:\\\\\\\\n${i.content}` },\\\\n  ],\\\\n};\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nThe rendered array is the entire call — single-turn, no tools, no streaming (Assumption 2). The smoothing template ports the POC's settled prompt text verbatim; the other six are written to its standard (concrete instruction, fact-preservation rule, length bound) and carry a `// PRE-DIAL-IN` marker comment. The chunk-brief template's input is already receipt-stripped by the Epic 02 handler before the provider is called — the adapter inherits that structural guarantee and the test plan re-asserts it through the adapter (TC-2.2).\\\\n\\\\nWhat the adapter must never do: parse the response for outcomes, receipts, or any mechanical fact. Those stamps happen in handlers from the record, exactly as under the deterministic provider (AC-2.2). The adapter's only additions to the result are the text and the config-known provenance strings.\\\\n\\\\n### Flow 3: Failure Classification (AC-3.1–3.3)\\\\n\\\\n`classify.ts` is two small exports. The table:\\\\n\\\\n```ts\\\\nexport const FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION: Record<ModelCallFailureKind, { retryable: boolean }> = {\\\\n  rate_limit: { retryable: true },\\\\n  timeout: { retryable: true },\\\\n  network: { retryable: true },\\\\n  empty_output: { retryable: true },\\\\n  other: { retryable: true },\\\\n  auth: { retryable: false },\\\\n  invalid_request: { retryable: false },\\\\n};\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nAnd `safeCall(call, input, timeoutMs)`: wraps the host function in try/catch (thrown → `{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\", message }`) and a timeout race (→ `{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\", message }`). The adapter maps the structured failure to Epic 02's `ProviderResult`: `{ ok: false, retryable: FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION[kind].retryable, reason: kind }`. From there the existing machinery does everything — backoff on retryable, immediate `failed` on terminal, exhaustion copying attempts/last-error into form metadata. The misconfigured-lane story (config names an unauthed provider) needs no code: the host function returns `auth`, classification makes it terminal, the health report shows `failed` forms with reason `auth` per assigned kind.\\\\n\\\\n### Flow 4: Real-Inference Verification (AC-4.1–4.2)\\\\n\\\\nThe suite (`test/inference-real.test.ts`) keys on `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`. Present: build the OpenRouter `ModelCall` fixture, construct an SDK with all seven kinds assigned to the configured cheap model (`LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL`, defaulted in the fixture), and run the legs. Absent: emit one structured not-ran record (a test that asserts-and-reports the skip reason loudly — visible in output as `NOT-RAN: real-inference (LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY unset)`, never a green checkmark indistinguishable from a pass). The accounting rule is implemented as a suite-level guard, not per-test `skip`s, so the run/not-run state is one fact reported once.\\\\n\\\\nThe capstone reuses Epic 04's lifecycle sequence with the assertion set swapped: where the deterministic leg asserted byte-exact replay and marker content, this leg asserts structure — every `FormKind` reaches `ready` at least once with non-empty text containing no deterministic marker pattern (`/^(smoothed|toolcall|toolresult|rendering|projection|detailed|brief)\\\\\\\\(/`), provenance naming the real model, and the checkpoint-coherence ladder (mutation clears → pending; drain → ready with *different* content; second compact's view reflects post-edit content).\\\\n\\\\n### Flow 5: Turn-End Boundary Advance (AC-5.1–5.5)\\\\n\\\\nTwo modules change. In `pipeline.ts`, the walk already knows whether the batch contained a `turn_end` (it processes the event kinds); the advance registration at line ~150 gains that condition. Everything about the registration — first-before-poke order, throw isolation, both host modes, flush-after-COMMIT — is untouched.\\\\n\\\\nIn `boundary.ts`, the zone read gains `turn_id` and the decision regroups:\\\\n\\\\n```\\\\nreadZoneToolResults: + turn_id per row (same WHERE, same order)\\\\ngroupZone: consecutive rows by turn_id → groups, each { turnId, sourceEventOrders, tokenSum }\\\\n           (turn_id NULL ⇒ singleton group, DD-10)\\\\nadvanceDecision(groups, { maxTokens, targetTokens }):\\\\n  total ≤ max → null\\\\n  walk groups oldest-first, excluding the newest group always:\\\\n    evict group only if (remaining − group.tokenSum) ≥ target   ← peek-ahead, AC-5.3\\\\n    newPosition = group's highest sourceEventOrder\\\\n  return newPosition (lands in [target, target + one group))\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nWorked example (golden G2 in the test plan): groups of 30k/25k/20k/15k tokens (oldest→newest), max 64k, target 32k. Total 90k > max. Evict 30k → 60k remaining, ≥ 32k ✓. Evict 25k → 35k remaining, ≥ 32k ✓. Peek 20k: 35k − 20k = 15k < 32k → stop. Boundary lands after the 25k group; zone = 35k, in [32k, 32k+20k). The 15k newest group was never a candidate.\\\\n\\\\nThe shared-query invariant survives: `visibilityZoneTokens` (status's sum) and the decision's walk read the same WHERE-clause population, so status and advance can never disagree about the zone — the property the existing code makes structural stays structural.\\\\n\\\\n`VisibilityBudgets` loses `floorTokens` everywhere it appears: the type, `resolveViewConfig`'s defaults and validation (which gains `maxTokens > targetTokens`), and the status read's reporting shape is unchanged (`zoneTokens`/`maxTokens` already). Existing Epic 03 tests asserting floor behavior are superseded — the test plan's amendment ledger names each one (the spec-sanctioned test-change path, as Epic 02's F-3 established).\\\\n\\\\n### Flow 6: CLI Retirement (AC-6.1–6.3)\\\\n\\\\nDeletion plus proof. Delete `src/cli/`, the `src/cli.ts` bin entrypoint (a separate file outside the directory — it imports `./cli/index.js` and breaks the build if left), the `bin` manifest entry, and the `dev:cli` script; delete `src/providers/registry.ts` and its two `sdk.ts` re-exports; delete the twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` files; rewrite `verify`/`verify-all` (the `SKIP: cli-process` echo and `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate go; `verify-all` becomes red-verify + full vitest including the self-accounting inference suite). The proof is three-legged: a public-API surface snapshot test (import the package entry, assert the export-name set against a checked-in expected list — catches both leftover CLI exports and accidental removals), a source-level grep test for `LHC_PROVIDER`/registry references, and the unchanged-SDK-coverage check (the full remaining suite green proves no behavior test rode out with the process suites — process suites asserted *parity*, never unique behavior, which is why deletion is safe).\\\\n\\\\nThe Epic 04 deviation note is written into this pack's deviation table at story completion: spawned `inspect health` parity was backfilled before Epic 05, and this story deletes the spawned-process parity surface instead of carrying it forward.\\\\n\\\\n## Interface Definitions\\\\n\\\\n```ts\\\\n// ── inference/types.ts ──────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n\\\\n/** The one function a host supplies. Single-turn completion; provider/model\\\\n *  are opaque routing keys the host's implementation interprets. (AC-1.2) */\\\\nexport type ModelCall = (input: ModelCallInput) => Promise<ModelCallResult>;\\\\n\\\\nexport interface ModelCallInput {\\\\n  provider: string;\\\\n  model: string;\\\\n  messages: { role: \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\"; content: string }[];\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nexport type ModelCallResult =\\\\n  | { ok: true; text: string }\\\\n  | { ok: false; kind: ModelCallFailureKind; message: string };\\\\n\\\\n/** `empty_output` is adapter-generated (AC-2.4); hosts never return it.\\\\n *  Thrown exceptions classify as `other` (AC-3.3). */\\\\nexport type ModelCallFailureKind =\\\\n  | \\\\\\\"rate_limit\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"network\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\"\\\\n  | \\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"invalid_request\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\nexport interface ModelAssignment {\\\\n  provider: string;\\\\n  model: string;\\\\n  prompt: string; // must name a PROMPT_REGISTRY entry (AC-1.3)\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n/** SdkConfig.inference — the alternative to SdkConfig.provider (DD-5). */\\\\nexport interface InferenceConfig {\\\\n  call: ModelCall;\\\\n  assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>; // all seven, validated complete\\\\n  timeoutMs?: number;      // default 60_000 (DD-6)\\\\n  maxInputChars?: number;  // default 200_000 (DD-7)\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// ── inference/prompts/index.ts ──────────────────────────────────\\\\n\\\\nexport interface PromptTemplate<I = unknown> {\\\\n  name: string;\\\\n  render(input: I): ModelCallInput[\\\\\\\"messages\\\\\\\"];\\\\n}\\\\nexport const PROMPT_REGISTRY: Record<string, PromptTemplate<never>>;\\\\n\\\\n// ── inference/classify.ts ───────────────────────────────────────\\\\n\\\\nexport const FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION: Record<ModelCallFailureKind, { retryable: boolean }>;\\\\n/** try/catch + timeout race around the host function (AC-3.3, DD-6). */\\\\nexport function safeCall(\\\\n  call: ModelCall, input: ModelCallInput, timeoutMs: number,\\\\n): Promise<ModelCallResult>;\\\\n\\\\n// ── inference/adapter.ts ────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n\\\\n/** The real provider. Same interface the deterministic provider implements;\\\\n *  createSdk slots it into resolved.provider (DD-2). */\\\\nexport function createInferenceProvider(config: ResolvedInferenceConfig): DerivationProvider;\\\\n\\\\n// ── shared/derivation.ts (additions only) ───────────────────────\\\\n\\\\nexport interface ProviderProvenance { provider: string; model: string; prompt: string }\\\\n// ProviderResult ok-branch: { ok: true; text: string; provenance?: ProviderProvenance }\\\\n// DerivedFormMetadata: + provenance?: ProviderProvenance   (DD-4; AC-2.5)\\\\n\\\\n// ── shared/view.ts (change) ─────────────────────────────────────\\\\n\\\\nexport interface VisibilityBudgets { maxTokens: number; targetTokens: number }\\\\n// defaults 64_000 / 32_000; resolveViewConfig validates maxTokens > targetTokens (AC-5.4)\\\\n\\\\n// ── sdk.ts (change) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n\\\\nexport interface SdkConfig {\\\\n  provider?: DerivationProvider;   // XOR with inference (AC-1.1)\\\\n  inference?: InferenceConfig;\\\\n  // …existing fields unchanged\\\\n}\\\\n// removed exports: resolveNamedProvider, registeredProviderNames (AC-6.1)\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nSkeleton stubs follow the house pattern: `inference/` functions are machine-readable-contract code, so stubs return structured failures (`{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\", message: \\\\\\\"not implemented\\\\\\\" }`) rather than throwing; `createInferenceProvider` may throw `NotImplementedError` at construction since construction errors throw by contract.\\\\n\\\\n## Error Contract\\\\n\\\\nConstruction errors are `TypeError`s naming the violated rule (existing contract): `\\\\\\\"createSdk config: exactly one of provider or inference\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"inference.assignments missing kind chunk_summary_brief\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"inference.assignments.smoothed_prompt.prompt names unknown template 'smoothing-v2'\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"view.visibility.maxTokens must be > targetTokens\\\\\\\"`. Operating failures never throw: they are `ProviderResult` failures with `reason` = the failure kind, flowing into form state via the unchanged Epic 02 paths (`failed` with reason `provider_failure` at exhaustion, terminal kinds immediately).\\\\n\\\\n## Runtime Prerequisites\\\\n\\\\n| Prerequisite | Where | How to Verify |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| Node ≥ 22 (`node:sqlite`, native `fetch`) | Local + CI | `node --version` (existing) |\\\\n| `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY` | Opt-in suite only | Suite self-reports ran/not-ran; never required by CI default |\\\\n| `LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL` | Opt-in suite, optional | Fixture default (cheap model) used when unset |\\\\n\\\\nNo new dependencies. The OpenRouter fixture uses plain `fetch` in test code (epic NFR).\\\\n\\\\n## Testing Strategy\\\\n\\\\nThe mock boundary moves with this epic, and naming it precisely is the design's most important testing statement: **the external boundary is now the `ModelCall` function.** Flows 1–3 test against scripted fake functions (canned successes, scripted failure sequences, recording wrappers) — these are boundary mocks, not internal mocks, because the function *is* where LHC's code ends. The `DerivationProvider` interface is no longer the mock seam for this epic's tests; it's internal wiring under test (the adapter is the unit). Epic 02–04 suites keep their provider-level doubles untouched — their subject was never the adapter.\\\\n\\\\nEverything else follows house rules: real temp SQLite for anything touching thread state (boundary tests, capstone), no internal module mocks, deterministic provider remains the CI default for all existing suites. The opt-in suite is the only network-touching code in the repo and is labeled in `verify-all` accounting as `ran` or `not-ran (reason)` — the four-tier verify scripts keep their names; composition changes are in Flow 6.\\\\n\\\\nTest segmentation after the process suites delete: one vitest tier (default, no network) plus the env-gated inference suite in the same runner. No separate integration runner — the capstone is a vitest test that happens to make real calls when keyed.\\\\n\\\\n## Work Breakdown\\\\n\\\\nChunk order mirrors the epic's story order; the test plan carries per-chunk Red/Green tables.\\\\n\\\\n| Chunk | Scope | ACs | Key risk |\\\\n|-------|-------|-----|----------|\\\\n| 0: Foundation | `inference/types.ts`, provenance type additions, fake-call fixtures (`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`: recording/scripted builders), prompt-template type + registry skeleton | — | Fixture validity: scripted fakes must satisfy the AC-1.2 contract shape |\\\\n| 1: CLI retirement | Flow 6 deletions + proofs + verify-script rewrite | 6.1–6.3 | Deleting a behavior test disguised as a process test (mitigation: surface snapshot + full-suite green before/after comparison) |\\\\n| 2: Seam + assignments | XOR, validation, adapter construction wiring | 1.1–1.4 | Validation drift from `FormKind` (mitigation: iterate the exported union, never a literal list) |\\\\n| 3: Adapter + prompts | Seven operations, templates, bounding, empty-output, provenance | 2.1–2.5 | Provenance authored from output instead of config (it's three config strings — keep it that way) |\\\\n| 4: Classification | Table, `safeCall`, handler provenance copies | 3.1–3.3 | Re-implementing retry logic the queue already owns (the adapter returns `retryable`; nothing more) |\\\\n| 5: Real suite + capstone | OpenRouter fixture, accounting guard, seven round-trips, capstone leg | 4.1–4.2 | Silent skip (the accounting guard is the deliverable; review it first) |\\\\n| 6: Boundary advance | Gate, grouping, peek-ahead, config change, Epic 03 test amendments | 5.1–5.5 | Floor-test amnesia — superseded tests must be amended per the ledger, not deleted wholesale |\\\\n\\\\nDependencies: 0 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5; 1 and 6 are independent of the pipeline chunks (1 first by story order; 6 anytime).\\\\n\\\\n## Deferred Items\\\\n\\\\n| Item | Related | Reason | Where |\\\\n|------|---------|--------|-------|\\\\n| Tuned prompts + model choices | AC-2.3 | Dial-in working period; needs harness corpora | Backfills this pack pre-acceptance |\\\\n| Config generalization (token-band routing, fallback chains) | Flow 1 | Vocabulary derived from seven dialed-in answers | Post-dial-in design |\\\\n| Mid-turn safety ceiling | Flow 5 | POC removed it; no usage evidence of need | Revisit on evidence |\\\\n| Full integrated harness (capture ext, corpora, converter, journeys) | Flow 4 | Paired working period post-epic | `post-epic-4-work.md` |\\\\n| PI extension transport + config values | Flow 1 | Extension PRD | `pi-ext-prd-notes.md` |\\\\n\\\",\\\"numLines\\\":336,\\\"startLine\\\":1,\\\"totalLines\\\":336}}}\\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:38:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"toolu_011UEvf2ef5fA5bCFxwd3URC\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"1\\\\t# Epic 05: Derivation Inference — Test Plan\\\\n2\\\\t\\\\n3\\\\t**Epic:** `epic.md` (22 ACs / 15 TCs)\\\\n4\\\\t**Tech Design:** `tech-design.md`\\\\n5\\\\t**Coverage:** all 15 TCs mapped below; no TC unowned, none double-owned.\\\\n6\\\\t\\\\n7\\\\t## Suite Layout\\\\n8\\\\t\\\\n9\\\\t| File | Tier | TCs |\\\\n10\\\\t|------|------|-----|\\\\n11\\\\t| `test/inference-construction.test.ts` | default | TC-1.1 |\\\\n12\\\\t| `test/inference-routing.test.ts` | default | TC-1.2 |\\\\n13\\\\t| `test/inference-adapter.test.ts` | default | TC-2.1, TC-2.3 |\\\\n14\\\\t| `test/inference-prompts.test.ts` | default | TC-2.2 |\\\\n15\\\\t| `test/inference-classification.test.ts` | default | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 |\\\\n16\\\\t| `test/inference-real.test.ts` | opt-in (`LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`) | TC-4.1, TC-4.2, TC-4.3 |\\\\n17\\\\t| `test/view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + `test/view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` (new) | default | TC-5.1, TC-5.2, TC-5.3 |\\\\n18\\\\t| `test/retirement.test.ts` | default | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 |\\\\n19\\\\t\\\\n20\\\\tDeleted with Flow 6: the twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` files. The `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate leaves `verify-all`; the opt-in inference suite self-reports inside the default runner (it is env-gated, not script-gated).\\\\n21\\\\t\\\\n22\\\\t## Fixture Contracts\\\\n23\\\\t\\\\n24\\\\t**`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the scripted-host builders every Flow 1–3 test uses:\\\\n25\\\\t\\\\n26\\\\t- `recordingCall(responses: Record<FormKind, string>)` → `{ call: ModelCall, log: ModelCallInput[] }`. Resolves each call with the kind's canned text (kind inferred from the assignment the test built — in practice keyed by `model` string, which tests assign uniquely per kind). Every input pushed to `log`.\\\\n27\\\\t- `scriptedCall(script: ModelCallResult[])` → `ModelCall` returning script entries in order; throws if exhausted (a test calling more than scripted is a test bug, loudly).\\\\n28\\\\t- `throwingCall(error: Error)` → `ModelCall` that rejects/throws.\\\\n29\\\\t- `hangingCall()` → `ModelCall` that never settles (timeout leg; tests pass a small `timeoutMs`).\\\\n30\\\\t- `validAssignments(overrides?)` → a complete seven-kind assignment map for construction tests, each kind assigned a distinct fake provider/model string.\\\\n31\\\\t\\\\n32\\\\tAll builders return contract-conformant shapes (AC-1.2); the conformance helper below runs against `recordingCall`'s output in its own test, so fixture drift from the contract is caught by the suite, not discovered in stories.\\\\n33\\\\t\\\\n34\\\\t**`test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts`** (Chunk 5) — the real host: `createOpenRouterCall(key, defaultModel)` → `ModelCall` over `fetch` to OpenRouter's OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint; implements the exact `ModelCall` contract (OpenRouter takes one model slug, so the fixture resolves the `provider`+`model` routing keys into that slug — the host-side interpretation AC-1.2 grants), maps HTTP 401/403 → `auth`, 429 → `rate_limit`, 400 → `invalid_request`, network errors → `network`, everything else → `other`, and extracts non-empty assistant text into the `ModelCallResult` success shape. `resolveRealSuiteEnv()` → `{ key, model } | { notRan: reason }`.\\\\n35\\\\t\\\\n36\\\\t**`test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the parameterized contract assertions (DD-13): `assertModelCallContract(call)` (shape of success/failure results on a probe call) and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(call, assignments)` (TC-1.2's routing assertions extracted so TC-4.3 runs them against the real host unchanged).\\\\n37\\\\t\\\\n38\\\\t**Boundary fixtures** — `test/fixtures/` gains `seedTurnedToolResults(db, turns: { results: number[] }[])`: closed turns with tool results of given token estimates, plus turnless-result and open-turn variants. Built on the existing Epic 03 fixture helpers; token estimates seeded via content sized to the estimator (existing pattern from view-boundary tests).\\\\n39\\\\t\\\\n40\\\\t## TC → Test Mapping\\\\n41\\\\t\\\\n42\\\\t### TC-1.1 — Construction matrix (AC-1.1, AC-1.3)\\\\n43\\\\t`inference-construction.test.ts`\\\\n44\\\\t- missing one kind (each of seven, parameterized) → `TypeError` naming the kind\\\\n45\\\\t- unknown prompt name → `TypeError` naming kind and prompt\\\\n46\\\\t- unknown kind key in assignments → `TypeError` naming it\\\\n47\\\\t- both `provider` and `inference` → `TypeError` naming the XOR rule\\\\n48\\\\t- neither → `TypeError` naming the XOR rule\\\\n49\\\\t- empty `provider`/`model` string in one assignment → `TypeError`\\\\n50\\\\t- complete valid config → constructs; a seeded drain lands a form `ready` (operates, not just constructs)\\\\n51\\\\t\\\\n52\\\\t### TC-1.2 — Routing (AC-1.2, AC-1.4)\\\\n53\\\\t`inference-routing.test.ts` — `recordingCall` + seven distinct lanes in `validAssignments`:\\\\n54\\\\t- seed a thread exercising all seven kinds; drain; assert `log` contains each kind's call carrying exactly its assigned provider/model strings\\\\n55\\\\t- every logged `messages` is single-turn shape (`system`+`user` roles only, string content)\\\\n56\\\\t- three-lane mixed config: per-call routing matches item kind with no cross-kind bleed\\\\n57\\\\t- `assertModelCallContract(recordingCall(...).call)` — fixture conformance leg\\\\n58\\\\t\\\\n59\\\\t### TC-2.1 — Seven kinds land with canned content + provenance (AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.5)\\\\n60\\\\t`inference-adapter.test.ts`\\\\n61\\\\t- drain seeded thread → all seven `FormKind`s `ready`; content equals the canned text (post-shaping)\\\\n62\\\\t- handler equivalence: the same seeded thread drained under the deterministic provider lands the same form rows (states, subjects) with marker content — proving handlers unchanged (AC-2.1)\\\\n63\\\\t- outcomes/receipts on tool and rendering forms match the record, not the canned text (canned text deliberately claims the wrong outcome; the stamp must disagree with it)\\\\n64\\\\t- each form's `metadata.provenance` equals its assignment `{ provider, model, prompt }`; deterministic-provider forms carry none\\\\n65\\\\t\\\\n66\\\\t### TC-2.2 — Prompt-rendering goldens (AC-2.2, AC-2.3)\\\\n67\\\\t`inference-prompts.test.ts` — golden files in `test/goldens/prompts/`:\\\\n68\\\\t- for each of the seven templates: `render(fixtureInput)` matches its golden (`smoothing-v1.golden.json` …) — fixture content embedded, single-turn shape\\\\n69\\\\t- registry completeness: every config-selectable name resolves; `PROMPT_REGISTRY` covers all seven default names\\\\n70\\\\t- brief-summary stripping: drive `summarizeChunkBrief` through the adapter with a recording call; the rendered messages contain outcome tokens but no receipt text from the fixture (the Epic 02 stripping contract holds through the adapter)\\\\n71\\\\t- bounding (DD-7): a `summarizeToolResult` input over `maxInputChars` renders with head+tail+marker, under-limit input renders whole\\\\n72\\\\t\\\\n73\\\\t### TC-2.3 — Empty output is a failure (AC-2.4)\\\\n74\\\\t`inference-adapter.test.ts`\\\\n75\\\\t- `scriptedCall([{ ok: true, text: \\\\\\\"  \\\\\\\" }, { ok: true, text: \\\\\\\"real\\\\\\\" }])` → first attempt classifies `empty_output` retryable; retry lands `ready` with `\\\\\\\"real\\\\\\\"`\\\\n76\\\\t- exhaustion leg: all-whitespace script → form `failed`, reason `provider_failure`, last error names `empty_output`\\\\n77\\\\t\\\\n78\\\\t### TC-3.1 — Classification table drives retry/terminal (AC-3.1, AC-3.2)\\\\n79\\\\t`inference-classification.test.ts`\\\\n80\\\\t- table assertion: `FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION` matches the AC-3.1 mapping exactly (data asserted as data)\\\\n81\\\\t- `rate_limit` ×2 then success → `ready`; attempts recorded on the path (report shows retrying mid-way under a paused clock, per existing Epic 02 clock-injection pattern)\\\\n82\\\\t- `auth` → `failed` first attempt; scripted call shows exactly one invocation; stable reason\\\\n83\\\\t- `network` to exhaustion → `failed`, `provider_failure`, `metadata.lastError` preserved\\\\n84\\\\t\\\\n85\\\\t### TC-3.2 — Exception containment (AC-3.2, AC-3.3)\\\\n86\\\\t`inference-classification.test.ts`\\\\n87\\\\t- `throwingCall` on one kind amid a multi-kind drain → that item retries as `other`; all other items complete; drain returns normally (no escape)\\\\n88\\\\t- exhaustion lands thrown message as last error\\\\n89\\\\t- `hangingCall` with `timeoutMs: 50` → classified `timeout`, drain continues (DD-6 leg)\\\\n90\\\\t\\\\n91\\\\t### TC-4.1 — Real round-trips + accounting (AC-4.1)\\\\n92\\\\t`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n93\\\\t- keyed: each of seven kinds round-trips once → `ready`, non-empty, no marker pattern\\\\n94\\\\t- unkeyed (controlled: suite re-invoked with key stripped from env in a subprocess-free way — the guard function called directly with empty env) → `notRan` record with reason; assert the record's shape and that it is not a pass\\\\n95\\\\t- the suite-level guard emits exactly one ran/not-ran line into the run output\\\\n96\\\\t\\\\n97\\\\t### TC-4.2 — Capstone (AC-4.2)\\\\n98\\\\t`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n99\\\\t- Epic 04's lifecycle sequence against the real adapter: intake → drain → compact → pull → inspect → edit → rebuild → drain → compact → materialize\\\\n100\\\\t- structural assertions: every kind `ready` ≥ once; no marker pattern anywhere; provenance names the real model; mutation-cleared forms regenerate with content ≠ pre-edit content; second compact's view reflects post-edit content; health coherent at each checkpoint\\\\n101\\\\t\\\\n102\\\\t### TC-4.3 — Contract conformance, both hosts (AC-1.2, AC-4.1)\\\\n103\\\\t`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n104\\\\t- `assertModelCallContract(openRouterCall)` and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(openRouterCall, realAssignments)` — the same helpers TC-1.2 ran against the fake, unchanged (DD-13)\\\\n105\\\\t\\\\n106\\\\t### TC-5.1 — Turn-end trigger + whole-turn eviction (AC-5.1, AC-5.2)\\\\n107\\\\t`view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts`\\\\n108\\\\t- mid-turn batches push zone past max → boundary unmoved; consecutive pulls byte-identical\\\\n109\\\\t- the `turn_end` batch commits → one advance; flipped set = whole oldest turns (every tool result in each evicted turn flipped together; assert per-turn all-or-nothing)\\\\n110\\\\t- next small turn closes under max → no movement\\\\n111\\\\t- turnless tool result (singleton group, DD-10) evicts whole-message\\\\n112\\\\t\\\\n113\\\\t### TC-5.2 — Peek-ahead landing + config (AC-5.3, AC-5.4)\\\\n114\\\\t`view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` — golden **G2** (worked in the design): 30k/25k/20k/15k turns, 64k/32k → boundary after the 25k group, zone 35k\\\\n115\\\\t- landing always in [target, target + one turn): G2 plus a variant where evicting the next turn would land exactly at target (boundary advances through it — `≥` boundary-condition leg)\\\\n116\\\\t- newest closed turn never evicted even when alone over target (oversized-newest variant)\\\\n117\\\\t- `maxTokens ≤ targetTokens` → construction `TypeError` naming the constraint; defaults resolve 64k/32k\\\\n118\\\\t\\\\n119\\\\t### TC-5.3 — Epic 03 contract regression under the new trigger (AC-5.5)\\\\n120\\\\t`view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + new file\\\\n121\\\\t- monotonic: flipped results stay flipped across later turn closes\\\\n122\\\\t- short form: summary-when-ready else truncation (existing assertions re-driven by turn-end trigger)\\\\n123\\\\t- compact reset: post-compact boundary = compact point, fresh full tail\\\\n124\\\\t- determinism: same record + budgets replayed → identical boundary trajectory (golden **G1**, re-cut for turn grouping)\\\\n125\\\\t- failure injection at `post-commit-advance` → intake unaffected, boundary unchanged, status shows over-budget zone, next turn close heals (Epic 03's failure-injection machinery — the `post-commit-advance` hook — retriggered)\\\\n126\\\\t\\\\n127\\\\t### TC-6.1 — Deletion proof (AC-6.1, AC-6.2)\\\\n128\\\\t`retirement.test.ts`\\\\n129\\\\t- public-API surface snapshot: export-name set of the package entry equals the checked-in SDK-only list (no `resolveNamedProvider`, no `registeredProviderNames`)\\\\n130\\\\t- package manifest has no `bin`\\\\n131\\\\t- full default suite green is the suite run itself; the SDK-coverage comparison is a one-time story-completion check recorded in the story DoD (suite files and their domain-operation coverage unchanged from pre-deletion, process files excepted)\\\\n132\\\\t\\\\n133\\\\t### TC-6.2 — Resolution path gone (AC-6.3)\\\\n134\\\\t`retirement.test.ts`\\\\n135\\\\t- source scan: zero `LHC_PROVIDER` / `--provider` references under `src/`\\\\n136\\\\t- constructing with neither `provider` nor `inference` → the XOR `TypeError` (no fallback resolution path exists to catch it)\\\\n137\\\\t\\\\n138\\\\t## Epic 03 Test Amendment Ledger (Flow 5 / Chunk 6)\\\\n139\\\\t\\\\n140\\\\tSpec-sanctioned amendments (the Epic 02 F-3 pattern: named, reasoned, red-manifest regenerated):\\\\n141\\\\t\\\\n142\\\\t| Existing test (in `view-boundary.test.ts` / fixtures) | Change | Reason |\\\\n143\\\\t|---|---|---|\\\\n144\\\\t| floor-protection legs (newest-whole-message floor, oversized-newest-result, floor-blocks-target cases) | superseded → replaced by newest-turn-protection legs in TC-5.2 | `floorTokens` retired (AC-5.4, DD-11) |\\\\n145\\\\t| per-intake-advance legs (advance fires on non-turn-end batches) | inverted → become TC-5.1's mid-turn-never-moves assertions | trigger moved (AC-5.1) |\\\\n146\\\\t| message-grained eviction goldens (G1 as cut for Epic 03) | re-cut for turn grouping | eviction granularity (AC-5.2) |\\\\n147\\\\t| construction tests passing `floorTokens` | updated to two-field budgets; one new test: passing `floorTokens` is rejected as unknown config | config surface change |\\\\n148\\\\t| status `zoneTokens` legs | unchanged — shared-query invariant holds | none needed |\\\\n149\\\\t\\\\n150\\\\tLifecycle/capstone deterministic legs (Epic 04's exercise) are untouched: they run the deterministic provider and never asserted boundary trigger timing.\\\\n151\\\\t\\\\n152\\\\t## Red/Green per Chunk\\\\n153\\\\t\\\\n154\\\\tHouse pattern: Red = failing tests + skeleton stubs returning structured not-implemented failures; `red-verify` (build, typecheck, lint, boundaries — no behavior tests); Green = implementation until the chunk's tests pass; immutability check on test files after Green.\\\\n155\\\\t\\\\n156\\\\t| Chunk | Red writes | Green implements | Exit gate |\\\\n157\\\\t|-------|-----------|------------------|-----------|\\\\n158\\\\t| 0: Foundation | fixture self-conformance test; prompt-registry completeness test (against skeleton registry) | `inference/types.ts`, fixture builders, registry skeleton, provenance type additions | `red-verify` + fixture conformance green; boundaries check includes `inference/ ↛ domains/` |\\\\n159\\\\t| 1: CLI retirement | `retirement.test.ts` (snapshot list authored = the post-deletion expected set) | deletions, manifest, verify-script rewrite | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 green; full default suite green; DoD coverage comparison recorded |\\\\n160\\\\t| 2: Seam | `inference-construction`, `inference-routing` | XOR + validation in `sdk.ts`, adapter construction wiring | TC-1.1, TC-1.2 green |\\\\n161\\\\t| 3: Adapter + prompts | `inference-adapter`, `inference-prompts` + goldens | seven operations, templates, bounding, empty-output | TC-2.1–2.3 green |\\\\n162\\\\t| 4: Classification | `inference-classification` | table, `safeCall`, handler provenance copies | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 green; full Epic 02 derivation suites still green (machinery untouched proof) |\\\\n163\\\\t| 5: Real suite | `inference-real` (guard testable unkeyed) | OpenRouter fixture, accounting guard, capstone leg | unkeyed: not-ran record asserted in CI; keyed: TC-4.1–4.3 green locally (run recorded in story DoD with model used) |\\\\n164\\\\t| 6: Boundary | `view-boundary-turn-end` + amendment ledger applied | gate, grouping, peek-ahead, config change | TC-5.1–5.3 green; amended Epic 03 suite green; red-manifest regenerated |\\\\n165\\\\t\\\\n166\\\\t**Story-completion accounting for Chunk 5:** the keyed run cannot gate CI (no secret in CI by design). The story's DoD requires a recorded keyed run — date, model, pass state — in the deviation/completion notes. A keyed run that has never happened blocks epic acceptance: the capstone is the integration-readiness gate, and an unexercised gate is the silent-skip failure mode this suite exists to prevent.\\\\n167\\\\t\\\\n168\\\\t## Architecture-Risk Tests\\\\n169\\\\t\\\\n170\\\\t| Risk | Test |\\\\n171\\\\t|------|------|\\\\n172\\\\t| Adapter parses model output for mechanical facts | TC-2.1's adversarial canned text (claims wrong outcome; stamp must come from record) |\\\\n173\\\\t| Validation drifts from `FormKind` union | TC-1.1 parameterizes over the exported union; a new kind fails the matrix automatically |\\\\n174\\\\t| Queue machinery quietly forked for inference | Chunk 4 exit gate: Epic 02 derivation suites green untouched |\\\\n175\\\\t| Boundary decision and status sum diverge | shared-query invariant + TC-5.3's status leg after each advance |\\\\n176\\\\t| Silent real-suite skip | TC-4.1's unkeyed leg asserts the not-ran record exists and is not a pass |\\\\n177\\\\t| Behavior test deleted with process suites | TC-6.1 snapshot + full-suite green + DoD coverage comparison |\\\\n178\\\\t\\\"}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"f6b9fc74-c799-40c7-88e4-2283ea0d92c3\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-06-12T23:20:46.113Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"file\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"# Epic 05: Derivation Inference — Test Plan\\\\n\\\\n**Epic:** `epic.md` (22 ACs / 15 TCs)\\\\n**Tech Design:** `tech-design.md`\\\\n**Coverage:** all 15 TCs mapped below; no TC unowned, none double-owned.\\\\n\\\\n## Suite Layout\\\\n\\\\n| File | Tier | TCs |\\\\n|------|------|-----|\\\\n| `test/inference-construction.test.ts` | default | TC-1.1 |\\\\n| `test/inference-routing.test.ts` | default | TC-1.2 |\\\\n| `test/inference-adapter.test.ts` | default | TC-2.1, TC-2.3 |\\\\n| `test/inference-prompts.test.ts` | default | TC-2.2 |\\\\n| `test/inference-classification.test.ts` | default | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 |\\\\n| `test/inference-real.test.ts` | opt-in (`LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`) | TC-4.1, TC-4.2, TC-4.3 |\\\\n| `test/view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + `test/view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` (new) | default | TC-5.1, TC-5.2, TC-5.3 |\\\\n| `test/retirement.test.ts` | default | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 |\\\\n\\\\nDeleted with Flow 6: the twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` files. The `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate leaves `verify-all`; the opt-in inference suite self-reports inside the default runner (it is env-gated, not script-gated).\\\\n\\\\n## Fixture Contracts\\\\n\\\\n**`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the scripted-host builders every Flow 1–3 test uses:\\\\n\\\\n- `recordingCall(responses: Record<FormKind, string>)` → `{ call: ModelCall, log: ModelCallInput[] }`. Resolves each call with the kind's canned text (kind inferred from the assignment the test built — in practice keyed by `model` string, which tests assign uniquely per kind). Every input pushed to `log`.\\\\n- `scriptedCall(script: ModelCallResult[])` → `ModelCall` returning script entries in order; throws if exhausted (a test calling more than scripted is a test bug, loudly).\\\\n- `throwingCall(error: Error)` → `ModelCall` that rejects/throws.\\\\n- `hangingCall()` → `ModelCall` that never settles (timeout leg; tests pass a small `timeoutMs`).\\\\n- `validAssignments(overrides?)` → a complete seven-kind assignment map for construction tests, each kind assigned a distinct fake provider/model string.\\\\n\\\\nAll builders return contract-conformant shapes (AC-1.2); the conformance helper below runs against `recordingCall`'s output in its own test, so fixture drift from the contract is caught by the suite, not discovered in stories.\\\\n\\\\n**`test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts`** (Chunk 5) — the real host: `createOpenRouterCall(key, defaultModel)` → `ModelCall` over `fetch` to OpenRouter's OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint; implements the exact `ModelCall` contract (OpenRouter takes one model slug, so the fixture resolves the `provider`+`model` routing keys into that slug — the host-side interpretation AC-1.2 grants), maps HTTP 401/403 → `auth`, 429 → `rate_limit`, 400 → `invalid_request`, network errors → `network`, everything else → `other`, and extracts non-empty assistant text into the `ModelCallResult` success shape. `resolveRealSuiteEnv()` → `{ key, model } | { notRan: reason }`.\\\\n\\\\n**`test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the parameterized contract assertions (DD-13): `assertModelCallContract(call)` (shape of success/failure results on a probe call) and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(call, assignments)` (TC-1.2's routing assertions extracted so TC-4.3 runs them against the real host unchanged).\\\\n\\\\n**Boundary fixtures** — `test/fixtures/` gains `seedTurnedToolResults(db, turns: { results: number[] }[])`: closed turns with tool results of given token estimates, plus turnless-result and open-turn variants. Built on the existing Epic 03 fixture helpers; token estimates seeded via content sized to the estimator (existing pattern from view-boundary tests).\\\\n\\\\n## TC → Test Mapping\\\\n\\\\n### TC-1.1 — Construction matrix (AC-1.1, AC-1.3)\\\\n`inference-construction.test.ts`\\\\n- missing one kind (each of seven, parameterized) → `TypeError` naming the kind\\\\n- unknown prompt name → `TypeError` naming kind and prompt\\\\n- unknown kind key in assignments → `TypeError` naming it\\\\n- both `provider` and `inference` → `TypeError` naming the XOR rule\\\\n- neither → `TypeError` naming the XOR rule\\\\n- empty `provider`/`model` string in one assignment → `TypeError`\\\\n- complete valid config → constructs; a seeded drain lands a form `ready` (operates, not just constructs)\\\\n\\\\n### TC-1.2 — Routing (AC-1.2, AC-1.4)\\\\n`inference-routing.test.ts` — `recordingCall` + seven distinct lanes in `validAssignments`:\\\\n- seed a thread exercising all seven kinds; drain; assert `log` contains each kind's call carrying exactly its assigned provider/model strings\\\\n- every logged `messages` is single-turn shape (`system`+`user` roles only, string content)\\\\n- three-lane mixed config: per-call routing matches item kind with no cross-kind bleed\\\\n- `assertModelCallContract(recordingCall(...).call)` — fixture conformance leg\\\\n\\\\n### TC-2.1 — Seven kinds land with canned content + provenance (AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.5)\\\\n`inference-adapter.test.ts`\\\\n- drain seeded thread → all seven `FormKind`s `ready`; content equals the canned text (post-shaping)\\\\n- handler equivalence: the same seeded thread drained under the deterministic provider lands the same form rows (states, subjects) with marker content — proving handlers unchanged (AC-2.1)\\\\n- outcomes/receipts on tool and rendering forms match the record, not the canned text (canned text deliberately claims the wrong outcome; the stamp must disagree with it)\\\\n- each form's `metadata.provenance` equals its assignment `{ provider, model, prompt }`; deterministic-provider forms carry none\\\\n\\\\n### TC-2.2 — Prompt-rendering goldens (AC-2.2, AC-2.3)\\\\n`inference-prompts.test.ts` — golden files in `test/goldens/prompts/`:\\\\n- for each of the seven templates: `render(fixtureInput)` matches its golden (`smoothing-v1.golden.json` …) — fixture content embedded, single-turn shape\\\\n- registry completeness: every config-selectable name resolves; `PROMPT_REGISTRY` covers all seven default names\\\\n- brief-summary stripping: drive `summarizeChunkBrief` through the adapter with a recording call; the rendered messages contain outcome tokens but no receipt text from the fixture (the Epic 02 stripping contract holds through the adapter)\\\\n- bounding (DD-7): a `summarizeToolResult` input over `maxInputChars` renders with head+tail+marker, under-limit input renders whole\\\\n\\\\n### TC-2.3 — Empty output is a failure (AC-2.4)\\\\n`inference-adapter.test.ts`\\\\n- `scriptedCall([{ ok: true, text: \\\\\\\"  \\\\\\\" }, { ok: true, text: \\\\\\\"real\\\\\\\" }])` → first attempt classifies `empty_output` retryable; retry lands `ready` with `\\\\\\\"real\\\\\\\"`\\\\n- exhaustion leg: all-whitespace script → form `failed`, reason `provider_failure`, last error names `empty_output`\\\\n\\\\n### TC-3.1 — Classification table drives retry/terminal (AC-3.1, AC-3.2)\\\\n`inference-classification.test.ts`\\\\n- table assertion: `FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION` matches the AC-3.1 mapping exactly (data asserted as data)\\\\n- `rate_limit` ×2 then success → `ready`; attempts recorded on the path (report shows retrying mid-way under a paused clock, per existing Epic 02 clock-injection pattern)\\\\n- `auth` → `failed` first attempt; scripted call shows exactly one invocation; stable reason\\\\n- `network` to exhaustion → `failed`, `provider_failure`, `metadata.lastError` preserved\\\\n\\\\n### TC-3.2 — Exception containment (AC-3.2, AC-3.3)\\\\n`inference-classification.test.ts`\\\\n- `throwingCall` on one kind amid a multi-kind drain → that item retries as `other`; all other items complete; drain returns normally (no escape)\\\\n- exhaustion lands thrown message as last error\\\\n- `hangingCall` with `timeoutMs: 50` → classified `timeout`, drain continues (DD-6 leg)\\\\n\\\\n### TC-4.1 — Real round-trips + accounting (AC-4.1)\\\\n`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n- keyed: each of seven kinds round-trips once → `ready`, non-empty, no marker pattern\\\\n- unkeyed (controlled: suite re-invoked with key stripped from env in a subprocess-free way — the guard function called directly with empty env) → `notRan` record with reason; assert the record's shape and that it is not a pass\\\\n- the suite-level guard emits exactly one ran/not-ran line into the run output\\\\n\\\\n### TC-4.2 — Capstone (AC-4.2)\\\\n`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n- Epic 04's lifecycle sequence against the real adapter: intake → drain → compact → pull → inspect → edit → rebuild → drain → compact → materialize\\\\n- structural assertions: every kind `ready` ≥ once; no marker pattern anywhere; provenance names the real model; mutation-cleared forms regenerate with content ≠ pre-edit content; second compact's view reflects post-edit content; health coherent at each checkpoint\\\\n\\\\n### TC-4.3 — Contract conformance, both hosts (AC-1.2, AC-4.1)\\\\n`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\n- `assertModelCallContract(openRouterCall)` and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(openRouterCall, realAssignments)` — the same helpers TC-1.2 ran against the fake, unchanged (DD-13)\\\\n\\\\n### TC-5.1 — Turn-end trigger + whole-turn eviction (AC-5.1, AC-5.2)\\\\n`view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts`\\\\n- mid-turn batches push zone past max → boundary unmoved; consecutive pulls byte-identical\\\\n- the `turn_end` batch commits → one advance; flipped set = whole oldest turns (every tool result in each evicted turn flipped together; assert per-turn all-or-nothing)\\\\n- next small turn closes under max → no movement\\\\n- turnless tool result (singleton group, DD-10) evicts whole-message\\\\n\\\\n### TC-5.2 — Peek-ahead landing + config (AC-5.3, AC-5.4)\\\\n`view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` — golden **G2** (worked in the design): 30k/25k/20k/15k turns, 64k/32k → boundary after the 25k group, zone 35k\\\\n- landing always in [target, target + one turn): G2 plus a variant where evicting the next turn would land exactly at target (boundary advances through it — `≥` boundary-condition leg)\\\\n- newest closed turn never evicted even when alone over target (oversized-newest variant)\\\\n- `maxTokens ≤ targetTokens` → construction `TypeError` naming the constraint; defaults resolve 64k/32k\\\\n\\\\n### TC-5.3 — Epic 03 contract regression under the new trigger (AC-5.5)\\\\n`view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + new file\\\\n- monotonic: flipped results stay flipped across later turn closes\\\\n- short form: summary-when-ready else truncation (existing assertions re-driven by turn-end trigger)\\\\n- compact reset: post-compact boundary = compact point, fresh full tail\\\\n- determinism: same record + budgets replayed → identical boundary trajectory (golden **G1**, re-cut for turn grouping)\\\\n- failure injection at `post-commit-advance` → intake unaffected, boundary unchanged, status shows over-budget zone, next turn close heals (Epic 03's failure-injection machinery — the `post-commit-advance` hook — retriggered)\\\\n\\\\n### TC-6.1 — Deletion proof (AC-6.1, AC-6.2)\\\\n`retirement.test.ts`\\\\n- public-API surface snapshot: export-name set of the package entry equals the checked-in SDK-only list (no `resolveNamedProvider`, no `registeredProviderNames`)\\\\n- package manifest has no `bin`\\\\n- full default suite green is the suite run itself; the SDK-coverage comparison is a one-time story-completion check recorded in the story DoD (suite files and their domain-operation coverage unchanged from pre-deletion, process files excepted)\\\\n\\\\n### TC-6.2 — Resolution path gone (AC-6.3)\\\\n`retirement.test.ts`\\\\n- source scan: zero `LHC_PROVIDER` / `--provider` references under `src/`\\\\n- constructing with neither `provider` nor `inference` → the XOR `TypeError` (no fallback resolution path exists to catch it)\\\\n\\\\n## Epic 03 Test Amendment Ledger (Flow 5 / Chunk 6)\\\\n\\\\nSpec-sanctioned amendments (the Epic 02 F-3 pattern: named, reasoned, red-manifest regenerated):\\\\n\\\\n| Existing test (in `view-boundary.test.ts` / fixtures) | Change | Reason |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| floor-protection legs (newest-whole-message floor, oversized-newest-result, floor-blocks-target cases) | superseded → replaced by newest-turn-protection legs in TC-5.2 | `floorTokens` retired (AC-5.4, DD-11) |\\\\n| per-intake-advance legs (advance fires on non-turn-end batches) | inverted → become TC-5.1's mid-turn-never-moves assertions | trigger moved (AC-5.1) |\\\\n| message-grained eviction goldens (G1 as cut for Epic 03) | re-cut for turn grouping | eviction granularity (AC-5.2) |\\\\n| construction tests passing `floorTokens` | updated to two-field budgets; one new test: passing `floorTokens` is rejected as unknown config | config surface change |\\\\n| status `zoneTokens` legs | unchanged — shared-query invariant holds | none needed |\\\\n\\\\nLifecycle/capstone deterministic legs (Epic 04's exercise) are untouched: they run the deterministic provider and never asserted boundary trigger timing.\\\\n\\\\n## Red/Green per Chunk\\\\n\\\\nHouse pattern: Red = failing tests + skeleton stubs returning structured not-implemented failures; `red-verify` (build, typecheck, lint, boundaries — no behavior tests); Green = implementation until the chunk's tests pass; immutability check on test files after Green.\\\\n\\\\n| Chunk | Red writes | Green implements | Exit gate |\\\\n|-------|-----------|------------------|-----------|\\\\n| 0: Foundation | fixture self-conformance test; prompt-registry completeness test (against skeleton registry) | `inference/types.ts`, fixture builders, registry skeleton, provenance type additions | `red-verify` + fixture conformance green; boundaries check includes `inference/ ↛ domains/` |\\\\n| 1: CLI retirement | `retirement.test.ts` (snapshot list authored = the post-deletion expected set) | deletions, manifest, verify-script rewrite | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 green; full default suite green; DoD coverage comparison recorded |\\\\n| 2: Seam | `inference-construction`, `inference-routing` | XOR + validation in `sdk.ts`, adapter construction wiring | TC-1.1, TC-1.2 green |\\\\n| 3: Adapter + prompts | `inference-adapter`, `inference-prompts` + goldens | seven operations, templates, bounding, empty-output | TC-2.1–2.3 green |\\\\n| 4: Classification | `inference-classification` | table, `safeCall`, handler provenance copies | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 green; full Epic 02 derivation suites still green (machinery untouched proof) |\\\\n| 5: Real suite | `inference-real` (guard testable unkeyed) | OpenRouter fixture, accounting guard, capstone leg | unkeyed: not-ran record asserted in CI; keyed: TC-4.1–4.3 green locally (run recorded in story DoD with model used) |\\\\n| 6: Boundary | `view-boundary-turn-end` + amendment ledger applied | gate, grouping, peek-ahead, config change | TC-5.1–5.3 green; amended Epic 03 suite green; red-manifest regenerated |\\\\n\\\\n**Story-completion accounting for Chunk 5:** the keyed run cannot gate CI (no secret in CI by design). The story's DoD requires a recorded keyed run — date, model, pass state — in the deviation/completion notes. A keyed run that has never happened blocks epic acceptance: the capstone is the integration-readiness gate, and an unexercised gate is the silent-skip failure mode this suite exists to prevent.\\\\n\\\\n## Architecture-Risk Tests\\\\n\\\\n| Risk | Test |\\\\n|------|------|\\\\n| Adapter parses model output for mechanical facts | TC-2.1's adversarial canned text (claims wrong outcome; stamp must come from record) |\\\\n| Validation drifts from `FormKind` union | TC-1.1 parameterizes over the exported union; a new kind fails the matrix automatically |\\\\n| Queue machinery quietly forked for inference | Chunk 4 exit gate: Epic 02 derivation suites green untouched |\\\\n| Boundary decision and status sum diverge | shared-query invariant + TC-5.3's status leg after each advance |\\\\n| Silent real-suite skip | TC-4.1's unkeyed leg asserts the not-ran record exists and is not a pass |\\\\n| Behavior test deleted with process suites | TC-6.1 snapshot + full-suite green + DoD coverage comparison |\\\\n\\\",\\\"numLines\\\":178,\\\"startLine\\\":1,\\\"totalLines\\\":178}}}\\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:126:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"toolu_01TC3sDjL7Qvv6GXpRvM4FJ9\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"1\\\\t// Parameterized seam-contract assertions (Epic 05 DD-13): the same helpers\\\\n2\\\\t// run against the scripted fakes (TC-1.2) and, unchanged, against the real\\\\n3\\\\t// OpenRouter host (TC-4.3) — shared assertion code, never duplicated tests.\\\\n4\\\\t// Framework-agnostic on purpose: plain node:assert throws surface in vitest\\\\n5\\\\t// and in any future runner identically.\\\\n6\\\\timport assert from \\\\\\\"node:assert/strict\\\\\\\";\\\\n7\\\\timport { createSdk, type DerivedForm, type Lhc } from \\\\\\\"../../src/index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n8\\\\timport { FORM_KINDS, type FormKind } from \\\\\\\"../../src/shared/derivation.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n9\\\\timport type {\\\\n10\\\\t  ModelAssignment,\\\\n11\\\\t  ModelCall,\\\\n12\\\\t  ModelCallInput,\\\\n13\\\\t} from \\\\\\\"../../src/inference/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n14\\\\timport { readDerivedForms } from \\\\\\\"./threads.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n15\\\\timport { validEvent, type TempStore } from \\\\\\\"./index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n16\\\\t\\\\n17\\\\tconst FAILURE_KINDS = new Set([\\\\n18\\\\t  \\\\\\\"rate_limit\\\\\\\",\\\\n19\\\\t  \\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\",\\\\n20\\\\t  \\\\\\\"network\\\\\\\",\\\\n21\\\\t  \\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\",\\\\n22\\\\t  \\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\",\\\\n23\\\\t  \\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\",\\\\n24\\\\t  \\\\\\\"invalid_request\\\\\\\",\\\\n25\\\\t]);\\\\n26\\\\t\\\\n27\\\\texport function probeInput(overrides: Partial<ModelCallInput> = {}): ModelCallInput {\\\\n28\\\\t  return {\\\\n29\\\\t    provider: \\\\\\\"probe-provider\\\\\\\",\\\\n30\\\\t    model: \\\\\\\"probe-model\\\\\\\",\\\\n31\\\\t    messages: [\\\\n32\\\\t      { role: \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\"You answer with one word.\\\\\\\" },\\\\n33\\\\t      { role: \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\"Say ok.\\\\\\\" },\\\\n34\\\\t    ],\\\\n35\\\\t    ...overrides,\\\\n36\\\\t  };\\\\n37\\\\t}\\\\n38\\\\t\\\\n39\\\\t// AC-1.2's result contract on one probe call: a ModelCall resolves to either\\\\n40\\\\t// `{ ok: true, text }` or `{ ok: false, kind, message }` with `kind` from the\\\\n41\\\\t// failure vocabulary. Catches fixture (and real-host) drift from the\\\\n42\\\\t// boundary contract.\\\\n43\\\\texport async function assertModelCallContract(\\\\n44\\\\t  call: ModelCall,\\\\n45\\\\t  probe: ModelCallInput = probeInput(),\\\\n46\\\\t): Promise<void> {\\\\n47\\\\t  const result = await call(probe);\\\\n48\\\\t  assert.ok(\\\\n49\\\\t    typeof result === \\\\\\\"object\\\\\\\" && result !== null,\\\\n50\\\\t    \\\\\\\"ModelCall must resolve to a result object\\\\\\\",\\\\n51\\\\t  );\\\\n52\\\\t  if (result.ok === true) {\\\\n53\\\\t    assert.equal(typeof result.text, \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"success result must carry string text\\\\\\\");\\\\n54\\\\t  } else if (result.ok === false) {\\\\n55\\\\t    assert.ok(\\\\n56\\\\t      FAILURE_KINDS.has(result.kind),\\\\n57\\\\t      `failure kind \\\\\\\"${String(result.kind)}\\\\\\\" is not in the failure vocabulary`,\\\\n58\\\\t    );\\\\n59\\\\t    assert.equal(typeof result.message, \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"failure result must carry a string message\\\\\\\");\\\\n60\\\\t  } else {\\\\n61\\\\t    assert.fail(\\\\\\\"ModelCall result must discriminate on a boolean ok\\\\\\\");\\\\n62\\\\t  }\\\\n63\\\\t}\\\\n64\\\\t\\\\n65\\\\texport interface RoutingRunResult {\\\\n66\\\\t  sdk: Lhc;\\\\n67\\\\t  filePath: string;\\\\n68\\\\t  forms: DerivedForm[];\\\\n69\\\\t  log: ModelCallInput[];\\\\n70\\\\t}\\\\n71\\\\t\\\\n72\\\\tasync function seedAllSevenKinds(sdk: Lhc, store: TempStore): Promise<string> {\\\\n73\\\\t  const filePath = store.threadPath();\\\\n74\\\\t  const created = await sdk.threads.newThread({ filePath, registryPath: store.registryPath });\\\\n75\\\\t  assert.ok(created.ok, \\\\\\\"conformance fixture: thread creation failed\\\\\\\");\\\\n76\\\\t  // Turn 1 carries a tool run (smoothing, tool-call/result summaries,\\\\n77\\\\t  // rendering, projection); turn 2 exists so turn 1's chunk closes under the\\\\n78\\\\t  // tiny target policy and the two chunk summaries enqueue.\\\\n79\\\\t  const result = await sdk.intakeStream.messageEvents({ filePath }, [\\\\n80\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"user_prompt\\\\\\\", { payload: { text: \\\\\\\"please inspect the fixture file\\\\\\\" } }),\\\\n81\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"tool_call\\\\\\\", {\\\\n82\\\\t      payload: {\\\\n83\\\\t        toolCallId: \\\\\\\"call-seam-1\\\\\\\",\\\\n84\\\\t        toolName: \\\\\\\"read_file\\\\\\\",\\\\n85\\\\t        arguments: { path: \\\\\\\"fixture.txt\\\\\\\" },\\\\n86\\\\t      },\\\\n87\\\\t    }),\\\\n88\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"tool_result\\\\\\\", {\\\\n89\\\\t      payload: { toolCallId: \\\\\\\"call-seam-1\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\"contents of fixture.txt\\\\\\\", isError: false },\\\\n90\\\\t    }),\\\\n91\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"assistant_text\\\\\\\", { payload: { text: \\\\\\\"the fixture file holds fixture text\\\\\\\" } }),\\\\n92\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"turn_end\\\\\\\"),\\\\n93\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"user_prompt\\\\\\\", { payload: { text: \\\\\\\"thanks, now summarize it\\\\\\\" } }),\\\\n94\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"assistant_text\\\\\\\", { payload: { text: \\\\\\\"summarized\\\\\\\" } }),\\\\n95\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"turn_end\\\\\\\"),\\\\n96\\\\t  ]);\\\\n97\\\\t  assert.ok(result.ok, \\\\\\\"conformance fixture: intake batch failed\\\\\\\");\\\\n98\\\\t  return filePath;\\\\n99\\\\t}\\\\n100\\\\t\\\\n101\\\\t// TC-1.2's routing assertions, extracted so TC-4.3 runs them against the\\\\n102\\\\t// real host unchanged: construct an SDK on the inference path, drive all\\\\n103\\\\t// seven kinds through a real intake → drain, and assert (1) every kind lands\\\\n104\\\\t// ready, (2) every call that crossed the boundary carried exactly one\\\\n105\\\\t// kind's assigned provider/model routing keys, (3) each kind's lane was\\\\n106\\\\t// exercised, and (4) every messages value is single-turn shape — system and\\\\n107\\\\t// user roles only, string content (AC-1.2, AC-1.4).\\\\n108\\\\texport async function assertRoutingThroughSdk(\\\\n109\\\\t  call: ModelCall,\\\\n110\\\\t  assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>,\\\\n111\\\\t  store: TempStore,\\\\n112\\\\t): Promise<RoutingRunResult> {\\\\n113\\\\t  const log: ModelCallInput[] = [];\\\\n114\\\\t  const logged: ModelCall = (input) => {\\\\n115\\\\t    log.push(structuredClone(input));\\\\n116\\\\t    return call(input);\\\\n117\\\\t  };\\\\n118\\\\t  const sdk = createSdk({\\\\n119\\\\t    inference: { call: logged, assignments },\\\\n120\\\\t    mode: \\\\\\\"manual\\\\\\\",\\\\n121\\\\t    retry: { budget: 3, backoffBaseMs: 0, backoffCapMs: 0 },\\\\n122\\\\t    // Tiny target: each placed turn crosses it, so the prior turn's chunk\\\\n123\\\\t    // closes during the drain and the chunk-summary kinds run too.\\\\n124\\\\t    chunkPolicy: { targetProjectedTokens: 5, maxProjectedTokens: 4400 },\\\\n125\\\\t  });\\\\n126\\\\t  const filePath = await seedAllSevenKinds(sdk, store);\\\\n127\\\\t\\\\n128\\\\t  const drained = await sdk.work.drain({ filePath });\\\\n129\\\\t  assert.ok(drained.ok, \\\\\\\"conformance drain failed\\\\\\\");\\\\n130\\\\t  assert.equal(drained.value.stoppedBecause, \\\\\\\"empty\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"conformance drain left work behind\\\\\\\");\\\\n131\\\\t  assert.equal(drained.value.remaining, 0, \\\\\\\"conformance drain left items remaining\\\\\\\");\\\\n132\\\\t\\\\n133\\\\t  const forms = readDerivedForms(filePath);\\\\n134\\\\t  for (const kind of FORM_KINDS) {\\\\n135\\\\t    assert.ok(\\\\n136\\\\t      forms.some((form) => form.form === kind && form.state === \\\\\\\"ready\\\\\\\"),\\\\n137\\\\t      `expected at least one ready ${kind} form after the drain`,\\\\n138\\\\t    );\\\\n139\\\\t  }\\\\n140\\\\t\\\\n141\\\\t  assert.ok(log.length > 0, \\\\\\\"no calls crossed the ModelCall boundary\\\\\\\");\\\\n142\\\\t  for (const input of log) {\\\\n143\\\\t    const matched = FORM_KINDS.filter(\\\\n144\\\\t      (kind) =>\\\\n145\\\\t        assignments[kind].provider === input.provider && assignments[kind].model === input.model,\\\\n146\\\\t    );\\\\n147\\\\t    assert.ok(\\\\n148\\\\t      matched.length > 0,\\\\n149\\\\t      `call carried provider/model \\\\\\\"${input.provider}\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\"${input.model}\\\\\\\" matching no kind's assignment`,\\\\n150\\\\t    );\\\\n151\\\\t    assert.ok(input.messages.length > 0, \\\\\\\"a call crossed the boundary with no messages\\\\\\\");\\\\n152\\\\t    assert.ok(\\\\n153\\\\t      input.messages.some((message) => message.role === \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\"),\\\\n154\\\\t      \\\\\\\"single-turn shape requires a user message\\\\\\\",\\\\n155\\\\t    );\\\\n156\\\\t    for (const message of input.messages) {\\\\n157\\\\t      assert.ok(\\\\n158\\\\t        message.role === \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" || message.role === \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\n159\\\\t        `message role \\\\\\\"${String(message.role)}\\\\\\\" is outside the single-turn vocabulary`,\\\\n160\\\\t      );\\\\n161\\\\t      assert.equal(typeof message.content, \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"message content must be a string\\\\\\\");\\\\n162\\\\t    }\\\\n163\\\\t  }\\\\n164\\\\t  for (const kind of FORM_KINDS) {\\\\n165\\\\t    assert.ok(\\\\n166\\\\t      log.some(\\\\n167\\\\t        (input) =>\\\\n168\\\\t          input.provider === assignments[kind].provider && input.model === assignments[kind].model,\\\\n169\\\\t      ),\\\\n170\\\\t      `no boundary call carried ${kind}'s assigned provider/model lane`,\\\\n171\\\\t    );\\\\n172\\\\t  }\\\\n173\\\\t  return { sdk, filePath, forms, log };\\\\n174\\\\t}\\\\n175\\\\t\\\"}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"6edb0c40-880a-479b-89bf-c49b4ad66e88\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-06-12T23:21:14.393Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"file\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"// Parameterized seam-contract assertions (Epic 05 DD-13): the same helpers\\\\n// run against the scripted fakes (TC-1.2) and, unchanged, against the real\\\\n// OpenRouter host (TC-4.3) — shared assertion code, never duplicated tests.\\\\n// Framework-agnostic on purpose: plain node:assert throws surface in vitest\\\\n// and in any future runner identically.\\\\nimport assert from \\\\\\\"node:assert/strict\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { createSdk, type DerivedForm, type Lhc } from \\\\\\\"../../src/index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { FORM_KINDS, type FormKind } from \\\\\\\"../../src/shared/derivation.js\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport type {\\\\n  ModelAssignment,\\\\n  ModelCall,\\\\n  ModelCallInput,\\\\n} from \\\\\\\"../../src/inference/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { readDerivedForms } from \\\\\\\"./threads.js\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { validEvent, type TempStore } from \\\\\\\"./index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\nconst FAILURE_KINDS = new Set([\\\\n  \\\\\\\"rate_limit\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"network\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"invalid_request\\\\\\\",\\\\n]);\\\\n\\\\nexport function probeInput(overrides: Partial<ModelCallInput> = {}): ModelCallInput {\\\\n  return {\\\\n    provider: \\\\\\\"probe-provider\\\\\\\",\\\\n    model: \\\\\\\"probe-model\\\\\\\",\\\\n    messages: [\\\\n      { role: \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\"You answer with one word.\\\\\\\" },\\\\n      { role: \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\"Say ok.\\\\\\\" },\\\\n    ],\\\\n    ...overrides,\\\\n  };\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// AC-1.2's result contract on one probe call: a ModelCall resolves to either\\\\n// `{ ok: true, text }` or `{ ok: false, kind, message }` with `kind` from the\\\\n// failure vocabulary. Catches fixture (and real-host) drift from the\\\\n// boundary contract.\\\\nexport async function assertModelCallContract(\\\\n  call: ModelCall,\\\\n  probe: ModelCallInput = probeInput(),\\\\n): Promise<void> {\\\\n  const result = await call(probe);\\\\n  assert.ok(\\\\n    typeof result === \\\\\\\"object\\\\\\\" && result !== null,\\\\n    \\\\\\\"ModelCall must resolve to a result object\\\\\\\",\\\\n  );\\\\n  if (result.ok === true) {\\\\n    assert.equal(typeof result.text, \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"success result must carry string text\\\\\\\");\\\\n  } else if (result.ok === false) {\\\\n    assert.ok(\\\\n      FAILURE_KINDS.has(result.kind),\\\\n      `failure kind \\\\\\\"${String(result.kind)}\\\\\\\" is not in the failure vocabulary`,\\\\n    );\\\\n    assert.equal(typeof result.message, \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"failure result must carry a string message\\\\\\\");\\\\n  } else {\\\\n    assert.fail(\\\\\\\"ModelCall result must discriminate on a boolean ok\\\\\\\");\\\\n  }\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nexport interface RoutingRunResult {\\\\n  sdk: Lhc;\\\\n  filePath: string;\\\\n  forms: DerivedForm[];\\\\n  log: ModelCallInput[];\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nasync function seedAllSevenKinds(sdk: Lhc, store: TempStore): Promise<string> {\\\\n  const filePath = store.threadPath();\\\\n  const created = await sdk.threads.newThread({ filePath, registryPath: store.registryPath });\\\\n  assert.ok(created.ok, \\\\\\\"conformance fixture: thread creation failed\\\\\\\");\\\\n  // Turn 1 carries a tool run (smoothing, tool-call/result summaries,\\\\n  // rendering, projection); turn 2 exists so turn 1's chunk closes under the\\\\n  // tiny target policy and the two chunk summaries enqueue.\\\\n  const result = await sdk.intakeStream.messageEvents({ filePath }, [\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"user_prompt\\\\\\\", { payload: { text: \\\\\\\"please inspect the fixture file\\\\\\\" } }),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"tool_call\\\\\\\", {\\\\n      payload: {\\\\n        toolCallId: \\\\\\\"call-seam-1\\\\\\\",\\\\n        toolName: \\\\\\\"read_file\\\\\\\",\\\\n        arguments: { path: \\\\\\\"fixture.txt\\\\\\\" },\\\\n      },\\\\n    }),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"tool_result\\\\\\\", {\\\\n      payload: { toolCallId: \\\\\\\"call-seam-1\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\"contents of fixture.txt\\\\\\\", isError: false },\\\\n    }),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"assistant_text\\\\\\\", { payload: { text: \\\\\\\"the fixture file holds fixture text\\\\\\\" } }),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"turn_end\\\\\\\"),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"user_prompt\\\\\\\", { payload: { text: \\\\\\\"thanks, now summarize it\\\\\\\" } }),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"assistant_text\\\\\\\", { payload: { text: \\\\\\\"summarized\\\\\\\" } }),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"turn_end\\\\\\\"),\\\\n  ]);\\\\n  assert.ok(result.ok, \\\\\\\"conformance fixture: intake batch failed\\\\\\\");\\\\n  return filePath;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// TC-1.2's routing assertions, extracted so TC-4.3 runs them against the\\\\n// real host unchanged: construct an SDK on the inference path, drive all\\\\n// seven kinds through a real intake → drain, and assert (1) every kind lands\\\\n// ready, (2) every call that crossed the boundary carried exactly one\\\\n// kind's assigned provider/model routing keys, (3) each kind's lane was\\\\n// exercised, and (4) every messages value is single-turn shape — system and\\\\n// user roles only, string content (AC-1.2, AC-1.4).\\\\nexport async function assertRoutingThroughSdk(\\\\n  call: ModelCall,\\\\n  assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>,\\\\n  store: TempStore,\\\\n): Promise<RoutingRunResult> {\\\\n  const log: ModelCallInput[] = [];\\\\n  const logged: ModelCall = (input) => {\\\\n    log.push(structuredClone(input));\\\\n    return call(input);\\\\n  };\\\\n  const sdk = createSdk({\\\\n    inference: { call: logged, assignments },\\\\n    mode: \\\\\\\"manual\\\\\\\",\\\\n    retry: { budget: 3, backoffBaseMs: 0, backoffCapMs: 0 },\\\\n    // Tiny target: each placed turn crosses it, so the prior turn's chunk\\\\n    // closes during the drain and the chunk-summary kinds run too.\\\\n    chunkPolicy: { targetProjectedTokens: 5, maxProjectedTokens: 4400 },\\\\n  });\\\\n  const filePath = await seedAllSevenKinds(sdk, store);\\\\n\\\\n  const drained = await sdk.work.drain({ filePath });\\\\n  assert.ok(drained.ok, \\\\\\\"conformance drain failed\\\\\\\");\\\\n  assert.equal(drained.value.stoppedBecause, \\\\\\\"empty\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"conformance drain left work behind\\\\\\\");\\\\n  assert.equal(drained.value.remaining, 0, \\\\\\\"conformance drain left items remaining\\\\\\\");\\\\n\\\\n  const forms = readDerivedForms(filePath);\\\\n  for (const kind of FORM_KINDS) {\\\\n    assert.ok(\\\\n      forms.some((form) => form.form === kind && form.state === \\\\\\\"ready\\\\\\\"),\\\\n      `expected at least one ready ${kind} form after the drain`,\\\\n    );\\\\n  }\\\\n\\\\n  assert.ok(log.length > 0, \\\\\\\"no calls crossed the ModelCall boundary\\\\\\\");\\\\n  for (const input of log) {\\\\n    const matched = FORM_KINDS.filter(\\\\n      (kind) =>\\\\n        assignments[kind].provider === input.provider && assignments[kind].model === input.model,\\\\n    );\\\\n    assert.ok(\\\\n      matched.length > 0,\\\\n      `call carried provider/model \\\\\\\"${input.provider}\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\"${input.model}\\\\\\\" matching no kind's assignment`,\\\\n    );\\\\n    assert.ok(input.messages.length > 0, \\\\\\\"a call crossed the boundary with no messages\\\\\\\");\\\\n    assert.ok(\\\\n      input.messages.some((message) => message.role === \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\"),\\\\n      \\\\\\\"single-turn shape requires a user message\\\\\\\",\\\\n    );\\\\n    for (const message of input.messages) {\\\\n      assert.ok(\\\\n        message.role === \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" || message.role === \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\n        `message role \\\\\\\"${String(message.role)}\\\\\\\" is outside the single-turn vocabulary`,\\\\n      );\\\\n      assert.equal(typeof message.content, \\\\\\\"string\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"message content must be a string\\\\\\\");\\\\n    }\\\\n  }\\\\n  for (const kind of FORM_KINDS) {\\\\n    assert.ok(\\\\n      log.some(\\\\n        (input) =>\\\\n          input.provider === assignments[kind].provider && input.model === assignments[kind].model,\\\\n      ),\\\\n      `no boundary call carried ${kind}'s assigned provider/model lane`,\\\\n    );\\\\n  }\\\\n  return { sdk, filePath, forms, log };\\\\n}\\\\n\\\",\\\"numLines\\\":175,\\\"startLine\\\":1,\\\"totalLines\\\":175}}}\\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:131:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"toolu_01UemtKZB62B1j5jov2kTUJA\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"1\\\\t// The scripted-host builders (Epic 05 test plan §Fixture Contracts): fake\\\\n2\\\\t// ModelCall implementations every Flow 1–3 test uses. These are boundary\\\\n3\\\\t// doubles, not internal mocks — the ModelCall function is where LHC's code\\\\n4\\\\t// ends (tech design §Testing Strategy). All builders return\\\\n5\\\\t// contract-conformant shapes (AC-1.2); seam-conformance.ts asserts that of\\\\n6\\\\t// recordingCall in its own test so fixture drift is caught by the suite.\\\\n7\\\\timport { FORM_KINDS, type FormKind } from \\\\\\\"../../src/shared/derivation.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n8\\\\timport { DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES } from \\\\\\\"../../src/inference/prompts/index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n9\\\\timport type {\\\\n10\\\\t  ModelAssignment,\\\\n11\\\\t  ModelCall,\\\\n12\\\\t  ModelCallInput,\\\\n13\\\\t  ModelCallResult,\\\\n14\\\\t} from \\\\\\\"../../src/inference/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n15\\\\t\\\\n16\\\\texport { FORM_KINDS, type FormKind };\\\\n17\\\\t\\\\n18\\\\t// validAssignments gives every kind a distinct fake provider/model lane;\\\\n19\\\\t// recordingCall infers the kind back from the model string, so per-kind\\\\n20\\\\t// routing is observable from the log alone (TC-1.2).\\\\n21\\\\texport const FAKE_PROVIDER_PREFIX = \\\\\\\"prov-\\\\\\\";\\\\n22\\\\texport const FAKE_MODEL_PREFIX = \\\\\\\"model-\\\\\\\";\\\\n23\\\\t\\\\n24\\\\texport function validAssignments(\\\\n25\\\\t  overrides: Partial<Record<FormKind, Partial<ModelAssignment>>> = {},\\\\n26\\\\t): Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment> {\\\\n27\\\\t  const map = {} as Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>;\\\\n28\\\\t  for (const kind of FORM_KINDS) {\\\\n29\\\\t    map[kind] = {\\\\n30\\\\t      provider: `${FAKE_PROVIDER_PREFIX}${kind}`,\\\\n31\\\\t      model: `${FAKE_MODEL_PREFIX}${kind}`,\\\\n32\\\\t      prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES[kind],\\\\n33\\\\t      ...(overrides[kind] ?? {}),\\\\n34\\\\t    };\\\\n35\\\\t  }\\\\n36\\\\t  return map;\\\\n37\\\\t}\\\\n38\\\\t\\\\n39\\\\t// One distinct canned sentence per kind, so cross-kind bleed is visible in\\\\n40\\\\t// landed form content, never just in call counts.\\\\n41\\\\texport function cannedResponses(): Record<FormKind, string> {\\\\n42\\\\t  const map = {} as Record<FormKind, string>;\\\\n43\\\\t  for (const kind of FORM_KINDS) {\\\\n44\\\\t    map[kind] = `canned ${kind} text from the fake host`;\\\\n45\\\\t  }\\\\n46\\\\t  return map;\\\\n47\\\\t}\\\\n48\\\\t\\\\n49\\\\t// Resolves each call with its kind's canned text — the kind inferred from\\\\n50\\\\t// the model routing key validAssignments made unique per kind. Every input\\\\n51\\\\t// is cloned into `log` in call order. An unknown model is a test bug and\\\\n52\\\\t// throws loudly.\\\\n53\\\\texport function recordingCall(responses: Record<FormKind, string>): {\\\\n54\\\\t  call: ModelCall;\\\\n55\\\\t  log: ModelCallInput[];\\\\n56\\\\t} {\\\\n57\\\\t  const log: ModelCallInput[] = [];\\\\n58\\\\t  const known = new Set<string>(FORM_KINDS);\\\\n59\\\\t  const call: ModelCall = (input) => {\\\\n60\\\\t    log.push(structuredClone(input));\\\\n61\\\\t    const kind = input.model.startsWith(FAKE_MODEL_PREFIX)\\\\n62\\\\t      ? input.model.slice(FAKE_MODEL_PREFIX.length)\\\\n63\\\\t      : input.model;\\\\n64\\\\t    if (!known.has(kind)) {\\\\n65\\\\t      throw new Error(`recordingCall: no canned response for model \\\\\\\"${input.model}\\\\\\\"`);\\\\n66\\\\t    }\\\\n67\\\\t    return Promise.resolve({ ok: true, text: responses[kind as FormKind] });\\\\n68\\\\t  };\\\\n69\\\\t  return { call, log };\\\\n70\\\\t}\\\\n71\\\\t\\\\n72\\\\t// Returns script entries in order; a call past the end of the script is a\\\\n73\\\\t// test bug and throws loudly.\\\\n74\\\\texport function scriptedCall(script: ModelCallResult[]): ModelCall {\\\\n75\\\\t  let next = 0;\\\\n76\\\\t  return (input) => {\\\\n77\\\\t    const entry = script[next];\\\\n78\\\\t    next += 1;\\\\n79\\\\t    if (entry === undefined) {\\\\n80\\\\t      throw new Error(\\\\n81\\\\t        `scriptedCall: script exhausted after ${String(script.length)} calls (model \\\\\\\"${input.model}\\\\\\\")`,\\\\n82\\\\t      );\\\\n83\\\\t    }\\\\n84\\\\t    return Promise.resolve(entry);\\\\n85\\\\t  };\\\\n86\\\\t}\\\\n87\\\\t\\\\n88\\\\t// A host whose function throws — the AC-3.3 containment leg.\\\\n89\\\\texport function throwingCall(error: Error): ModelCall {\\\\n90\\\\t  return () => Promise.reject(error);\\\\n91\\\\t}\\\\n92\\\\t\\\\n93\\\\t// A host that never settles — the DD-6 timeout leg (tests pass a small\\\\n94\\\\t// timeoutMs).\\\\n95\\\\texport function hangingCall(): ModelCall {\\\\n96\\\\t  return () => new Promise<ModelCallResult>(() => {});\\\\n97\\\\t}\\\\n98\\\\t\\\"}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"a20442e6-c3cd-404f-b566-d8cac661c7bd\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-06-12T23:21:14.747Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"file\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/test/fixtures/model-call.ts\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"// The scripted-host builders (Epic 05 test plan §Fixture Contracts): fake\\\\n// ModelCall implementations every Flow 1–3 test uses. These are boundary\\\\n// doubles, not internal mocks — the ModelCall function is where LHC's code\\\\n// ends (tech design §Testing Strategy). All builders return\\\\n// contract-conformant shapes (AC-1.2); seam-conformance.ts asserts that of\\\\n// recordingCall in its own test so fixture drift is caught by the suite.\\\\nimport { FORM_KINDS, type FormKind } from \\\\\\\"../../src/shared/derivation.js\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES } from \\\\\\\"../../src/inference/prompts/index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport type {\\\\n  ModelAssignment,\\\\n  ModelCall,\\\\n  ModelCallInput,\\\\n  ModelCallResult,\\\\n} from \\\\\\\"../../src/inference/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\nexport { FORM_KINDS, type FormKind };\\\\n\\\\n// validAssignments gives every kind a distinct fake provider/model lane;\\\\n// recordingCall infers the kind back from the model string, so per-kind\\\\n// routing is observable from the log alone (TC-1.2).\\\\nexport const FAKE_PROVIDER_PREFIX = \\\\\\\"prov-\\\\\\\";\\\\nexport const FAKE_MODEL_PREFIX = \\\\\\\"model-\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\nexport function validAssignments(\\\\n  overrides: Partial<Record<FormKind, Partial<ModelAssignment>>> = {},\\\\n): Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment> {\\\\n  const map = {} as Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>;\\\\n  for (const kind of FORM_KINDS) {\\\\n    map[kind] = {\\\\n      provider: `${FAKE_PROVIDER_PREFIX}${kind}`,\\\\n      model: `${FAKE_MODEL_PREFIX}${kind}`,\\\\n      prompt: DEFAULT_PROMPT_NAMES[kind],\\\\n      ...(overrides[kind] ?? {}),\\\\n    };\\\\n  }\\\\n  return map;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// One distinct canned sentence per kind, so cross-kind bleed is visible in\\\\n// landed form content, never just in call counts.\\\\nexport function cannedResponses(): Record<FormKind, string> {\\\\n  const map = {} as Record<FormKind, string>;\\\\n  for (const kind of FORM_KINDS) {\\\\n    map[kind] = `canned ${kind} text from the fake host`;\\\\n  }\\\\n  return map;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// Resolves each call with its kind's canned text — the kind inferred from\\\\n// the model routing key validAssignments made unique per kind. Every input\\\\n// is cloned into `log` in call order. An unknown model is a test bug and\\\\n// throws loudly.\\\\nexport function recordingCall(responses: Record<FormKind, string>): {\\\\n  call: ModelCall;\\\\n  log: ModelCallInput[];\\\\n} {\\\\n  const log: ModelCallInput[] = [];\\\\n  const known = new Set<string>(FORM_KINDS);\\\\n  const call: ModelCall = (input) => {\\\\n    log.push(structuredClone(input));\\\\n    const kind = input.model.startsWith(FAKE_MODEL_PREFIX)\\\\n      ? input.model.slice(FAKE_MODEL_PREFIX.length)\\\\n      : input.model;\\\\n    if (!known.has(kind)) {\\\\n      throw new Error(`recordingCall: no canned response for model \\\\\\\"${input.model}\\\\\\\"`);\\\\n    }\\\\n    return Promise.resolve({ ok: true, text: responses[kind as FormKind] });\\\\n  };\\\\n  return { call, log };\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// Returns script entries in order; a call past the end of the script is a\\\\n// test bug and throws loudly.\\\\nexport function scriptedCall(script: ModelCallResult[]): ModelCall {\\\\n  let next = 0;\\\\n  return (input) => {\\\\n    const entry = script[next];\\\\n    next += 1;\\\\n    if (entry === undefined) {\\\\n      throw new Error(\\\\n        `scriptedCall: script exhausted after ${String(script.length)} calls (model \\\\\\\"${input.model}\\\\\\\")`,\\\\n      );\\\\n    }\\\\n    return Promise.resolve(entry);\\\\n  };\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// A host whose function throws — the AC-3.3 containment leg.\\\\nexport function throwingCall(error: Error): ModelCall {\\\\n  return () => Promise.reject(error);\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// A host that never settles — the DD-6 timeout leg (tests pass a small\\\\n// timeoutMs).\\\\nexport function hangingCall(): ModelCall {\\\\n  return () => new Promise<ModelCallResult>(() => {});\\\\n}\\\\n\\\",\\\"numLines\\\":98,\\\"startLine\\\":1,\\\"totalLines\\\":98}}}\\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:144:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"toolu_01WCNKXxjusCqhbhSCnv3ikX\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"1\\\\t// The Epic 04 lifecycle script (Story 4, Flow 5): every built v1 surface\\\\n2\\\\t// driven once, in PI-extension call order, against one real SDK\\\\n3\\\\t// configuration with the deterministic provider — zero network, zero real\\\\n4\\\\t// provider, no mocks anywhere. The script owns the sequence and the phase\\\\n5\\\\t// names (tech design Flow 5); assertions live in the driving tests, so the\\\\n6\\\\t// replay, teardown, and process legs all run THIS sequence rather than a\\\\n7\\\\t// re-described one (DD-8).\\\\n8\\\\t//\\\\n9\\\\t// Phases: create | intake | drain | status | compact1 | pull1 | inspect1 |\\\\n10\\\\t// mutate | rebuild | health2 | compact2 | pull2 | materialize.\\\\n11\\\\t//\\\\n12\\\\t// Mode is background — the production posture the PI extension will run —\\\\n13\\\\t// and the drain/rebuild phases settle through `drainSettled`, the real\\\\n14\\\\t// background awaitable. The post-mutation health snapshot (AC-5.2's\\\\n15\\\\t// \\\\\\\"cleared set pending\\\\\\\") is deterministic because every SDK operation here\\\\n16\\\\t// is synchronous SQLite chained through microtasks, while a background\\\\n17\\\\t// drain pass always starts on a macrotask (scheduler runLoop defers through\\\\n18\\\\t// setImmediate): the edit → delete → health reads inside the mutate phase\\\\n19\\\\t// run as one unbroken microtask cascade and complete before the poked drain\\\\n20\\\\t// can claim its first item. The rebuild phase then yields via drainSettled\\\\n21\\\\t// and the drain runs to empty.\\\\n22\\\\timport { join } from \\\\\\\"node:path\\\\\\\";\\\\n23\\\\timport {\\\\n24\\\\t  createDeterministicProvider,\\\\n25\\\\t  createSdk,\\\\n26\\\\t  type BatchResult,\\\\n27\\\\t  type CompactReceipt,\\\\n28\\\\t  type FormReportEntry,\\\\n29\\\\t  type HealthReport,\\\\n30\\\\t  type InspectOverview,\\\\n31\\\\t  type Lhc,\\\\n32\\\\t  type MessageEventInput,\\\\n33\\\\t  type MutationResult,\\\\n34\\\\t  type OpResult,\\\\n35\\\\t  type PullResult,\\\\n36\\\\t  type ViewContentsReport,\\\\n37\\\\t  type ViewStatus,\\\\n38\\\\t} from \\\\\\\"../../src/index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n39\\\\timport { validEvent, type TempStore } from \\\\\\\"./index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n40\\\\t\\\\n41\\\\t// ── the one SDK configuration (AC-5.1) ────────────────────────────\\\\n42\\\\t//\\\\n43\\\\t// Deterministic provider injected at construction (the only provider-arrival\\\\n44\\\\t// path since the CLI retired), background mode, zero-backoff retry so a\\\\n45\\\\t// deterministic run never arms a wake timer, the Epic 03 fixture's pinned\\\\n46\\\\t// chunk policy (12 fixed-shape turns cut into 4 chunks, c1–c3 closed), and\\\\n47\\\\t// a named \\\\\\\"lifecycle\\\\\\\" profile so compact runs profile-addressed (story\\\\n48\\\\t// scope: \\\\\\\"compact (profile)\\\\\\\") with all three lower bands non-empty on this\\\\n49\\\\t// thread (Epic 03 Story 2's reference gradient).\\\\n50\\\\texport const LIFECYCLE_PROFILE = {\\\\n51\\\\t  name: \\\\\\\"lifecycle\\\\\\\",\\\\n52\\\\t  lowerBound: 400,\\\\n53\\\\t  percentages: { full: 25, smooth: 16, detailed: 10, brief: 49 },\\\\n54\\\\t} as const;\\\\n55\\\\t\\\\n56\\\\texport function createLifecycleSdk(): Lhc {\\\\n57\\\\t  return createSdk({\\\\n58\\\\t    provider: createDeterministicProvider(),\\\\n59\\\\t    mode: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\",\\\\n60\\\\t    retry: { budget: 3, backoffBaseMs: 0, backoffCapMs: 0 },\\\\n61\\\\t    chunkPolicy: { targetProjectedTokens: 90, maxProjectedTokens: 4400 },\\\\n62\\\\t    view: {\\\\n63\\\\t      profiles: [{ ...LIFECYCLE_PROFILE, percentages: { ...LIFECYCLE_PROFILE.percentages } }],\\\\n64\\\\t      // Low threshold so the status checkpoint genuinely recommends the\\\\n65\\\\t      // compact the sequence performs next.\\\\n66\\\\t      compactThreshold: 300,\\\\n67\\\\t    },\\\\n68\\\\t  });\\\\n69\\\\t}\\\\n70\\\\t\\\\n71\\\\t// ── the recorded conversation ─────────────────────────────────────\\\\n72\\\\t//\\\\n73\\\\t// Same shape and text profile as the Epic 03 derived-thread fixture (12\\\\n74\\\\t// turns, tool-heavy middle 5–8, two tool runs each) so the pinned chunk\\\\n75\\\\t// policy cuts the same 4-chunk shape — but with explicit per-run\\\\n76\\\\t// idempotency keys so a replay on a fresh thread records byte-identical\\\\n77\\\\t// event payloads regardless of any process-global fixture counters.\\\\n78\\\\tconst TURN_COUNT = 12;\\\\n79\\\\tconst TOOL_HEAVY_TURNS = new Set([5, 6, 7, 8]);\\\\n80\\\\tconst TURNS_PER_BATCH = 3; // multi-turn batches (story scope)\\\\n81\\\\t\\\\n82\\\\t// Mutation targets: both in the post-compact tail (compact point at t9's\\\\n83\\\\t// start under the lifecycle profile), so the second compact's pull shows the\\\\n84\\\\t// edit verbatim and the deletion's absence — the tail is full fidelity.\\\\n85\\\\texport const EDIT_TARGET = { turnId: \\\\\\\"t12\\\\\\\", kind: \\\\\\\"user_prompt\\\\\\\" } as const;\\\\n86\\\\texport const EDITED_MESSAGE_TEXT =\\\\n87\\\\t  \\\\\\\"turn 12 revised: drop area 12 and re-check area 5 instead\\\\\\\";\\\\n88\\\\texport const DELETE_TARGET = { turnId: \\\\\\\"t10\\\\\\\", kind: \\\\\\\"assistant_text\\\\\\\" } as const;\\\\n89\\\\texport const DELETED_MESSAGE_TEXT = \\\\\\\"findings for area 10\\\\\\\";\\\\n90\\\\t\\\\n91\\\\tfunction turnEvents(turn: number): MessageEventInput[] {\\\\n92\\\\t  let seq = 0;\\\\n93\\\\t  const key = (): string => {\\\\n94\\\\t    seq += 1;\\\\n95\\\\t    return `lc-t${turn}-e${seq}`;\\\\n96\\\\t  };\\\\n97\\\\t  const events: MessageEventInput[] = [\\\\n98\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"user_prompt\\\\\\\", {\\\\n99\\\\t      idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n100\\\\t      payload: { text: `turn ${turn}: please investigate area ${turn}` },\\\\n101\\\\t    }),\\\\n102\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"assistant_thinking\\\\\\\", {\\\\n103\\\\t      idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n104\\\\t      payload: { text: `considering what area ${turn} contains` },\\\\n105\\\\t    }),\\\\n106\\\\t  ];\\\\n107\\\\t  if (TOOL_HEAVY_TURNS.has(turn)) {\\\\n108\\\\t    for (const run of [1, 2]) {\\\\n109\\\\t      const toolCallId = `call-lc-${turn}-${run}`;\\\\n110\\\\t      events.push(\\\\n111\\\\t        validEvent(\\\\\\\"tool_call\\\\\\\", {\\\\n112\\\\t          idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n113\\\\t          payload: {\\\\n114\\\\t            toolCallId,\\\\n115\\\\t            toolName: \\\\\\\"read_file\\\\\\\",\\\\n116\\\\t            arguments: { path: `area-${turn}/file-${run}.txt` },\\\\n117\\\\t          },\\\\n118\\\\t        }),\\\\n119\\\\t        validEvent(\\\\\\\"tool_result\\\\\\\", {\\\\n120\\\\t          idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n121\\\\t          payload: {\\\\n122\\\\t            toolCallId,\\\\n123\\\\t            content: `contents of area-${turn}/file-${run}.txt: detail ${turn}.${run} with enough text to summarize`,\\\\n124\\\\t            isError: false,\\\\n125\\\\t          },\\\\n126\\\\t        }),\\\\n127\\\\t      );\\\\n128\\\\t    }\\\\n129\\\\t  }\\\\n130\\\\t  events.push(\\\\n131\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"assistant_text\\\\\\\", {\\\\n132\\\\t      idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n133\\\\t      payload: { text: `findings for area ${turn}` },\\\\n134\\\\t    }),\\\\n135\\\\t    validEvent(\\\\\\\"turn_end\\\\\\\", { idempotencyKey: key() }),\\\\n136\\\\t  );\\\\n137\\\\t  return events;\\\\n138\\\\t}\\\\n139\\\\t\\\\n140\\\\tfunction intakeBatches(): MessageEventInput[][] {\\\\n141\\\\t  const batches: MessageEventInput[][] = [];\\\\n142\\\\t  for (let first = 1; first <= TURN_COUNT; first += TURNS_PER_BATCH) {\\\\n143\\\\t    const batch: MessageEventInput[] = [];\\\\n144\\\\t    for (let turn = first; turn < first + TURNS_PER_BATCH && turn <= TURN_COUNT; turn += 1) {\\\\n145\\\\t      batch.push(...turnEvents(turn));\\\\n146\\\\t    }\\\\n147\\\\t    batches.push(batch);\\\\n148\\\\t  }\\\\n149\\\\t  return batches;\\\\n150\\\\t}\\\\n151\\\\t\\\\n152\\\\t// ── run shape ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n153\\\\t\\\\n154\\\\texport interface LifecyclePhases {\\\\n155\\\\t  create: OpResult<{ threadId: string; filePath: string }>;\\\\n156\\\\t  intake: Array<OpResult<BatchResult>>;\\\\n157\\\\t  drain: { settled: true };\\\\n158\\\\t  status: OpResult<ViewStatus>;\\\\n159\\\\t  compact1: OpResult<CompactReceipt>;\\\\n160\\\\t  pull1: OpResult<PullResult>;\\\\n161\\\\t  inspect1: {\\\\n162\\\\t    overview: OpResult<InspectOverview>;\\\\n163\\\\t    view: OpResult<ViewContentsReport>;\\\\n164\\\\t    health: OpResult<HealthReport>;\\\\n165\\\\t  };\\\\n166\\\\t  mutate: {\\\\n167\\\\t    editedMessageId: string;\\\\n168\\\\t    deletedMessageId: string;\\\\n169\\\\t    edit: OpResult<MutationResult>;\\\\n170\\\\t    delete: OpResult<MutationResult>;\\\\n171\\\\t    // Captured inside the mutate phase, before the background drain's first\\\\n172\\\\t    // macrotask: the cleared-set-pending evidence (AC-5.2).\\\\n173\\\\t    healthAfterMutate: OpResult<HealthReport>;\\\\n174\\\\t    messagesNotReady: OpResult<FormReportEntry[]>;\\\\n175\\\\t    turnsNotReady: OpResult<FormReportEntry[]>;\\\\n176\\\\t  };\\\\n177\\\\t  rebuild: { settled: true };\\\\n178\\\\t  health2: OpResult<HealthReport>;\\\\n179\\\\t  compact2: OpResult<CompactReceipt>;\\\\n180\\\\t  pull2: OpResult<PullResult>;\\\\n181\\\\t  materialize: OpResult<{ writtenPath: string }>;\\\\n182\\\\t}\\\\n183\\\\t\\\\n184\\\\texport type LifecycleCheckpoint = \\\\\\\"inspect1\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"health2\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"materialize\\\\\\\";\\\\n185\\\\t\\\\n186\\\\texport interface LifecycleOptions {\\\\n187\\\\t  // Distinguishes the thread file and materialize target so replay and\\\\n188\\\\t  // teardown legs run side by side in one store (same dir ⇒ same header cwd).\\\\n189\\\\t  name?: string;\\\\n190\\\\t  // TC-5.3: a fresh createSdk between the intake / compact / mutation /\\\\n191\\\\t  // render phase groups — every group boundary is a quiescent point (no\\\\n192\\\\t  // queued work, no running drain), so the abandoned instance can never act\\\\n193\\\\t  // after its group ends.\\\\n194\\\\t  freshSdkBetweenGroups?: boolean;\\\\n195\\\\t  // TC-5.4: spawned-CLI parity probes at the three read checkpoints.\\\\n196\\\\t  onCheckpoint?: (\\\\n197\\\\t    checkpoint: LifecycleCheckpoint,\\\\n198\\\\t    ctx: { sdk: Lhc; filePath: string },\\\\n199\\\\t  ) => Promise<void>;\\\\n200\\\\t}\\\\n201\\\\t\\\\n202\\\\texport interface LifecycleRun {\\\\n203\\\\t  filePath: string;\\\\n204\\\\t  outPath: string;\\\\n205\\\\t  threadId: string;\\\\n206\\\\t  phases: LifecyclePhases;\\\\n207\\\\t}\\\\n208\\\\t\\\\n209\\\\tfunction expectOk<T>(result: OpResult<T>, phase: string): T {\\\\n210\\\\t  if (!result.ok) {\\\\n211\\\\t    throw new Error(`lifecycle ${phase} failed: ${result.error.code} — ${result.error.reason}`);\\\\n212\\\\t  }\\\\n213\\\\t  return result.value;\\\\n214\\\\t}\\\\n215\\\\t\\\\n216\\\\t// ── the sequence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n217\\\\t\\\\n218\\\\texport async function runLifecycle(\\\\n219\\\\t  store: TempStore,\\\\n220\\\\t  opts: LifecycleOptions = {},\\\\n221\\\\t): Promise<LifecycleRun> {\\\\n222\\\\t  const name = opts.name ?? \\\\\\\"lifecycle\\\\\\\";\\\\n223\\\\t  const filePath = store.threadPath(name);\\\\n224\\\\t  const outPath = join(store.dir, `${name}-session.jsonl`);\\\\n225\\\\t  const ref = { filePath };\\\\n226\\\\t\\\\n227\\\\t  let sdk = createLifecycleSdk();\\\\n228\\\\t  const nextGroup = (): void => {\\\\n229\\\\t    // The fresh instance is the same configuration assembled again — the\\\\n230\\\\t    // continuity proof is that no phase depends on the prior instance's\\\\n231\\\\t    // memory, only on the thread file.\\\\n232\\\\t    if (opts.freshSdkBetweenGroups) sdk = createLifecycleSdk();\\\\n233\\\\t  };\\\\n234\\\\t  const checkpoint = async (cp: LifecycleCheckpoint): Promise<void> => {\\\\n235\\\\t    if (opts.onCheckpoint !== undefined) await opts.onCheckpoint(cp, { sdk, filePath });\\\\n236\\\\t  };\\\\n237\\\\t\\\\n238\\\\t  // ── group 1: create → intake → drain → status ──\\\\n239\\\\t  const create = await sdk.threads.newThread({\\\\n240\\\\t    filePath,\\\\n241\\\\t    registryPath: store.registryPath,\\\\n242\\\\t  });\\\\n243\\\\t  const threadId = expectOk(create, \\\\\\\"create\\\\\\\").threadId;\\\\n244\\\\t\\\\n245\\\\t  const intake: Array<OpResult<BatchResult>> = [];\\\\n246\\\\t  for (const batch of intakeBatches()) {\\\\n247\\\\t    const sent = await sdk.intakeStream.messageEvents(ref, batch);\\\\n248\\\\t    intake.push(sent);\\\\n249\\\\t    expectOk(sent, \\\\\\\"intake\\\\\\\");\\\\n250\\\\t  }\\\\n251\\\\t\\\\n252\\\\t  // Background drain settles (story scope): the intake pokes scheduled the\\\\n253\\\\t  // work; this is the await that lets the scheduler run it to empty.\\\\n254\\\\t  await sdk.drainSettled(ref);\\\\n255\\\\t  const drain = { settled: true } as const;\\\\n256\\\\t\\\\n257\\\\t  const status = await sdk.threadView.status(ref);\\\\n258\\\\t  expectOk(status, \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\");\\\\n259\\\\t\\\\n260\\\\t  // ── group 2: compact1 → pull1 → inspect1 ──\\\\n261\\\\t  nextGroup();\\\\n262\\\\t  const compact1 = await sdk.threadView.compact(ref, { profile: LIFECYCLE_PROFILE.name });\\\\n263\\\\t  expectOk(compact1, \\\\\\\"compact1\\\\\\\");\\\\n264\\\\t  const pull1 = await sdk.threadView.pull(ref);\\\\n265\\\\t  expectOk(pull1, \\\\\\\"pull1\\\\\\\");\\\\n266\\\\t  const inspect1 = {\\\\n267\\\\t    overview: await sdk.inspect.overview(ref),\\\\n268\\\\t    view: await sdk.inspect.view(ref),\\\\n269\\\\t    health: await sdk.inspect.health(ref),\\\\n270\\\\t  };\\\\n271\\\\t  expectOk(inspect1.overview, \\\\\\\"inspect1.overview\\\\\\\");\\\\n272\\\\t  expectOk(inspect1.view, \\\\\\\"inspect1.view\\\\\\\");\\\\n273\\\\t  expectOk(inspect1.health, \\\\\\\"inspect1.health\\\\\\\");\\\\n274\\\\t  await checkpoint(\\\\\\\"inspect1\\\\\\\");\\\\n275\\\\t\\\\n276\\\\t  // ── group 3: mutate → rebuild → health2 ──\\\\n277\\\\t  nextGroup();\\\\n278\\\\t  const listed = expectOk(await sdk.messages.listMessages(ref), \\\\\\\"mutate.list\\\\\\\");\\\\n279\\\\t  const editTarget = listed.find(\\\\n280\\\\t    (record) => record.kind === EDIT_TARGET.kind && record.turnId === EDIT_TARGET.turnId,\\\\n281\\\\t  );\\\\n282\\\\t  const deleteTarget = listed.find(\\\\n283\\\\t    (record) => record.kind === DELETE_TARGET.kind && record.turnId === DELETE_TARGET.turnId,\\\\n284\\\\t  );\\\\n285\\\\t  if (editTarget === undefined || deleteTarget === undefined) {\\\\n286\\\\t    throw new Error(\\\\\\\"lifecycle invariant: edit/delete targets not found in the record\\\\\\\");\\\\n287\\\\t  }\\\\n288\\\\t  // Edit, delete, and the pending-state reads run in one microtask cascade\\\\n289\\\\t  // (see module header): the snapshot is taken before the poked background\\\\n290\\\\t  // drain starts its first pass.\\\\n291\\\\t  const edit = await sdk.messages.edit(ref, {\\\\n292\\\\t    messageId: editTarget.messageId,\\\\n293\\\\t    content: EDITED_MESSAGE_TEXT,\\\\n294\\\\t  });\\\\n295\\\\t  expectOk(edit, \\\\\\\"mutate.edit\\\\\\\");\\\\n296\\\\t  const deleted = await sdk.messages.deleteMessage(ref, { messageId: deleteTarget.messageId });\\\\n297\\\\t  expectOk(deleted, \\\\\\\"mutate.delete\\\\\\\");\\\\n298\\\\t  const mutate: LifecyclePhases[\\\\\\\"mutate\\\\\\\"] = {\\\\n299\\\\t    editedMessageId: editTarget.messageId,\\\\n300\\\\t    deletedMessageId: deleteTarget.messageId,\\\\n301\\\\t    edit,\\\\n302\\\\t    delete: deleted,\\\\n303\\\\t    healthAfterMutate: await sdk.inspect.health(ref),\\\\n304\\\\t    messagesNotReady: await sdk.messages.report(ref, { notReady: true }),\\\\n305\\\\t    turnsNotReady: await sdk.turns.report(ref, { notReady: true }),\\\\n306\\\\t  };\\\\n307\\\\t  expectOk(mutate.healthAfterMutate, \\\\\\\"mutate.health\\\\\\\");\\\\n308\\\\t  expectOk(mutate.messagesNotReady, \\\\\\\"mutate.messagesNotReady\\\\\\\");\\\\n309\\\\t  expectOk(mutate.turnsNotReady, \\\\\\\"mutate.turnsNotReady\\\\\\\");\\\\n310\\\\t\\\\n311\\\\t  // Rebuild drains: the mutations' pokes already scheduled the cascade\\\\n312\\\\t  // rebuilds on THIS instance's scheduler; settle is the production await.\\\\n313\\\\t  await sdk.drainSettled(ref);\\\\n314\\\\t  const rebuild = { settled: true } as const;\\\\n315\\\\t\\\\n316\\\\t  const health2 = await sdk.inspect.health(ref);\\\\n317\\\\t  expectOk(health2, \\\\\\\"health2\\\\\\\");\\\\n318\\\\t  await checkpoint(\\\\\\\"health2\\\\\\\");\\\\n319\\\\t\\\\n320\\\\t  // ── group 4: compact2 → pull2 → materialize ──\\\\n321\\\\t  nextGroup();\\\\n322\\\\t  const compact2 = await sdk.threadView.compact(ref, { profile: LIFECYCLE_PROFILE.name });\\\\n323\\\\t  expectOk(compact2, \\\\\\\"compact2\\\\\\\");\\\\n324\\\\t  const pull2 = await sdk.threadView.pull(ref);\\\\n325\\\\t  expectOk(pull2, \\\\\\\"pull2\\\\\\\");\\\\n326\\\\t  const materialize = await sdk.threadView.materialize(ref, { path: outPath });\\\\n327\\\\t  expectOk(materialize, \\\\\\\"materialize\\\\\\\");\\\\n328\\\\t  await checkpoint(\\\\\\\"materialize\\\\\\\");\\\\n329\\\\t\\\\n330\\\\t  return {\\\\n331\\\\t    filePath,\\\\n332\\\\t    outPath,\\\\n333\\\\t    threadId,\\\\n334\\\\t    phases: {\\\\n335\\\\t      create,\\\\n336\\\\t      intake,\\\\n337\\\\t      drain,\\\\n338\\\\t      status,\\\\n339\\\\t      compact1,\\\\n340\\\\t      pull1,\\\\n341\\\\t      inspect1,\\\\n342\\\\t      mutate,\\\\n343\\\\t      rebuild,\\\\n344\\\\t      health2,\\\\n345\\\\t      compact2,\\\\n346\\\\t      pull2,\\\\n347\\\\t      materialize,\\\\n348\\\\t    },\\\\n349\\\\t  };\\\\n350\\\\t}\\\\n351\\\\t\\\"}]},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"29748b91-283e-4097-bcb3-33b0be9e7cd0\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-06-12T23:21:18.758Z\\\",\\\"tool_use_result\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"file\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/test/fixtures/lifecycle.ts\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"// The Epic 04 lifecycle script (Story 4, Flow 5): every built v1 surface\\\\n// driven once, in PI-extension call order, against one real SDK\\\\n// configuration with the deterministic provider — zero network, zero real\\\\n// provider, no mocks anywhere. The script owns the sequence and the phase\\\\n// names (tech design Flow 5); assertions live in the driving tests, so the\\\\n// replay, teardown, and process legs all run THIS sequence rather than a\\\\n// re-described one (DD-8).\\\\n//\\\\n// Phases: create | intake | drain | status | compact1 | pull1 | inspect1 |\\\\n// mutate | rebuild | health2 | compact2 | pull2 | materialize.\\\\n//\\\\n// Mode is background — the production posture the PI extension will run —\\\\n// and the drain/rebuild phases settle through `drainSettled`, the real\\\\n// background awaitable. The post-mutation health snapshot (AC-5.2's\\\\n// \\\\\\\"cleared set pending\\\\\\\") is deterministic because every SDK operation here\\\\n// is synchronous SQLite chained through microtasks, while a background\\\\n// drain pass always starts on a macrotask (scheduler runLoop defers through\\\\n// setImmediate): the edit → delete → health reads inside the mutate phase\\\\n// run as one unbroken microtask cascade and complete before the poked drain\\\\n// can claim its first item. The rebuild phase then yields via drainSettled\\\\n// and the drain runs to empty.\\\\nimport { join } from \\\\\\\"node:path\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport {\\\\n  createDeterministicProvider,\\\\n  createSdk,\\\\n  type BatchResult,\\\\n  type CompactReceipt,\\\\n  type FormReportEntry,\\\\n  type HealthReport,\\\\n  type InspectOverview,\\\\n  type Lhc,\\\\n  type MessageEventInput,\\\\n  type MutationResult,\\\\n  type OpResult,\\\\n  type PullResult,\\\\n  type ViewContentsReport,\\\\n  type ViewStatus,\\\\n} from \\\\\\\"../../src/index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\nimport { validEvent, type TempStore } from \\\\\\\"./index.js\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\n// ── the one SDK configuration (AC-5.1) ────────────────────────────\\\\n//\\\\n// Deterministic provider injected at construction (the only provider-arrival\\\\n// path since the CLI retired), background mode, zero-backoff retry so a\\\\n// deterministic run never arms a wake timer, the Epic 03 fixture's pinned\\\\n// chunk policy (12 fixed-shape turns cut into 4 chunks, c1–c3 closed), and\\\\n// a named \\\\\\\"lifecycle\\\\\\\" profile so compact runs profile-addressed (story\\\\n// scope: \\\\\\\"compact (profile)\\\\\\\") with all three lower bands non-empty on this\\\\n// thread (Epic 03 Story 2's reference gradient).\\\\nexport const LIFECYCLE_PROFILE = {\\\\n  name: \\\\\\\"lifecycle\\\\\\\",\\\\n  lowerBound: 400,\\\\n  percentages: { full: 25, smooth: 16, detailed: 10, brief: 49 },\\\\n} as const;\\\\n\\\\nexport function createLifecycleSdk(): Lhc {\\\\n  return createSdk({\\\\n    provider: createDeterministicProvider(),\\\\n    mode: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\",\\\\n    retry: { budget: 3, backoffBaseMs: 0, backoffCapMs: 0 },\\\\n    chunkPolicy: { targetProjectedTokens: 90, maxProjectedTokens: 4400 },\\\\n    view: {\\\\n      profiles: [{ ...LIFECYCLE_PROFILE, percentages: { ...LIFECYCLE_PROFILE.percentages } }],\\\\n      // Low threshold so the status checkpoint genuinely recommends the\\\\n      // compact the sequence performs next.\\\\n      compactThreshold: 300,\\\\n    },\\\\n  });\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// ── the recorded conversation ─────────────────────────────────────\\\\n//\\\\n// Same shape and text profile as the Epic 03 derived-thread fixture (12\\\\n// turns, tool-heavy middle 5–8, two tool runs each) so the pinned chunk\\\\n// policy cuts the same 4-chunk shape — but with explicit per-run\\\\n// idempotency keys so a replay on a fresh thread records byte-identical\\\\n// event payloads regardless of any process-global fixture counters.\\\\nconst TURN_COUNT = 12;\\\\nconst TOOL_HEAVY_TURNS = new Set([5, 6, 7, 8]);\\\\nconst TURNS_PER_BATCH = 3; // multi-turn batches (story scope)\\\\n\\\\n// Mutation targets: both in the post-compact tail (compact point at t9's\\\\n// start under the lifecycle profile), so the second compact's pull shows the\\\\n// edit verbatim and the deletion's absence — the tail is full fidelity.\\\\nexport const EDIT_TARGET = { turnId: \\\\\\\"t12\\\\\\\", kind: \\\\\\\"user_prompt\\\\\\\" } as const;\\\\nexport const EDITED_MESSAGE_TEXT =\\\\n  \\\\\\\"turn 12 revised: drop area 12 and re-check area 5 instead\\\\\\\";\\\\nexport const DELETE_TARGET = { turnId: \\\\\\\"t10\\\\\\\", kind: \\\\\\\"assistant_text\\\\\\\" } as const;\\\\nexport const DELETED_MESSAGE_TEXT = \\\\\\\"findings for area 10\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\nfunction turnEvents(turn: number): MessageEventInput[] {\\\\n  let seq = 0;\\\\n  const key = (): string => {\\\\n    seq += 1;\\\\n    return `lc-t${turn}-e${seq}`;\\\\n  };\\\\n  const events: MessageEventInput[] = [\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"user_prompt\\\\\\\", {\\\\n      idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n      payload: { text: `turn ${turn}: please investigate area ${turn}` },\\\\n    }),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"assistant_thinking\\\\\\\", {\\\\n      idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n      payload: { text: `considering what area ${turn} contains` },\\\\n    }),\\\\n  ];\\\\n  if (TOOL_HEAVY_TURNS.has(turn)) {\\\\n    for (const run of [1, 2]) {\\\\n      const toolCallId = `call-lc-${turn}-${run}`;\\\\n      events.push(\\\\n        validEvent(\\\\\\\"tool_call\\\\\\\", {\\\\n          idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n          payload: {\\\\n            toolCallId,\\\\n            toolName: \\\\\\\"read_file\\\\\\\",\\\\n            arguments: { path: `area-${turn}/file-${run}.txt` },\\\\n          },\\\\n        }),\\\\n        validEvent(\\\\\\\"tool_result\\\\\\\", {\\\\n          idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n          payload: {\\\\n            toolCallId,\\\\n            content: `contents of area-${turn}/file-${run}.txt: detail ${turn}.${run} with enough text to summarize`,\\\\n            isError: false,\\\\n          },\\\\n        }),\\\\n      );\\\\n    }\\\\n  }\\\\n  events.push(\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"assistant_text\\\\\\\", {\\\\n      idempotencyKey: key(),\\\\n      payload: { text: `findings for area ${turn}` },\\\\n    }),\\\\n    validEvent(\\\\\\\"turn_end\\\\\\\", { idempotencyKey: key() }),\\\\n  );\\\\n  return events;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nfunction intakeBatches(): MessageEventInput[][] {\\\\n  const batches: MessageEventInput[][] = [];\\\\n  for (let first = 1; first <= TURN_COUNT; first += TURNS_PER_BATCH) {\\\\n    const batch: MessageEventInput[] = [];\\\\n    for (let turn = first; turn < first + TURNS_PER_BATCH && turn <= TURN_COUNT; turn += 1) {\\\\n      batch.push(...turnEvents(turn));\\\\n    }\\\\n    batches.push(batch);\\\\n  }\\\\n  return batches;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// ── run shape ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n\\\\nexport interface LifecyclePhases {\\\\n  create: OpResult<{ threadId: string; filePath: string }>;\\\\n  intake: Array<OpResult<BatchResult>>;\\\\n  drain: { settled: true };\\\\n  status: OpResult<ViewStatus>;\\\\n  compact1: OpResult<CompactReceipt>;\\\\n  pull1: OpResult<PullResult>;\\\\n  inspect1: {\\\\n    overview: OpResult<InspectOverview>;\\\\n    view: OpResult<ViewContentsReport>;\\\\n    health: OpResult<HealthReport>;\\\\n  };\\\\n  mutate: {\\\\n    editedMessageId: string;\\\\n    deletedMessageId: string;\\\\n    edit: OpResult<MutationResult>;\\\\n    delete: OpResult<MutationResult>;\\\\n    // Captured inside the mutate phase, before the background drain's first\\\\n    // macrotask: the cleared-set-pending evidence (AC-5.2).\\\\n    healthAfterMutate: OpResult<HealthReport>;\\\\n    messagesNotReady: OpResult<FormReportEntry[]>;\\\\n    turnsNotReady: OpResult<FormReportEntry[]>;\\\\n  };\\\\n  rebuild: { settled: true };\\\\n  health2: OpResult<HealthReport>;\\\\n  compact2: OpResult<CompactReceipt>;\\\\n  pull2: OpResult<PullResult>;\\\\n  materialize: OpResult<{ writtenPath: string }>;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nexport type LifecycleCheckpoint = \\\\\\\"inspect1\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"health2\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\"materialize\\\\\\\";\\\\n\\\\nexport interface LifecycleOptions {\\\\n  // Distinguishes the thread file and materialize target so replay and\\\\n  // teardown legs run side by side in one store (same dir ⇒ same header cwd).\\\\n  name?: string;\\\\n  // TC-5.3: a fresh createSdk between the intake / compact / mutation /\\\\n  // render phase groups — every group boundary is a quiescent point (no\\\\n  // queued work, no running drain), so the abandoned instance can never act\\\\n  // after its group ends.\\\\n  freshSdkBetweenGroups?: boolean;\\\\n  // TC-5.4: spawned-CLI parity probes at the three read checkpoints.\\\\n  onCheckpoint?: (\\\\n    checkpoint: LifecycleCheckpoint,\\\\n    ctx: { sdk: Lhc; filePath: string },\\\\n  ) => Promise<void>;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nexport interface LifecycleRun {\\\\n  filePath: string;\\\\n  outPath: string;\\\\n  threadId: string;\\\\n  phases: LifecyclePhases;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\nfunction expectOk<T>(result: OpResult<T>, phase: string): T {\\\\n  if (!result.ok) {\\\\n    throw new Error(`lifecycle ${phase} failed: ${result.error.code} — ${result.error.reason}`);\\\\n  }\\\\n  return result.value;\\\\n}\\\\n\\\\n// ── the sequence ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\n\\\\nexport async function runLifecycle(\\\\n  store: TempStore,\\\\n  opts: LifecycleOptions = {},\\\\n): Promise<LifecycleRun> {\\\\n  const name = opts.name ?? \\\\\\\"lifecycle\\\\\\\";\\\\n  const filePath = store.threadPath(name);\\\\n  const outPath = join(store.dir, `${name}-session.jsonl`);\\\\n  const ref = { filePath };\\\\n\\\\n  let sdk = createLifecycleSdk();\\\\n  const nextGroup = (): void => {\\\\n    // The fresh instance is the same configuration assembled again — the\\\\n    // continuity proof is that no phase depends on the prior instance's\\\\n    // memory, only on the thread file.\\\\n    if (opts.freshSdkBetweenGroups) sdk = createLifecycleSdk();\\\\n  };\\\\n  const checkpoint = async (cp: LifecycleCheckpoint): Promise<void> => {\\\\n    if (opts.onCheckpoint !== undefined) await opts.onCheckpoint(cp, { sdk, filePath });\\\\n  };\\\\n\\\\n  // ── group 1: create → intake → drain → status ──\\\\n  const create = await sdk.threads.newThread({\\\\n    filePath,\\\\n    registryPath: store.registryPath,\\\\n  });\\\\n  const threadId = expectOk(create, \\\\\\\"create\\\\\\\").threadId;\\\\n\\\\n  const intake: Array<OpResult<BatchResult>> = [];\\\\n  for (const batch of intakeBatches()) {\\\\n    const sent = await sdk.intakeStream.messageEvents(ref, batch);\\\\n    intake.push(sent);\\\\n    expectOk(sent, \\\\\\\"intake\\\\\\\");\\\\n  }\\\\n\\\\n  // Background drain settles (story scope): the intake pokes scheduled the\\\\n  // work; this is the await that lets the scheduler run it to empty.\\\\n  await sdk.drainSettled(ref);\\\\n  const drain = { settled: true } as const;\\\\n\\\\n  const status = await sdk.threadView.status(ref);\\\\n  expectOk(status, \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\");\\\\n\\\\n  // ── group 2: compact1 → pull1 → inspect1 ──\\\\n  nextGroup();\\\\n  const compact1 = await sdk.threadView.compact(ref, { profile: LIFECYCLE_PROFILE.name });\\\\n  expectOk(compact1, \\\\\\\"compact1\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const pull1 = await sdk.threadView.pull(ref);\\\\n  expectOk(pull1, \\\\\\\"pull1\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const inspect1 = {\\\\n    overview: await sdk.inspect.overview(ref),\\\\n    view: await sdk.inspect.view(ref),\\\\n    health: await sdk.inspect.health(ref),\\\\n  };\\\\n  expectOk(inspect1.overview, \\\\\\\"inspect1.overview\\\\\\\");\\\\n  expectOk(inspect1.view, \\\\\\\"inspect1.view\\\\\\\");\\\\n  expectOk(inspect1.health, \\\\\\\"inspect1.health\\\\\\\");\\\\n  await checkpoint(\\\\\\\"inspect1\\\\\\\");\\\\n\\\\n  // ── group 3: mutate → rebuild → health2 ──\\\\n  nextGroup();\\\\n  const listed = expectOk(await sdk.messages.listMessages(ref), \\\\\\\"mutate.list\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const editTarget = listed.find(\\\\n    (record) => record.kind === EDIT_TARGET.kind && record.turnId === EDIT_TARGET.turnId,\\\\n  );\\\\n  const deleteTarget = listed.find(\\\\n    (record) => record.kind === DELETE_TARGET.kind && record.turnId === DELETE_TARGET.turnId,\\\\n  );\\\\n  if (editTarget === undefined || deleteTarget === undefined) {\\\\n    throw new Error(\\\\\\\"lifecycle invariant: edit/delete targets not found in the record\\\\\\\");\\\\n  }\\\\n  // Edit, delete, and the pending-state reads run in one microtask cascade\\\\n  // (see module header): the snapshot is taken before the poked background\\\\n  // drain starts its first pass.\\\\n  const edit = await sdk.messages.edit(ref, {\\\\n    messageId: editTarget.messageId,\\\\n    content: EDITED_MESSAGE_TEXT,\\\\n  });\\\\n  expectOk(edit, \\\\\\\"mutate.edit\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const deleted = await sdk.messages.deleteMessage(ref, { messageId: deleteTarget.messageId });\\\\n  expectOk(deleted, \\\\\\\"mutate.delete\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const mutate: LifecyclePhases[\\\\\\\"mutate\\\\\\\"] = {\\\\n    editedMessageId: editTarget.messageId,\\\\n    deletedMessageId: deleteTarget.messageId,\\\\n    edit,\\\\n    delete: deleted,\\\\n    healthAfterMutate: await sdk.inspect.health(ref),\\\\n    messagesNotReady: await sdk.messages.report(ref, { notReady: true }),\\\\n    turnsNotReady: await sdk.turns.report(ref, { notReady: true }),\\\\n  };\\\\n  expectOk(mutate.healthAfterMutate, \\\\\\\"mutate.health\\\\\\\");\\\\n  expectOk(mutate.messagesNotReady, \\\\\\\"mutate.messagesNotReady\\\\\\\");\\\\n  expectOk(mutate.turnsNotReady, \\\\\\\"mutate.turnsNotReady\\\\\\\");\\\\n\\\\n  // Rebuild drains: the mutations' pokes already scheduled the cascade\\\\n  // rebuilds on THIS instance's scheduler; settle is the production await.\\\\n  await sdk.drainSettled(ref);\\\\n  const rebuild = { settled: true } as const;\\\\n\\\\n  const health2 = await sdk.inspect.health(ref);\\\\n  expectOk(health2, \\\\\\\"health2\\\\\\\");\\\\n  await checkpoint(\\\\\\\"health2\\\\\\\");\\\\n\\\\n  // ── group 4: compact2 → pull2 → materialize ──\\\\n  nextGroup();\\\\n  const compact2 = await sdk.threadView.compact(ref, { profile: LIFECYCLE_PROFILE.name });\\\\n  expectOk(compact2, \\\\\\\"compact2\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const pull2 = await sdk.threadView.pull(ref);\\\\n  expectOk(pull2, \\\\\\\"pull2\\\\\\\");\\\\n  const materialize = await sdk.threadView.materialize(ref, { path: outPath });\\\\n  expectOk(materialize, \\\\\\\"materialize\\\\\\\");\\\\n  await checkpoint(\\\\\\\"materialize\\\\\\\");\\\\n\\\\n  return {\\\\n    filePath,\\\\n    outPath,\\\\n    threadId,\\\\n    phases: {\\\\n      create,\\\\n      intake,\\\\n      drain,\\\\n      status,\\\\n      compact1,\\\\n      pull1,\\\\n      inspect1,\\\\n      mutate,\\\\n      rebuild,\\\\n      health2,\\\\n      compact2,\\\\n      pull2,\\\\n      materialize,\\\\n    },\\\\n  };\\\\n}\\\\n\\\",\\\"numLines\\\":351,\\\"startLine\\\":1,\\\"totalLines\\\":351}}}\\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:206:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"toolu_013qcP9H1cTsbjRatd4oHPeA\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"files\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/threads.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"59e4ba25250a5c1e49c2ca2fd7c558319580da3bb97279db6779139f14287fbd\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/smoke.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"5d737c488c61457eafa6c672a39e51502da72f9c05ad92339bede702a1da35ec\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/validation.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"0392107e9aa5e13ac67ce9da53624dc3972f03368acc18c21b49fd94d747e422\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/idempotency.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"715b2a8cbfa9eac1029ec431745ec898e493ba6be849e86676475ff4611b3079\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/intake.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"c352c13c4d5e21df97bd1ba98b84559fcafb2381b773f4a6b7884e211d0a56ef\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/projection.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"e7c3b43a2c17141afb0bbced55678e9058c6ae8cb879495c9fd59f10ca668c10\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/thread-migration.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"d48bd9960e50bc6290c66c0f1d4ac9a33744d629a5828496cbc46537a92548b4\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/turns.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"f70c2fd47b44246776594259935523a853dc5b41edcfe136fb34cbdd3b8d88a0\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/state-machine.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"c056bb4d49194d6157c8dcf98301a8a9fd028ef0a7aea74ddf136ff5945f84d9\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/work-queue.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"ea5f3c119d6fa35b61289eeb622da7ffd456d9b229369eea4ec359c3eac5aab2\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/fixtures.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"cc323c1ae3ce1f0a1e87826a1800c36a0037756ce775903b8dfd09cc3faced3e\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"test/work-execution.test.ts\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"eccf1434cd245dd7286-5.4)\\\\\\\\n276\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n277\\\\\\\\t// ── sdk.ts (change) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n278\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n279\\\\\\\\texport interface SdkConfig {\\\\\\\\n280\\\\\\\\t  provider?: DerivationProvider;   // XOR with inference (AC-1.1)\\\\\\\\n281\\\\\\\\t  inference?: InferenceConfig;\\\\\\\\n282\\\\\\\\t  // …existing fields unchanged\\\\\\\\n283\\\\\\\\t}\\\\\\\\n284\\\\\\\\t// removed exports: resolveNamedProvider, registeredProviderNames (AC-6.1)\\\\\\\\n285\\\\\\\\t```\\\\\\\\n286\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n287\\\\\\\\tSkeleton stubs follow the house pattern: `inference/` functions are machine-readable-contract code, so stubs return structured failures (`{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", message: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"not implemented\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }`) rather than throwing; `createInferenceProvider` may throw `NotImplementedError` at construction since construction errors throw by contract.\\\\\\\\n288\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n289\\\\\\\\t## Error Contract\\\\\\\\n290\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n291\\\\\\\\tConstruction errors are `TypeError`s naming the violated rule (existing contract): `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"createSdk config: exactly one of provider or inference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"inference.assignments missing kind chunk_summary_brief\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"inference.assignments.smoothed_prompt.prompt names unknown template 'smoothing-v2'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"view.visibility.maxTokens must be > targetTokens\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`. Operating failures never throw: they are `ProviderResult` failures with `reason` = the failure kind, flowing into form state via the unchanged Epic 02 paths (`failed` with reason `provider_failure` at exhaustion, terminal kinds immediately).\\\\\\\\n292\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n293\\\\\\\\t## Runtime Prerequisites\\\\\\\\n294\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n295\\\\\\\\t| Prerequisite | Where | How to Verify |\\\\\\\\n296\\\\\\\\t|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\n297\\\\\\\\t| Node ≥ 22 (`node:sqlite`, native `fetch`) | Local + CI | `node --version` (existing) |\\\\\\\\n298\\\\\\\\t| `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY` | Opt-in suite only | Suite self-reports ran/not-ran; never required by CI default |\\\\\\\\n299\\\\\\\\t| `LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL` | Opt-in suite, optional | Fixture default (cheap model) used when unset |\\\\\\\\n300\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n301\\\\\\\\tNo new dependencies. The OpenRouter fixture uses plain `fetch` in test code (epic NFR).\\\\\\\\n302\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n303\\\\\\\\t## Testing Strategy\\\\\\\\n304\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n305\\\\\\\\tThe mock boundary moves with this epic, and naming it precisely is the design's most important testing statement: **the external boundary is now the `ModelCall` function.** Flows 1–3 test against scripted fake functions (canned successes, scripted failure sequences, recording wrappers) — these are boundary mocks, not internal mocks, because the function *is* where LHC's code ends. The `DerivationProvider` interface is no longer the mock seam for this epic's tests; it's internal wiring under test (the adapter is the unit). Epic 02–04 suites keep their provider-level doubles untouched — their subject was never the adapter.\\\\\\\\n306\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n307\\\\\\\\tEverything else follows house rules: real temp SQLite for anything touching thread state (boundary tests, capstone), no internal module mocks, deterministic provider remains the CI default for all existing suites. The opt-in suite is the only network-touching code in the repo and is labeled in `verify-all` accounting as `ran` or `not-ran (reason)` — the four-tier verify scripts keep their names; composition changes are in Flow 6.\\\\\\\\n308\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n309\\\\\\\\tTest segmentation after the process suites delete: one vitest tier (default, no network) plus the env-gated inference suite in the same runner. No separate integration runner — the capstone is a vitest test that happens to make real calls when keyed.\\\\\\\\n310\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n311\\\\\\\\t## Work Breakdown\\\\\\\\n312\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n313\\\\\\\\tChunk order mirrors the epic's story order; the test plan carries per-chunk Red/Green tables.\\\\\\\\n314\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n315\\\\\\\\t| Chunk | Scope | ACs | Key risk |\\\\\\\\n316\\\\\\\\t|-------|-------|-----|----------|\\\\\\\\n317\\\\\\\\t| 0: Foundation | `inference/types.ts`, provenance type additions, fake-call fixtures (`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`: recording/scripted builders), prompt-template type + registry skeleton | — | Fixture validity: scripted fakes must satisfy the AC-1.2 contract shape |\\\\\\\\n318\\\\\\\\t| 1: CLI retirement | Flow 6 deletions + proofs + verify-script rewrite | 6.1–6.3 | Deleting a behavior test disguised as a process test (mitigation: surface snapshot + full-suite green before/after comparison) |\\\\\\\\n319\\\\\\\\t| 2: Seam + assignments | XOR, validation, adapter construction wiring | 1.1–1.4 | Validation drift from `FormKind` (mitigation: iterate the exported union, never a literal list) |\\\\\\\\n320\\\\\\\\t| 3: Adapter + prompts | Seven operations, templates, bounding, empty-output, provenance | 2.1–2.5 | Provenance authored from output instead of config (it's three config strings — keep it that way) |\\\\\\\\n321\\\\\\\\t| 4: Classification | Table, `safeCall`, handler provenance copies | 3.1–3.3 | Re-implementing retry logic the queue already owns (the adapter returns `retryable`; nothing more) |\\\\\\\\n322\\\\\\\\t| 5: Real suite + capstone | OpenRouter fixture, accounting guard, seven round-trips, capstone leg | 4.1–4.2 | Silent skip (the accounting guard is the deliverable; review it first) |\\\\\\\\n323\\\\\\\\t| 6: Boundary advance | Gate, grouping, peek-ahead, config change, Epic 03 test amendments | 5.1–5.5 | Floor-test amnesia — superseded tests must be amended per the ledger, not deleted wholesale |\\\\\\\\n324\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n325\\\\\\\\tDependencies: 0 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5; 1 and 6 are independent of the pipeline chunks (1 first by story order; 6 anytime).\\\\\\\\n326\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n327\\\\\\\\t## Deferred Items\\\\\\\\n328\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n329\\\\\\\\t| Item | Related | Reason | Where |\\\\\\\\n330\\\\\\\\t|------|---------|--------|-------|\\\\\\\\n331\\\\\\\\t| Tuned prompts + model choices | AC-2.3 | Dial-in working period; needs harness corpora | Backfills this pack pre-acceptance |\\\\\\\\n332\\\\\\\\t| Config generalization (token-band routing, fallback chains) | Flow 1 | Vocabulary derived from seven dialed-in answers | Post-dial-in design |\\\\\\\\n333\\\\\\\\t| Mid-turn safety ceiling | Flow 5 | POC removed it; no usage evidence of need | Revisit on evidence |\\\\\\\\n334\\\\\\\\t| Full integrated harness (capture ext, corpora, converter, journeys) | Flow 4 | Paired working period post-epic | `post-epic-4-work.md` |\\\\\\\\n335\\\\\\\\t| PI extension transport + config values | Flow 1 | Extension PRD | `pi-ext-prd-notes.md` |\\\\\\\\n336\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\"}]},\\\\\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"session_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"uuid\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"9b33975e-11b1-498f-8628-daa665e421dc\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"timestamp\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-12T23:20:42.061Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool_use_result\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"file\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"filePath\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"# Epic 05: Derivation Inference — Tech Design\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Epic:** `epic.md` (22 ACs / 15 TCs across 6 flows)\\\\\\\\n**Test Plan:** `test-plan.md` (full TC→test mapping, Red/Green detail)\\\\\\\\n**Tech Arch:** `../01-tech-arch.md`\\\\\\\\n**Consumed contracts:** Epic 02 provider seam and queue machinery (as built, deviations closed), Epic 03 visibility boundary (Flow 5 patches its contract), Epic 04 lifecycle exercise (as built; deviation state — e.g. overview boundary from `pull.meta` — is the consumed truth)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Context\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nFour epics built a derivation pipeline that has never made a model call. The seam was designed for this moment: `DerivationProvider` is an interface with seven semantic operations, the deterministic provider implements it with marker strings, and every handler, queue row, retry path, and state transition in `domains/` is provider-agnostic. This epic builds the second implementation of that interface — the real one — plus the injection path that lets a host supply model access without LHC ever touching a credential.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe design splits inference into two layers with different owners. The **semantic layer** is LHC's and lives in a new `src/inference/` area: seven prompt templates, output shaping, per-kind model assignment, failure classification, provenance stamping. The **transport layer** is the host's and crosses the boundary as exactly one function: `(provider, model, messages) → text or structured failure`. The host's function is the only code that knows what `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"anthropic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` means; LHC treats provider and model strings as opaque routing keys carried from config to call. This is the seam decision recorded in `post-epic-4-work.md`, and it is why the PI extension's eventual contribution is ~15 lines while the seven prompts stay tunable inside LHC without touching any host.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTwo riders land in the same epic because they consolidate decisions the inference work exposes. The CLI retirement deletes the one consumer of the named-provider registry and env-resolution path, which means provider arrival collapses to a single mechanism — injection at `createSdk` — exactly as the new `inference` config option arrives. Doing these together means the construction-validation story is written once against the final shape rather than patched twice. The boundary-advance patch is independent of inference entirely; it rides because it is small, fully validated in the POC (commits `1cf2dc45`, `6a9aa7a4`, `f12a850d` are the reference implementation), and it shares this pack's spec cycle rather than deserving its own.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOne thing this design deliberately does not do: chase output quality. The prompts ship structurally sound and explicitly pre-dial-in; the dial-in working period that follows the integrated-harness pairing produces the tuned prompts and model choices, and its findings backfill this pack before acceptance. Every TC in this epic asserts structure, routing, classification, and provenance — never whether a summary is good.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Design Decisions\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **DD-1: One transport function, not seven.** The host injects a single `ModelCall`; per-kind knowledge (which provider/model/prompt) lives in LHC config. Injecting seven functions would move LHC's derivation catalog into every host's wiring and make an eighth kind a breaking host change. The function takes the provider string per call, so multi-lane hosts route inside one function.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-2: The adapter is a `DerivationProvider`.** No seam movement. `createInferenceProvider(inference, config)` returns the same interface the deterministic provider implements; `createSdk` and everything below it see a provider, full stop. Handlers, queue, drain, states: zero changes for Flows 1–4 (except the one-line provenance copy, DD-4).\\\\\\\\n- **DD-3: Classification maps onto the existing `ProviderResult`.** Epic 02's failure shape is already `{ ok: false, retryable, reason }`. The classification table is a pure function `ModelCallFailureKind → { retryable, reason }` where `reason` is the kind string itself — stable, machine-readable, and the queue machinery consumes `retryable` exactly as it always has. No queue changes, no new states.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-4: Provenance rides the `ProviderResult` and is copied, never authored.** The ok branch gains `provenance?: { provider, model, prompt }`. The adapter stamps it (it alone knows the assignment); handlers copy it into `DerivedFormMetadata.provenance` alongside the existing mechanical stamps. The deterministic provider simply never sets it. This keeps the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"metadata is mechanically stamped\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" invariant: provenance comes from config-known strings, not model output.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-5: Construction is `provider` XOR `inference`.** `createSdk` accepts exactly one. The `inference` path validates the assignment map (all seven `FormKind`s present, every prompt name known) and builds the adapter; the `provider` path is unchanged and keeps the deterministic provider as the test default. Both/neither is a `TypeError` at construction, matching the existing config-mistakes-throw contract.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-6: The adapter owns a per-call timeout.** Epic tech-design question 2, resolved: a hung host function must not stall a drain, and the host shouldn't have to remember a duty LHC can enforce. `Promise.race` against `inference.timeoutMs` (default 60 000); a timeout classifies as `timeout` (retryable). A host that resolves later resolves into nothing.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-7: Input bounding is an adapter seam with a generous default.** Question 3, resolved: `summarizeToolResult` content over `inference.maxInputChars` (default 200 000) is truncated head-and-tail with a marker before prompt rendering. The bound exists so a pathological tool result cannot blow a small-context model; the value is a dial-in tunable, the seam is design.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-8: Prompts are modules in a name-keyed registry.** Question 1, resolved: `src/inference/prompts/` holds one module per template exporting `{ name, render(input) → messages }`; `prompts/index.ts` assembles the registry. Config selects by name; dial-in swaps by adding a module and editing config — no handler, adapter, or host changes. Versioning is in the name (`smoothing-v1`).\\\\\\\\n- **DD-9: The turn-end trigger is a gate in the pipeline, not a new seam.** Question 4, resolved: `pipeline.ts` already registers the advance `onCommit`, ordered before the queue poke, throw-isolated, both host modes. The change is registering it only when the batch's walk saw a `turn_end` event. Ordering against the queue poke is untouched — same registration site, same pinned order. The advance still runs at flush strictly after intake's COMMIT, in its own short transaction.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-10: Whole-turn eviction via `turn_id` grouping; turnless results degrade to singleton groups.** The zone walk groups tool results by `turn_id` (the message column), oldest group first by the group's lowest `source_event_order`. A `turn_id IS NULL` tool result (turnless intake is legal) is its own group — whole-turn semantics degenerate to whole-message for messages that belong to no turn, which preserves the epic's \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"no turn is ever partially flipped\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" without inventing membership.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-11: Floor retirement is a config-surface change, not a migration.** `VisibilityBudgets` becomes `{ maxTokens, targetTokens }` (64 000 / 32 000 defaults); `floorTokens` is deleted from the type, resolution, and validation. No schema change — `view_boundary` is untouched; the decision function changes shape, the storage doesn't. The protected floor's *job* survives structurally: the newest closed turn group is never evicted, and the open turn is invisible to a check that only runs at turn close.\\\\\\\\n- **DD-12: CLI deletion removes the registry, keeps the deterministic provider.** `src/providers/registry.ts` (named resolution, `LHC_PROVIDER`) dies with its only consumer. `src/providers/deterministic.ts` stays — it is the CI-default test provider and the byte-stable fixture substrate. The twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` suites delete; `verify-all` drops the `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate and gains the opt-in inference suite (which self-reports ran/not-ran).\\\\\\\\n- **DD-13: The real-inference suite is a second host of the same contract.** The OpenRouter-backed `ModelCall` lives in `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts`, implements the AC-1.2 contract over plain `fetch`, and runs Flow 1's construction/routing assertions parameterized (question 5, resolved: shared assertion helpers parameterized over the function, not duplicated tests). Key arrives via `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`; absence produces an explicit not-ran record, never a silent pass.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Top-Tier Surfaces\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Surface | Source | This Epic's Role |\\\\\\\\n|---------|--------|------------------|\\\\\\\\n| SDK assembly (`src/sdk.ts`) | Tech arch | Gains the `inference` construction path (DD-5); loses CLI-only exports |\\\\\\\\n| `shared/` | Tech arch | `derivation.ts` gains provenance on `ProviderResult`/metadata; `view.ts` loses `floorTokens` |\\\\\\\\n| `inference/` (new, peer of `providers/`) | This epic | The semantic layer: adapter, classification, prompts. Not a domain — it implements the provider seam, owns no thread state, queues no work |\\\\\\\\n| `domains/thread-view` | Tech arch | `boundary.ts` decision reshape (DD-10); seam/registration untouched except the gate (DD-9) |\\\\\\\\n| `domains/intake-stream` | Tech arch | One-line gate on the advance registration (DD-9) |\\\\\\\\n| `domains/messages`, `domains/turns` | Tech arch | One-line provenance copy in each handler's form write (DD-4) |\\\\\\\\n| `cli/`, `providers/registry` | Tech arch (retiring) | Deleted (DD-12) |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`inference/` must not import `domains/`; it depends only on `shared/` types. The boundaries check gains that rule.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Module Architecture\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\nsrc/\\\\\\\\n├── inference/                          # NEW: the semantic layer (DD-1..DD-8)\\\\\\\\n│   ├── types.ts                        # NEW: ModelCall, ModelCallResult, ModelCallFailureKind,\\\\\\\\n│   │                                   #      InferenceConfig, ModelAssignment, ResolvedInferenceConfig\\\\\\\\n│   ├── classify.ts                     # NEW: classification table + exception containment (DD-3)\\\\\\\\n│   ├── adapter.ts                      # NEW: createInferenceProvider — DerivationProvider impl (DD-2)\\\\\\\\n│   └── prompts/\\\\\\\\n│       ├── index.ts                    # NEW: PROMPT_REGISTRY (name → PromptTemplate) (DD-8)\\\\\\\\n│       ├── smoothing-v1.ts             # NEW: ported from POC, settled\\\\\\\\n│       ├── tool-call-v1.ts             # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\\\\\n│       ├── tool-result-v1.ts           # NEW: pre-dial-in (input bounding lives here-adjacent, DD-7)\\\\\\\\n│       ├── turn-compose-v1.ts          # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\\\\\n│       ├── lower-band-v1.ts            # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\\\\\n│       ├── chunk-detailed-v1.ts        # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\\\\\n│       └── chunk-brief-v1.ts           # NEW: pre-dial-in\\\\\\\\n├── sdk.ts                              # MODIFIED: provider XOR inference; assignment validation;\\\\\\\\n│                                       #           export cleanup (registry exports removed)\\\\\\\\n├── shared/\\\\\\\\n│   ├── derivation.ts                   # MODIFIED: ProviderResult ok-branch + DerivedFormMetadata\\\\\\\\n│   │                                   #           gain provenance? (DD-4)\\\\\\\\n│   └── view.ts                         # MODIFIED: VisibilityBudgets → { maxTokens, targetTokens },\\\\\\\\n│                                       #           defaults 64000/32000 (DD-11)\\\\\\\\n├── domains/\\\\\\\\n│   ├── intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts   # MODIFIED: advance registration gated on turn_end (DD-9)\\\\\\\\n│   ├── thread-view/internal/boundary.ts     # MODIFIED: turn-grouped zone, peek-ahead decision (DD-10)\\\\\\\\n│   ├── messages/internal/…handlers          # MODIFIED: copy result.provenance → metadata (DD-4)\\\\\\\\n│   └── turns/internal/…handlers             # MODIFIED: same one-line copy (DD-4)\\\\\\\\n├── providers/\\\\\\\\n│   ├── deterministic.ts                # EXISTS: unchanged — CI-default test provider\\\\\\\\n│   └── registry.ts                     # DELETED (DD-12)\\\\\\\\n└── cli/                                # DELETED entirely (DD-12)\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Module Responsibility Matrix\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Module | Status | Responsibility | Dependencies | ACs |\\\\\\\\n|--------|--------|----------------|--------------|-----|\\\\\\\\n| `inference/types.ts` | NEW | The boundary vocabulary: `ModelCall` contract, failure kinds, config shapes | none (pure types) | AC-1.2 |\\\\\\\\n| `inference/classify.ts` | NEW | `classifyFailure(kind)` table; `safeCall` wrapper (timeout, thrown-exception containment) | `types` | AC-3.1, AC-3.3, AC-2.4 (empty-output check lives in adapter but classifies here) |\\\\\\\\n| `inference/adapter.ts` | NEW | The seven operations: render prompt → bounded input → `safeCall` → shape → `ProviderResult` with provenance | `types`, `classify`, `prompts`, `shared/derivation` | AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.4, AC-2.5, AC-1.4 |\\\\\\\\n| `inference/prompts/*` | NEW | Named templates: `render(input) → messages`; registry | `shared/derivation` (input types) | AC-2.2, AC-2.3 |\\\\\\\\n| `sdk.ts` | MODIFIED | XOR validation, assignment validation (all seven kinds, known prompts), adapter construction; SDK-only export surface | `inference/*`, existing | AC-1.1, AC-1.3, AC-6.1 |\\\\\\\\n| `shared/derivation.ts` | MODIFIED | Provenance fields on result/metadata | — | AC-2.5 |\\\\\\\\n| `domains/*/handlers` | MODIFIED | Copy `provenance` into form metadata on success | — | AC-2.5 |\\\\\\\\n| `shared/view.ts` | MODIFIED | Two-field budgets, 64k/32k defaults, `max > target` validation | — | AC-5.4 |\\\\\\\\n| `intake-stream/pipeline.ts` | MODIFIED | Register advance only when batch committed a `turn_end` | — | AC-5.1 |\\\\\\\\n| `thread-view/boundary.ts` | MODIFIED | Turn-grouped zone read; peek-ahead whole-group decision; newest-group protection | — | AC-5.1–5.3, AC-5.5 |\\\\\\\\n| `test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts` | NEW (test) | The real `ModelCall` host: fetch to OpenRouter, contract-conformant failures, key resolution | — | AC-4.1 |\\\\\\\\n| deletions (`cli/`, `registry.ts`, process suites) | DELETED | — | — | AC-6.1–6.3 |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nQueue machinery, drain, states, repair report: explicitly **untouched** (DD-3). Flow 3's behavior lands entirely in `classify.ts` + the adapter; the queue consumes `retryable` as it has since Epic 02.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Flow-by-Flow Design\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Flow 1: Inference Wiring and Model Assignment (AC-1.1–1.4)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nConstruction is where every mistake should die. `createSdk` gains the XOR check first (before any other validation, so the error names the rule, not a downstream symptom), then — on the `inference` path — assignment validation: iterate the seven `FormKind`s, require each present with non-empty `provider`/`model` strings and a `prompt` name found in `PROMPT_REGISTRY`; reject unknown kind keys. The resolved config then feeds `createInferenceProvider`, whose return value drops into the existing `resolved.provider` slot — everything downstream of construction is Epic 02 code.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\n    participant Host\\\\\\\\n    participant SDK as createSdk\\\\\\\\n    participant ADP as inference/adapter\\\\\\\\n    participant Q as drain/handler (Epic 02, unchanged)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    Note over Host,SDK: AC-1.1, AC-1.3: XOR + assignment validation\\\\\\\\n    Host->>SDK: createSdk({ inference: { call, assignments } })\\\\\\\\n    SDK->>SDK: validate XOR, all 7 kinds, known prompts (throws naming violation)\\\\\\\\n    SDK->>ADP: createInferenceProvider(resolvedInference, config)\\\\\\\\n    ADP-->>SDK: DerivationProvider\\\\\\\\n    Note over Q,ADP: AC-1.4: per-call routing at drain time\\\\\\\\n    Q->>ADP: provider.smoothPrompt({ text })\\\\\\\\n    ADP->>Host: call({ provider, model, messages }) — the kind's assignment\\\\\\\\n    Host-->>ADP: { ok: true, text }\\\\\\\\n    ADP-->>Q: { ok: true, text, provenance }\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe routing fact worth stating precisely: the adapter closes over the assignment map at construction; each operation looks up its own kind's entry at call time. There is no per-thread or per-item routing state — two drained items of different kinds calling different lanes is just two closure lookups.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Flow 2: The Adapter and the Seven Prompts (AC-2.1–2.5)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEach adapter operation is the same five-step pipeline: (1) bound the input where DD-7 applies, (2) `render` the kind's prompt template into a messages array, (3) `safeCall` the host function with the kind's assignment, (4) reject empty/whitespace text as `empty_output`, (5) return `{ ok: true, text, provenance }`. The operations differ only in which template renders and which input fields embed — the pipeline is one private function parameterized by kind.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA prompt template's contract, worked example (`tool-result-v1`):\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\n// prompts/tool-result-v1.ts\\\\\\\\nexport const toolResultV1: PromptTemplate<{ toolName: string; content: string }> = {\\\\\\\\n  name: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tool-result-v1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  render: (i) => [\\\\\\\\n    { role: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", content: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"You summarize tool output for an engineering record. Preserve concrete facts: paths, identifiers, counts, error text. State the outcome plainly. No commentary, no speculation, 150 words maximum.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" },\\\\\\\\n    { role: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", content: `Tool: ${i.toolName}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nOutput:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n${i.content}` },\\\\\\\\n  ],\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe rendered array is the entire call — single-turn, no tools, no streaming (Assumption 2). The smoothing template ports the POC's settled prompt text verbatim; the other six are written to its standard (concrete instruction, fact-preservation rule, length bound) and carry a `// PRE-DIAL-IN` marker comment. The chunk-brief template's input is already receipt-stripped by the Epic 02 handler before the provider is called — the adapter inherits that structural guarantee and the test plan re-asserts it through the adapter (TC-2.2).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWhat the adapter must never do: parse the response for outcomes, receipts, or any mechanical fact. Those stamps happen in handlers from the record, exactly as under the deterministic provider (AC-2.2). The adapter's only additions to the result are the text and the config-known provenance strings.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Flow 3: Failure Classification (AC-3.1–3.3)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`classify.ts` is two small exports. The table:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\nexport const FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION: Record<ModelCallFailureKind, { retryable: boolean }> = {\\\\\\\\n  rate_limit: { retryable: true },\\\\\\\\n  timeout: { retryable: true },\\\\\\\\n  network: { retryable: true },\\\\\\\\n  empty_output: { retryable: true },\\\\\\\\n  other: { retryable: true },\\\\\\\\n  auth: { retryable: false },\\\\\\\\n  invalid_request: { retryable: false },\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAnd `safeCall(call, input, timeoutMs)`: wraps the host function in try/catch (thrown → `{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", message }`) and a timeout race (→ `{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", message }`). The adapter maps the structured failure to Epic 02's `ProviderResult`: `{ ok: false, retryable: FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION[kind].retryable, reason: kind }`. From there the existing machinery does everything — backoff on retryable, immediate `failed` on terminal, exhaustion copying attempts/last-error into form metadata. The misconfigured-lane story (config names an unauthed provider) needs no code: the host function returns `auth`, classification makes it terminal, the health report shows `failed` forms with reason `auth` per assigned kind.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Flow 4: Real-Inference Verification (AC-4.1–4.2)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe suite (`test/inference-real.test.ts`) keys on `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`. Present: build the OpenRouter `ModelCall` fixture, construct an SDK with all seven kinds assigned to the configured cheap model (`LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL`, defaulted in the fixture), and run the legs. Absent: emit one structured not-ran record (a test that asserts-and-reports the skip reason loudly — visible in output as `NOT-RAN: real-inference (LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY unset)`, never a green checkmark indistinguishable from a pass). The accounting rule is implemented as a suite-level guard, not per-test `skip`s, so the run/not-run state is one fact reported once.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe capstone reuses Epic 04's lifecycle sequence with the assertion set swapped: where the deterministic leg asserted byte-exact replay and marker content, this leg asserts structure — every `FormKind` reaches `ready` at least once with non-empty text containing no deterministic marker pattern (`/^(smoothed|toolcall|toolresult|rendering|projection|detailed|brief)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\(/`), provenance naming the real model, and the checkpoint-coherence ladder (mutation clears → pending; drain → ready with *different* content; second compact's view reflects post-edit content).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Flow 5: Turn-End Boundary Advance (AC-5.1–5.5)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTwo modules change. In `pipeline.ts`, the walk already knows whether the batch contained a `turn_end` (it processes the event kinds); the advance registration at line ~150 gains that condition. Everything about the registration — first-before-poke order, throw isolation, both host modes, flush-after-COMMIT — is untouched.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIn `boundary.ts`, the zone read gains `turn_id` and the decision regroups:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\nreadZoneToolResults: + turn_id per row (same WHERE, same order)\\\\\\\\ngroupZone: consecutive rows by turn_id → groups, each { turnId, sourceEventOrders, tokenSum }\\\\\\\\n           (turn_id NULL ⇒ singleton group, DD-10)\\\\\\\\nadvanceDecision(groups, { maxTokens, targetTokens }):\\\\\\\\n  total ≤ max → null\\\\\\\\n  walk groups oldest-first, excluding the newest group always:\\\\\\\\n    evict group only if (remaining − group.tokenSum) ≥ target   ← peek-ahead, AC-5.3\\\\\\\\n    newPosition = group's highest sourceEventOrder\\\\\\\\n  return newPosition (lands in [target, target + one group))\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWorked example (golden G2 in the test plan): groups of 30k/25k/20k/15k tokens (oldest→newest), max 64k, target 32k. Total 90k > max. Evict 30k → 60k remaining, ≥ 32k ✓. Evict 25k → 35k remaining, ≥ 32k ✓. Peek 20k: 35k − 20k = 15k < 32k → stop. Boundary lands after the 25k group; zone = 35k, in [32k, 32k+20k). The 15k newest group was never a candidate.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe shared-query invariant survives: `visibilityZoneTokens` (status's sum) and the decision's walk read the same WHERE-clause population, so status and advance can never disagree about the zone — the property the existing code makes structural stays structural.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`VisibilityBudgets` loses `floorTokens` everywhere it appears: the type, `resolveViewConfig`'s defaults and validation (which gains `maxTokens > targetTokens`), and the status read's reporting shape is unchanged (`zoneTokens`/`maxTokens` already). Existing Epic 03 tests asserting floor behavior are superseded — the test plan's amendment ledger names each one (the spec-sanctioned test-change path, as Epic 02's F-3 established).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Flow 6: CLI Retirement (AC-6.1–6.3)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDeletion plus proof. Delete `src/cli/`, the `src/cli.ts` bin entrypoint (a separate file outside the directory — it imports `./cli/index.js` and breaks the build if left), the `bin` manifest entry, and the `dev:cli` script; delete `src/providers/registry.ts` and its two `sdk.ts` re-exports; delete the twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` files; rewrite `verify`/`verify-all` (the `SKIP: cli-process` echo and `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate go; `verify-all` becomes red-verify + full vitest including the self-accounting inference suite). The proof is three-legged: a public-API surface snapshot test (import the package entry, assert the export-name set against a checked-in expected list — catches both leftover CLI exports and accidental removals), a source-level grep test for `LHC_PROVIDER`/registry references, and the unchanged-SDK-coverage check (the full remaining suite green proves no behavior test rode out with the process suites — process suites asserted *parity*, never unique behavior, which is why deletion is safe).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe Epic 04 deviation note is written into this pack's deviation table at story completion: spawned `inspect health` parity was backfilled before Epic 05, and this story deletes the spawned-process parity surface instead of carrying it forward.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Interface Definitions\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\n// ── inference/types.ts ──────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n/** The one function a host supplies. Single-turn completion; provider/model\\\\\\\\n *  are opaque routing keys the host's implementation interprets. (AC-1.2) */\\\\\\\\nexport type ModelCall = (input: ModelCallInput) => Promise<ModelCallResult>;\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nexport interface ModelCallInput {\\\\\\\\n  provider: string;\\\\\\\\n  model: string;\\\\\\\\n  messages: { role: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; content: string }[];\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nexport type ModelCallResult =\\\\\\\\n  | { ok: true; text: string }\\\\\\\\n  | { ok: false; kind: ModelCallFailureKind; message: string };\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n/** `empty_output` is adapter-generated (AC-2.4); hosts never return it.\\\\\\\\n *  Thrown exceptions classify as `other` (AC-3.3). */\\\\\\\\nexport type ModelCallFailureKind =\\\\\\\\n  | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rate_limit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"network\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"invalid_request\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\";\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nexport interface ModelAssignment {\\\\\\\\n  provider: string;\\\\\\\\n  model: string;\\\\\\\\n  prompt: string; // must name a PROMPT_REGISTRY entry (AC-1.3)\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n/** SdkConfig.inference — the alternative to SdkConfig.provider (DD-5). */\\\\\\\\nexport interface InferenceConfig {\\\\\\\\n  call: ModelCall;\\\\\\\\n  assignments: Record<FormKind, ModelAssignment>; // all seven, validated complete\\\\\\\\n  timeoutMs?: number;      // default 60_000 (DD-6)\\\\\\\\n  maxInputChars?: number;  // default 200_000 (DD-7)\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n// ── inference/prompts/index.ts ──────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nexport interface PromptTemplate<I = unknown> {\\\\\\\\n  name: string;\\\\\\\\n  render(input: I): ModelCallInput[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"messages\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"];\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\nexport const PROMPT_REGISTRY: Record<string, PromptTemplate<never>>;\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n// ── inference/classify.ts ───────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nexport const FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION: Record<ModelCallFailureKind, { retryable: boolean }>;\\\\\\\\n/** try/catch + timeout race around the host function (AC-3.3, DD-6). */\\\\\\\\nexport function safeCall(\\\\\\\\n  call: ModelCall, input: ModelCallInput, timeoutMs: number,\\\\\\\\n): Promise<ModelCallResult>;\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n// ── inference/adapter.ts ────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n/** The real provider. Same interface the deterministic provider implements;\\\\\\\\n *  createSdk slots it into resolved.provider (DD-2). */\\\\\\\\nexport function createInferenceProvider(config: ResolvedInferenceConfig): DerivationProvider;\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n// ── shared/derivation.ts (additions only) ───────────────────────\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nexport interface ProviderProvenance { provider: string; model: string; prompt: string }\\\\\\\\n// ProviderResult ok-branch: { ok: true; text: string; provenance?: ProviderProvenance }\\\\\\\\n// DerivedFormMetadata: + provenance?: ProviderProvenance   (DD-4; AC-2.5)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n// ── shared/view.ts (change) ─────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nexport interface VisibilityBudgets { maxTokens: number; targetTokens: number }\\\\\\\\n// defaults 64_000 / 32_000; resolveViewConfig validates maxTokens > targetTokens (AC-5.4)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n// ── sdk.ts (change) ─────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nexport interface SdkConfig {\\\\\\\\n  provider?: DerivationProvider;   // XOR with inference (AC-1.1)\\\\\\\\n  inference?: InferenceConfig;\\\\\\\\n  // …existing fields unchanged\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\n// removed exports: resolveNamedProvider, registeredProviderNames (AC-6.1)\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSkeleton stubs follow the house pattern: `inference/` functions are machine-readable-contract code, so stubs return structured failures (`{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", message: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"not implemented\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }`) rather than throwing; `createInferenceProvider` may throw `NotImplementedError` at construction since construction errors throw by contract.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Error Contract\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nConstruction errors are `TypeError`s naming the violated rule (existing contract): `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"createSdk config: exactly one of provider or inference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"inference.assignments missing kind chunk_summary_brief\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"inference.assignments.smoothed_prompt.prompt names unknown template 'smoothing-v2'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"view.visibility.maxTokens must be > targetTokens\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`. Operating failures never throw: they are `ProviderResult` failures with `reason` = the failure kind, flowing into form state via the unchanged Epic 02 paths (`failed` with reason `provider_failure` at exhaustion, terminal kinds immediately).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Runtime Prerequisites\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Prerequisite | Where | How to Verify |\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\n| Node ≥ 22 (`node:sqlite`, native `fetch`) | Local + CI | `node --version` (existing) |\\\\\\\\n| `LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY` | Opt-in suite only | Suite self-reports ran/not-ran; never required by CI default |\\\\\\\\n| `LHC_OPENROUTER_MODEL` | Opt-in suite, optional | Fixture default (cheap model) used when unset |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nNo new dependencies. The OpenRouter fixture uses plain `fetch` in test code (epic NFR).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Testing Strategy\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe mock boundary moves with this epic, and naming it precisely is the design's most important testing statement: **the external boundary is now the `ModelCall` function.** Flows 1–3 test against scripted fake functions (canned successes, scripted failure sequences, recording wrappers) — these are boundary mocks, not internal mocks, because the function *is* where LHC's code ends. The `DerivationProvider` interface is no longer the mock seam for this epic's tests; it's internal wiring under test (the adapter is the unit). Epic 02–04 suites keep their provider-level doubles untouched — their subject was never the adapter.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEverything else follows house rules: real temp SQLite for anything touching thread state (boundary tests, capstone), no internal module mocks, deterministic provider remains the CI default for all existing suites. The opt-in suite is the only network-touching code in the repo and is labeled in `verify-all` accounting as `ran` or `not-ran (reason)` — the four-tier verify scripts keep their names; composition changes are in Flow 6.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTest segmentation after the process suites delete: one vitest tier (default, no network) plus the env-gated inference suite in the same runner. No separate integration runner — the capstone is a vitest test that happens to make real calls when keyed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Work Breakdown\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nChunk order mirrors the epic's story order; the test plan carries per-chunk Red/Green tables.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Chunk | Scope | ACs | Key risk |\\\\\\\\n|-------|-------|-----|----------|\\\\\\\\n| 0: Foundation | `inference/types.ts`, provenance type additions, fake-call fixtures (`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`: recording/scripted builders), prompt-template type + registry skeleton | — | Fixture validity: scripted fakes must satisfy the AC-1.2 contract shape |\\\\\\\\n| 1: CLI retirement | Flow 6 deletions + proofs + verify-script rewrite | 6.1–6.3 | Deleting a behavior test disguised as a process test (mitigation: surface snapshot + full-suite green before/after comparison) |\\\\\\\\n| 2: Seam + assignments | XOR, validation, adapter construction wiring | 1.1–1.4 | Validation drift from `FormKind` (mitigation: iterate the exported union, never a literal list) |\\\\\\\\n| 3: Adapter + prompts | Seven operations, templates, bounding, empty-output, provenance | 2.1–2.5 | Provenance authored from output instead of config (it's three config strings — keep it that way) |\\\\\\\\n| 4: Classification | Table, `safeCall`, handler provenance copies | 3.1–3.3 | Re-implementing retry logic the queue already owns (the adapter returns `retryable`; nothing more) |\\\\\\\\n| 5: Real suite + capstone | OpenRouter fixture, accounting guard, seven round-trips, capstone leg | 4.1–4.2 | Silent skip (the accounting guard is the deliverable; review it first) |\\\\\\\\n| 6: Boundary advance | Gate, grouping, peek-ahead, config change, Epic 03 test amendments | 5.1–5.5 | Floor-test amnesia — superseded tests must be amended per the ledger, not deleted wholesale |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDependencies: 0 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5; 1 and 6 are independent of the pipeline chunks (1 first by story order; 6 anytime).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Deferred Items\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Item | Related | Reason | Where |\\\\\\\\n|------|---------|--------|-------|\\\\\\\\n| Tuned prompts + model choices | AC-2.3 | Dial-in working period; needs harness corpora | Backfills this pack pre-acceptance |\\\\\\\\n| Config generalization (token-band routing, fallback chains) | Flow 1 | Vocabulary derived from seven dialed-in answers | Post-dial-in design |\\\\\\\\n| Mid-turn safety ceiling | Flow 5 | POC removed it; no usage evidence of need | Revisit on evidence |\\\\\\\\n| Full integrated harness (capture ext, corpora, converter, journeys) | Flow 4 | Paired working period post-epic | `post-epic-4-work.md` |\\\\\\\\n| PI extension transport + config values | Flow 1 | Extension PRD | `pi-ext-prd-notes.md` |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"numLines\\\\\\\":336,\\\\\\\"startLine\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\"totalLines\\\\\\\":336}}}\\\\ndocs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:38:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"tool_use_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"toolu_011UEvf2ef5fA5bCFxwd3URC\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool_result\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"1\\\\\\\\t# Epic 05: Derivation Inference — Test Plan\\\\\\\\n2\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n3\\\\\\\\t**Epic:** `epic.md` (22 ACs / 15 TCs)\\\\\\\\n4\\\\\\\\t**Tech Design:** `tech-design.md`\\\\\\\\n5\\\\\\\\t**Coverage:** all 15 TCs mapped below; no TC unowned, none double-owned.\\\\\\\\n6\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n7\\\\\\\\t## Suite Layout\\\\\\\\n8\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n9\\\\\\\\t| File | Tier | TCs |\\\\\\\\n10\\\\\\\\t|------|------|-----|\\\\\\\\n11\\\\\\\\t| `test/inference-construction.test.ts` | default | TC-1.1 |\\\\\\\\n12\\\\\\\\t| `test/inference-routing.test.ts` | default | TC-1.2 |\\\\\\\\n13\\\\\\\\t| `test/inference-adapter.test.ts` | default | TC-2.1, TC-2.3 |\\\\\\\\n14\\\\\\\\t| `test/inference-prompts.test.ts` | default | TC-2.2 |\\\\\\\\n15\\\\\\\\t| `test/inference-classification.test.ts` | default | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 |\\\\\\\\n16\\\\\\\\t| `test/inference-real.test.ts` | opt-in (`LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`) | TC-4.1, TC-4.2, TC-4.3 |\\\\\\\\n17\\\\\\\\t| `test/view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + `test/view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` (new) | default | TC-5.1, TC-5.2, TC-5.3 |\\\\\\\\n18\\\\\\\\t| `test/retirement.test.ts` | default | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 |\\\\\\\\n19\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n20\\\\\\\\tDeleted with Flow 6: the twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` files. The `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate leaves `verify-all`; the opt-in inference suite self-reports inside the default runner (it is env-gated, not script-gated).\\\\\\\\n21\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n22\\\\\\\\t## Fixture Contracts\\\\\\\\n23\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n24\\\\\\\\t**`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the scripted-host builders every Flow 1–3 test uses:\\\\\\\\n25\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n26\\\\\\\\t- `recordingCall(responses: Record<FormKind, string>)` → `{ call: ModelCall, log: ModelCallInput[] }`. Resolves each call with the kind's canned text (kind inferred from the assignment the test built — in practice keyed by `model` string, which tests assign uniquely per kind). Every input pushed to `log`.\\\\\\\\n27\\\\\\\\t- `scriptedCall(script: ModelCallResult[])` → `ModelCall` returning script entries in order; throws if exhausted (a test calling more than scripted is a test bug, loudly).\\\\\\\\n28\\\\\\\\t- `throwingCall(error: Error)` → `ModelCall` that rejects/throws.\\\\\\\\n29\\\\\\\\t- `hangingCall()` → `ModelCall` that never settles (timeout leg; tests pass a small `timeoutMs`).\\\\\\\\n30\\\\\\\\t- `validAssignments(overrides?)` → a complete seven-kind assignment map for construction tests, each kind assigned a distinct fake provider/model string.\\\\\\\\n31\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n32\\\\\\\\tAll builders return contract-conformant shapes (AC-1.2); the conformance helper below runs against `recordingCall`'s output in its own test, so fixture drift from the contract is caught by the suite, not discovered in stories.\\\\\\\\n33\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n34\\\\\\\\t**`test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts`** (Chunk 5) — the real host: `createOpenRouterCall(key, defaultModel)` → `ModelCall` over `fetch` to OpenRouter's OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint; implements the exact `ModelCall` contract (OpenRouter takes one model slug, so the fixture resolves the `provider`+`model` routing keys into that slug — the host-side interpretation AC-1.2 grants), maps HTTP 401/403 → `auth`, 429 → `rate_limit`, 400 → `invalid_request`, network errors → `network`, everything else → `other`, and extracts non-empty assistant text into the `ModelCallResult` success shape. `resolveRealSuiteEnv()` → `{ key, model } | { notRan: reason }`.\\\\\\\\n35\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n36\\\\\\\\t**`test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the parameterized contract assertions (DD-13): `assertModelCallContract(call)` (shape of success/failure results on a probe call) and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(call, assignments)` (TC-1.2's routing assertions extracted so TC-4.3 runs them against the real host unchanged).\\\\\\\\n37\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n38\\\\\\\\t**Boundary fixtures** — `test/fixtures/` gains `seedTurnedToolResults(db, turns: { results: number[] }[])`: closed turns with tool results of given token estimates, plus turnless-result and open-turn variants. Built on the existing Epic 03 fixture helpers; token estimates seeded via content sized to the estimator (existing pattern from view-boundary tests).\\\\\\\\n39\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n40\\\\\\\\t## TC → Test Mapping\\\\\\\\n41\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n42\\\\\\\\t### TC-1.1 — Construction matrix (AC-1.1, AC-1.3)\\\\\\\\n43\\\\\\\\t`inference-construction.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n44\\\\\\\\t- missing one kind (each of seven, parameterized) → `TypeError` naming the kind\\\\\\\\n45\\\\\\\\t- unknown prompt name → `TypeError` naming kind and prompt\\\\\\\\n46\\\\\\\\t- unknown kind key in assignments → `TypeError` naming it\\\\\\\\n47\\\\\\\\t- both `provider` and `inference` → `TypeError` naming the XOR rule\\\\\\\\n48\\\\\\\\t- neither → `TypeError` naming the XOR rule\\\\\\\\n49\\\\\\\\t- empty `provider`/`model` string in one assignment → `TypeError`\\\\\\\\n50\\\\\\\\t- complete valid config → constructs; a seeded drain lands a form `ready` (operates, not just constructs)\\\\\\\\n51\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n52\\\\\\\\t### TC-1.2 — Routing (AC-1.2, AC-1.4)\\\\\\\\n53\\\\\\\\t`inference-routing.test.ts` — `recordingCall` + seven distinct lanes in `validAssignments`:\\\\\\\\n54\\\\\\\\t- seed a thread exercising all seven kinds; drain; assert `log` contains each kind's call carrying exactly its assigned provider/model strings\\\\\\\\n55\\\\\\\\t- every logged `messages` is single-turn shape (`system`+`user` roles only, string content)\\\\\\\\n56\\\\\\\\t- three-lane mixed config: per-call routing matches item kind with no cross-kind bleed\\\\\\\\n57\\\\\\\\t- `assertModelCallContract(recordingCall(...).call)` — fixture conformance leg\\\\\\\\n58\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n59\\\\\\\\t### TC-2.1 — Seven kinds land with canned content + provenance (AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.5)\\\\\\\\n60\\\\\\\\t`inference-adapter.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n61\\\\\\\\t- drain seeded thread → all seven `FormKind`s `ready`; content equals the canned text (post-shaping)\\\\\\\\n62\\\\\\\\t- handler equivalence: the same seeded thread drained under the deterministic provider lands the same form rows (states, subjects) with marker content — proving handlers unchanged (AC-2.1)\\\\\\\\n63\\\\\\\\t- outcomes/receipts on tool and rendering forms match the record, not the canned text (canned text deliberately claims the wrong outcome; the stamp must disagree with it)\\\\\\\\n64\\\\\\\\t- each form's `metadata.provenance` equals its assignment `{ provider, model, prompt }`; deterministic-provider forms carry none\\\\\\\\n65\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n66\\\\\\\\t### TC-2.2 — Prompt-rendering goldens (AC-2.2, AC-2.3)\\\\\\\\n67\\\\\\\\t`inference-prompts.test.ts` — golden files in `test/goldens/prompts/`:\\\\\\\\n68\\\\\\\\t- for each of the seven templates: `render(fixtureInput)` matches its golden (`smoothing-v1.golden.json` …) — fixture content embedded, single-turn shape\\\\\\\\n69\\\\\\\\t- registry completeness: every config-selectable name resolves; `PROMPT_REGISTRY` covers all seven default names\\\\\\\\n70\\\\\\\\t- brief-summary stripping: drive `summarizeChunkBrief` through the adapter with a recording call; the rendered messages contain outcome tokens but no receipt text from the fixture (the Epic 02 stripping contract holds through the adapter)\\\\\\\\n71\\\\\\\\t- bounding (DD-7): a `summarizeToolResult` input over `maxInputChars` renders with head+tail+marker, under-limit input renders whole\\\\\\\\n72\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n73\\\\\\\\t### TC-2.3 — Empty output is a failure (AC-2.4)\\\\\\\\n74\\\\\\\\t`inference-adapter.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n75\\\\\\\\t- `scriptedCall([{ ok: true, text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }, { ok: true, text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"real\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }])` → first attempt classifies `empty_output` retryable; retry lands `ready` with `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"real\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`\\\\\\\\n76\\\\\\\\t- exhaustion leg: all-whitespace script → form `failed`, reason `provider_failure`, last error names `empty_output`\\\\\\\\n77\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n78\\\\\\\\t### TC-3.1 — Classification table drives retry/terminal (AC-3.1, AC-3.2)\\\\\\\\n79\\\\\\\\t`inference-classification.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n80\\\\\\\\t- table assertion: `FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION` matches the AC-3.1 mapping exactly (data asserted as data)\\\\\\\\n81\\\\\\\\t- `rate_limit` ×2 then success → `ready`; attempts recorded on the path (report shows retrying mid-way under a paused clock, per existing Epic 02 clock-injection pattern)\\\\\\\\n82\\\\\\\\t- `auth` → `failed` first attempt; scripted call shows exactly one invocation; stable reason\\\\\\\\n83\\\\\\\\t- `network` to exhaustion → `failed`, `provider_failure`, `metadata.lastError` preserved\\\\\\\\n84\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n85\\\\\\\\t### TC-3.2 — Exception containment (AC-3.2, AC-3.3)\\\\\\\\n86\\\\\\\\t`inference-classification.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n87\\\\\\\\t- `throwingCall` on one kind amid a multi-kind drain → that item retries as `other`; all other items complete; drain returns normally (no escape)\\\\\\\\n88\\\\\\\\t- exhaustion lands thrown message as last error\\\\\\\\n89\\\\\\\\t- `hangingCall` with `timeoutMs: 50` → classified `timeout`, drain continues (DD-6 leg)\\\\\\\\n90\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n91\\\\\\\\t### TC-4.1 — Real round-trips + accounting (AC-4.1)\\\\\\\\n92\\\\\\\\t`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n93\\\\\\\\t- keyed: each of seven kinds round-trips once → `ready`, non-empty, no marker pattern\\\\\\\\n94\\\\\\\\t- unkeyed (controlled: suite re-invoked with key stripped from env in a subprocess-free way — the guard function called directly with empty env) → `notRan` record with reason; assert the record's shape and that it is not a pass\\\\\\\\n95\\\\\\\\t- the suite-level guard emits exactly one ran/not-ran line into the run output\\\\\\\\n96\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n97\\\\\\\\t### TC-4.2 — Capstone (AC-4.2)\\\\\\\\n98\\\\\\\\t`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n99\\\\\\\\t- Epic 04's lifecycle sequence against the real adapter: intake → drain → compact → pull → inspect → edit → rebuild → drain → compact → materialize\\\\\\\\n100\\\\\\\\t- structural assertions: every kind `ready` ≥ once; no marker pattern anywhere; provenance names the real model; mutation-cleared forms regenerate with content ≠ pre-edit content; second compact's view reflects post-edit content; health coherent at each checkpoint\\\\\\\\n101\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n102\\\\\\\\t### TC-4.3 — Contract conformance, both hosts (AC-1.2, AC-4.1)\\\\\\\\n103\\\\\\\\t`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n104\\\\\\\\t- `assertModelCallContract(openRouterCall)` and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(openRouterCall, realAssignments)` — the same helpers TC-1.2 ran against the fake, unchanged (DD-13)\\\\\\\\n105\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n106\\\\\\\\t### TC-5.1 — Turn-end trigger + whole-turn eviction (AC-5.1, AC-5.2)\\\\\\\\n107\\\\\\\\t`view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n108\\\\\\\\t- mid-turn batches push zone past max → boundary unmoved; consecutive pulls byte-identical\\\\\\\\n109\\\\\\\\t- the `turn_end` batch commits → one advance; flipped set = whole oldest turns (every tool result in each evicted turn flipped together; assert per-turn all-or-nothing)\\\\\\\\n110\\\\\\\\t- next small turn closes under max → no movement\\\\\\\\n111\\\\\\\\t- turnless tool result (singleton group, DD-10) evicts whole-message\\\\\\\\n112\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n113\\\\\\\\t### TC-5.2 — Peek-ahead landing + config (AC-5.3, AC-5.4)\\\\\\\\n114\\\\\\\\t`view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` — golden **G2** (worked in the design): 30k/25k/20k/15k turns, 64k/32k → boundary after the 25k group, zone 35k\\\\\\\\n115\\\\\\\\t- landing always in [target, target + one turn): G2 plus a variant where evicting the next turn would land exactly at target (boundary advances through it — `≥` boundary-condition leg)\\\\\\\\n116\\\\\\\\t- newest closed turn never evicted even when alone over target (oversized-newest variant)\\\\\\\\n117\\\\\\\\t- `maxTokens ≤ targetTokens` → construction `TypeError` naming the constraint; defaults resolve 64k/32k\\\\\\\\n118\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n119\\\\\\\\t### TC-5.3 — Epic 03 contract regression under the new trigger (AC-5.5)\\\\\\\\n120\\\\\\\\t`view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + new file\\\\\\\\n121\\\\\\\\t- monotonic: flipped results stay flipped across later turn closes\\\\\\\\n122\\\\\\\\t- short form: summary-when-ready else truncation (existing assertions re-driven by turn-end trigger)\\\\\\\\n123\\\\\\\\t- compact reset: post-compact boundary = compact point, fresh full tail\\\\\\\\n124\\\\\\\\t- determinism: same record + budgets replayed → identical boundary trajectory (golden **G1**, re-cut for turn grouping)\\\\\\\\n125\\\\\\\\t- failure injection at `post-commit-advance` → intake unaffected, boundary unchanged, status shows over-budget zone, next turn close heals (Epic 03's failure-injection machinery — the `post-commit-advance` hook — retriggered)\\\\\\\\n126\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n127\\\\\\\\t### TC-6.1 — Deletion proof (AC-6.1, AC-6.2)\\\\\\\\n128\\\\\\\\t`retirement.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n129\\\\\\\\t- public-API surface snapshot: export-name set of the package entry equals the checked-in SDK-only list (no `resolveNamedProvider`, no `registeredProviderNames`)\\\\\\\\n130\\\\\\\\t- package manifest has no `bin`\\\\\\\\n131\\\\\\\\t- full default suite green is the suite run itself; the SDK-coverage comparison is a one-time story-completion check recorded in the story DoD (suite files and their domain-operation coverage unchanged from pre-deletion, process files excepted)\\\\\\\\n132\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n133\\\\\\\\t### TC-6.2 — Resolution path gone (AC-6.3)\\\\\\\\n134\\\\\\\\t`retirement.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n135\\\\\\\\t- source scan: zero `LHC_PROVIDER` / `--provider` references under `src/`\\\\\\\\n136\\\\\\\\t- constructing with neither `provider` nor `inference` → the XOR `TypeError` (no fallback resolution path exists to catch it)\\\\\\\\n137\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n138\\\\\\\\t## Epic 03 Test Amendment Ledger (Flow 5 / Chunk 6)\\\\\\\\n139\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n140\\\\\\\\tSpec-sanctioned amendments (the Epic 02 F-3 pattern: named, reasoned, red-manifest regenerated):\\\\\\\\n141\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n142\\\\\\\\t| Existing test (in `view-boundary.test.ts` / fixtures) | Change | Reason |\\\\\\\\n143\\\\\\\\t|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\n144\\\\\\\\t| floor-protection legs (newest-whole-message floor, oversized-newest-result, floor-blocks-target cases) | superseded → replaced by newest-turn-protection legs in TC-5.2 | `floorTokens` retired (AC-5.4, DD-11) |\\\\\\\\n145\\\\\\\\t| per-intake-advance legs (advance fires on non-turn-end batches) | inverted → become TC-5.1's mid-turn-never-moves assertions | trigger moved (AC-5.1) |\\\\\\\\n146\\\\\\\\t| message-grained eviction goldens (G1 as cut for Epic 03) | re-cut for turn grouping | eviction granularity (AC-5.2) |\\\\\\\\n147\\\\\\\\t| construction tests passing `floorTokens` | updated to two-field budgets; one new test: passing `floorTokens` is rejected as unknown config | config surface change |\\\\\\\\n148\\\\\\\\t| status `zoneTokens` legs | unchanged — shared-query invariant holds | none needed |\\\\\\\\n149\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n150\\\\\\\\tLifecycle/capstone deterministic legs (Epic 04's exercise) are untouched: they run the deterministic provider and never asserted boundary trigger timing.\\\\\\\\n151\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n152\\\\\\\\t## Red/Green per Chunk\\\\\\\\n153\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n154\\\\\\\\tHouse pattern: Red = failing tests + skeleton stubs returning structured not-implemented failures; `red-verify` (build, typecheck, lint, boundaries — no behavior tests); Green = implementation until the chunk's tests pass; immutability check on test files after Green.\\\\\\\\n155\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n156\\\\\\\\t| Chunk | Red writes | Green implements | Exit gate |\\\\\\\\n157\\\\\\\\t|-------|-----------|------------------|-----------|\\\\\\\\n158\\\\\\\\t| 0: Foundation | fixture self-conformance test; prompt-registry completeness test (against skeleton registry) | `inference/types.ts`, fixture builders, registry skeleton, provenance type additions | `red-verify` + fixture conformance green; boundaries check includes `inference/ ↛ domains/` |\\\\\\\\n159\\\\\\\\t| 1: CLI retirement | `retirement.test.ts` (snapshot list authored = the post-deletion expected set) | deletions, manifest, verify-script rewrite | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 green; full default suite green; DoD coverage comparison recorded |\\\\\\\\n160\\\\\\\\t| 2: Seam | `inference-construction`, `inference-routing` | XOR + validation in `sdk.ts`, adapter construction wiring | TC-1.1, TC-1.2 green |\\\\\\\\n161\\\\\\\\t| 3: Adapter + prompts | `inference-adapter`, `inference-prompts` + goldens | seven operations, templates, bounding, empty-output | TC-2.1–2.3 green |\\\\\\\\n162\\\\\\\\t| 4: Classification | `inference-classification` | table, `safeCall`, handler provenance copies | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 green; full Epic 02 derivation suites still green (machinery untouched proof) |\\\\\\\\n163\\\\\\\\t| 5: Real suite | `inference-real` (guard testable unkeyed) | OpenRouter fixture, accounting guard, capstone leg | unkeyed: not-ran record asserted in CI; keyed: TC-4.1–4.3 green locally (run recorded in story DoD with model used) |\\\\\\\\n164\\\\\\\\t| 6: Boundary | `view-boundary-turn-end` + amendment ledger applied | gate, grouping, peek-ahead, config change | TC-5.1–5.3 green; amended Epic 03 suite green; red-manifest regenerated |\\\\\\\\n165\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n166\\\\\\\\t**Story-completion accounting for Chunk 5:** the keyed run cannot gate CI (no secret in CI by design). The story's DoD requires a recorded keyed run — date, model, pass state — in the deviation/completion notes. A keyed run that has never happened blocks epic acceptance: the capstone is the integration-readiness gate, and an unexercised gate is the silent-skip failure mode this suite exists to prevent.\\\\\\\\n167\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n168\\\\\\\\t## Architecture-Risk Tests\\\\\\\\n169\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\n170\\\\\\\\t| Risk | Test |\\\\\\\\n171\\\\\\\\t|------|------|\\\\\\\\n172\\\\\\\\t| Adapter parses model output for mechanical facts | TC-2.1's adversarial canned text (claims wrong outcome; stamp must come from record) |\\\\\\\\n173\\\\\\\\t| Validation drifts from `FormKind` union | TC-1.1 parameterizes over the exported union; a new kind fails the matrix automatically |\\\\\\\\n174\\\\\\\\t| Queue machinery quietly forked for inference | Chunk 4 exit gate: Epic 02 derivation suites green untouched |\\\\\\\\n175\\\\\\\\t| Boundary decision and status sum diverge | shared-query invariant + TC-5.3's status leg after each advance |\\\\\\\\n176\\\\\\\\t| Silent real-suite skip | TC-4.1's unkeyed leg asserts the not-ran record exists and is not a pass |\\\\\\\\n177\\\\\\\\t| Behavior test deleted with process suites | TC-6.1 snapshot + full-suite green + DoD coverage comparison |\\\\\\\\n178\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\"}]},\\\\\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"session_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"uuid\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"f6b9fc74-c799-40c7-88e4-2283ea0d92c3\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"timestamp\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"2026-06-12T23:20:46.113Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool_use_result\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"file\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"filePath\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/test-plan.md\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"# Epic 05: Derivation Inference — Test Plan\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Epic:** `epic.md` (22 ACs / 15 TCs)\\\\\\\\n**Tech Design:** `tech-design.md`\\\\\\\\n**Coverage:** all 15 TCs mapped below; no TC unowned, none double-owned.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Suite Layout\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| File | Tier | TCs |\\\\\\\\n|------|------|-----|\\\\\\\\n| `test/inference-construction.test.ts` | default | TC-1.1 |\\\\\\\\n| `test/inference-routing.test.ts` | default | TC-1.2 |\\\\\\\\n| `test/inference-adapter.test.ts` | default | TC-2.1, TC-2.3 |\\\\\\\\n| `test/inference-prompts.test.ts` | default | TC-2.2 |\\\\\\\\n| `test/inference-classification.test.ts` | default | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 |\\\\\\\\n| `test/inference-real.test.ts` | opt-in (`LHC_OPENROUTER_KEY`) | TC-4.1, TC-4.2, TC-4.3 |\\\\\\\\n| `test/view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + `test/view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` (new) | default | TC-5.1, TC-5.2, TC-5.3 |\\\\\\\\n| `test/retirement.test.ts` | default | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDeleted with Flow 6: the twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` files. The `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate leaves `verify-all`; the opt-in inference suite self-reports inside the default runner (it is env-gated, not script-gated).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Fixture Contracts\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**`test/fixtures/model-call.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the scripted-host builders every Flow 1–3 test uses:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `recordingCall(responses: Record<FormKind, string>)` → `{ call: ModelCall, log: ModelCallInput[] }`. Resolves each call with the kind's canned text (kind inferred from the assignment the test built — in practice keyed by `model` string, which tests assign uniquely per kind). Every input pushed to `log`.\\\\\\\\n- `scriptedCall(script: ModelCallResult[])` → `ModelCall` returning script entries in order; throws if exhausted (a test calling more than scripted is a test bug, loudly).\\\\\\\\n- `throwingCall(error: Error)` → `ModelCall` that rejects/throws.\\\\\\\\n- `hangingCall()` → `ModelCall` that never settles (timeout leg; tests pass a small `timeoutMs`).\\\\\\\\n- `validAssignments(overrides?)` → a complete seven-kind assignment map for construction tests, each kind assigned a distinct fake provider/model string.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAll builders return contract-conformant shapes (AC-1.2); the conformance helper below runs against `recordingCall`'s output in its own test, so fixture drift from the contract is caught by the suite, not discovered in stories.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**`test/fixtures/openrouter-call.ts`** (Chunk 5) — the real host: `createOpenRouterCall(key, defaultModel)` → `ModelCall` over `fetch` to OpenRouter's OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint; implements the exact `ModelCall` contract (OpenRouter takes one model slug, so the fixture resolves the `provider`+`model` routing keys into that slug — the host-side interpretation AC-1.2 grants), maps HTTP 401/403 → `auth`, 429 → `rate_limit`, 400 → `invalid_request`, network errors → `network`, everything else → `other`, and extracts non-empty assistant text into the `ModelCallResult` success shape. `resolveRealSuiteEnv()` → `{ key, model } | { notRan: reason }`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**`test/fixtures/seam-conformance.ts`** (Chunk 0) — the parameterized contract assertions (DD-13): `assertModelCallContract(call)` (shape of success/failure results on a probe call) and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(call, assignments)` (TC-1.2's routing assertions extracted so TC-4.3 runs them against the real host unchanged).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Boundary fixtures** — `test/fixtures/` gains `seedTurnedToolResults(db, turns: { results: number[] }[])`: closed turns with tool results of given token estimates, plus turnless-result and open-turn variants. Built on the existing Epic 03 fixture helpers; token estimates seeded via content sized to the estimator (existing pattern from view-boundary tests).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## TC → Test Mapping\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-1.1 — Construction matrix (AC-1.1, AC-1.3)\\\\\\\\n`inference-construction.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- missing one kind (each of seven, parameterized) → `TypeError` naming the kind\\\\\\\\n- unknown prompt name → `TypeError` naming kind and prompt\\\\\\\\n- unknown kind key in assignments → `TypeError` naming it\\\\\\\\n- both `provider` and `inference` → `TypeError` naming the XOR rule\\\\\\\\n- neither → `TypeError` naming the XOR rule\\\\\\\\n- empty `provider`/`model` string in one assignment → `TypeError`\\\\\\\\n- complete valid config → constructs; a seeded drain lands a form `ready` (operates, not just constructs)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-1.2 — Routing (AC-1.2, AC-1.4)\\\\\\\\n`inference-routing.test.ts` — `recordingCall` + seven distinct lanes in `validAssignments`:\\\\\\\\n- seed a thread exercising all seven kinds; drain; assert `log` contains each kind's call carrying exactly its assigned provider/model strings\\\\\\\\n- every logged `messages` is single-turn shape (`system`+`user` roles only, string content)\\\\\\\\n- three-lane mixed config: per-call routing matches item kind with no cross-kind bleed\\\\\\\\n- `assertModelCallContract(recordingCall(...).call)` — fixture conformance leg\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-2.1 — Seven kinds land with canned content + provenance (AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.5)\\\\\\\\n`inference-adapter.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- drain seeded thread → all seven `FormKind`s `ready`; content equals the canned text (post-shaping)\\\\\\\\n- handler equivalence: the same seeded thread drained under the deterministic provider lands the same form rows (states, subjects) with marker content — proving handlers unchanged (AC-2.1)\\\\\\\\n- outcomes/receipts on tool and rendering forms match the record, not the canned text (canned text deliberately claims the wrong outcome; the stamp must disagree with it)\\\\\\\\n- each form's `metadata.provenance` equals its assignment `{ provider, model, prompt }`; deterministic-provider forms carry none\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-2.2 — Prompt-rendering goldens (AC-2.2, AC-2.3)\\\\\\\\n`inference-prompts.test.ts` — golden files in `test/goldens/prompts/`:\\\\\\\\n- for each of the seven templates: `render(fixtureInput)` matches its golden (`smoothing-v1.golden.json` …) — fixture content embedded, single-turn shape\\\\\\\\n- registry completeness: every config-selectable name resolves; `PROMPT_REGISTRY` covers all seven default names\\\\\\\\n- brief-summary stripping: drive `summarizeChunkBrief` through the adapter with a recording call; the rendered messages contain outcome tokens but no receipt text from the fixture (the Epic 02 stripping contract holds through the adapter)\\\\\\\\n- bounding (DD-7): a `summarizeToolResult` input over `maxInputChars` renders with head+tail+marker, under-limit input renders whole\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-2.3 — Empty output is a failure (AC-2.4)\\\\\\\\n`inference-adapter.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- `scriptedCall([{ ok: true, text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }, { ok: true, text: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"real\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" }])` → first attempt classifies `empty_output` retryable; retry lands `ready` with `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"real\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`\\\\\\\\n- exhaustion leg: all-whitespace script → form `failed`, reason `provider_failure`, last error names `empty_output`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-3.1 — Classification table drives retry/terminal (AC-3.1, AC-3.2)\\\\\\\\n`inference-classification.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- table assertion: `FAILURE_CLASSIFICATION` matches the AC-3.1 mapping exactly (data asserted as data)\\\\\\\\n- `rate_limit` ×2 then success → `ready`; attempts recorded on the path (report shows retrying mid-way under a paused clock, per existing Epic 02 clock-injection pattern)\\\\\\\\n- `auth` → `failed` first attempt; scripted call shows exactly one invocation; stable reason\\\\\\\\n- `network` to exhaustion → `failed`, `provider_failure`, `metadata.lastError` preserved\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-3.2 — Exception containment (AC-3.2, AC-3.3)\\\\\\\\n`inference-classification.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- `throwingCall` on one kind amid a multi-kind drain → that item retries as `other`; all other items complete; drain returns normally (no escape)\\\\\\\\n- exhaustion lands thrown message as last error\\\\\\\\n- `hangingCall` with `timeoutMs: 50` → classified `timeout`, drain continues (DD-6 leg)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-4.1 — Real round-trips + accounting (AC-4.1)\\\\\\\\n`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- keyed: each of seven kinds round-trips once → `ready`, non-empty, no marker pattern\\\\\\\\n- unkeyed (controlled: suite re-invoked with key stripped from env in a subprocess-free way — the guard function called directly with empty env) → `notRan` record with reason; assert the record's shape and that it is not a pass\\\\\\\\n- the suite-level guard emits exactly one ran/not-ran line into the run output\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-4.2 — Capstone (AC-4.2)\\\\\\\\n`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- Epic 04's lifecycle sequence against the real adapter: intake → drain → compact → pull → inspect → edit → rebuild → drain → compact → materialize\\\\\\\\n- structural assertions: every kind `ready` ≥ once; no marker pattern anywhere; provenance names the real model; mutation-cleared forms regenerate with content ≠ pre-edit content; second compact's view reflects post-edit content; health coherent at each checkpoint\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-4.3 — Contract conformance, both hosts (AC-1.2, AC-4.1)\\\\\\\\n`inference-real.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- `assertModelCallContract(openRouterCall)` and `assertRoutingThroughSdk(openRouterCall, realAssignments)` — the same helpers TC-1.2 ran against the fake, unchanged (DD-13)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-5.1 — Turn-end trigger + whole-turn eviction (AC-5.1, AC-5.2)\\\\\\\\n`view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- mid-turn batches push zone past max → boundary unmoved; consecutive pulls byte-identical\\\\\\\\n- the `turn_end` batch commits → one advance; flipped set = whole oldest turns (every tool result in each evicted turn flipped together; assert per-turn all-or-nothing)\\\\\\\\n- next small turn closes under max → no movement\\\\\\\\n- turnless tool result (singleton group, DD-10) evicts whole-message\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-5.2 — Peek-ahead landing + config (AC-5.3, AC-5.4)\\\\\\\\n`view-boundary-turn-end.test.ts` — golden **G2** (worked in the design): 30k/25k/20k/15k turns, 64k/32k → boundary after the 25k group, zone 35k\\\\\\\\n- landing always in [target, target + one turn): G2 plus a variant where evicting the next turn would land exactly at target (boundary advances through it — `≥` boundary-condition leg)\\\\\\\\n- newest closed turn never evicted even when alone over target (oversized-newest variant)\\\\\\\\n- `maxTokens ≤ targetTokens` → construction `TypeError` naming the constraint; defaults resolve 64k/32k\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-5.3 — Epic 03 contract regression under the new trigger (AC-5.5)\\\\\\\\n`view-boundary.test.ts` (amended) + new file\\\\\\\\n- monotonic: flipped results stay flipped across later turn closes\\\\\\\\n- short form: summary-when-ready else truncation (existing assertions re-driven by turn-end trigger)\\\\\\\\n- compact reset: post-compact boundary = compact point, fresh full tail\\\\\\\\n- determinism: same record + budgets replayed → identical boundary trajectory (golden **G1**, re-cut for turn grouping)\\\\\\\\n- failure injection at `post-commit-advance` → intake unaffected, boundary unchanged, status shows over-budget zone, next turn close heals (Epic 03's failure-injection machinery — the `post-commit-advance` hook — retriggered)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-6.1 — Deletion proof (AC-6.1, AC-6.2)\\\\\\\\n`retirement.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- public-API surface snapshot: export-name set of the package entry equals the checked-in SDK-only list (no `resolveNamedProvider`, no `registeredProviderNames`)\\\\\\\\n- package manifest has no `bin`\\\\\\\\n- full default suite green is the suite run itself; the SDK-coverage comparison is a one-time story-completion check recorded in the story DoD (suite files and their domain-operation coverage unchanged from pre-deletion, process files excepted)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### TC-6.2 — Resolution path gone (AC-6.3)\\\\\\\\n`retirement.test.ts`\\\\\\\\n- source scan: zero `LHC_PROVIDER` / `--provider` references under `src/`\\\\\\\\n- constructing with neither `provider` nor `inference` → the XOR `TypeError` (no fallback resolution path exists to catch it)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Epic 03 Test Amendment Ledger (Flow 5 / Chunk 6)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSpec-sanctioned amendments (the Epic 02 F-3 pattern: named, reasoned, red-manifest regenerated):\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Existing test (in `view-boundary.test.ts` / fixtures) | Change | Reason |\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\n| floor-protection legs (newest-whole-message floor, oversized-newest-result, floor-blocks-target cases) | superseded → replaced by newest-turn-protection legs in TC-5.2 | `floorTokens` retired (AC-5.4, DD-11) |\\\\\\\\n| per-intake-advance legs (advance fires on non-turn-end batches) | inverted → become TC-5.1's mid-turn-never-moves assertions | trigger moved (AC-5.1) |\\\\\\\\n| message-grained eviction goldens (G1 as cut for Epic 03) | re-cut for turn grouping | eviction granularity (AC-5.2) |\\\\\\\\n| construction tests passing `floorTokens` | updated to two-field budgets; one new test: passing `floorTokens` is rejected as unknown config | config surface change |\\\\\\\\n| status `zoneTokens` legs | unchanged — shared-query invariant holds | none needed |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nLifecycle/capstone deterministic legs (Epic 04's exercise) are untouched: they run the deterministic provider and never asserted boundary trigger timing.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Red/Green per Chunk\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nHouse pattern: Red = failing tests + skeleton stubs returning structured not-implemented failures; `red-verify` (build, typecheck, lint, boundaries — no behavior tests); Green = implementation until the chunk's tests pass; immutability check on test files after Green.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Chunk | Red writes | Green implements | Exit gate |\\\\\\\\n|-------|-----------|------------------|-----------|\\\\\\\\n| 0: Foundation | fixture self-conformance test; prompt-registry completeness test (against skeleton registry) | `inference/types.ts`, fixture builders, registry skeleton, provenance type additions | `red-verify` + fixture conformance green; boundaries check includes `inference/ ↛ domains/` |\\\\\\\\n| 1: CLI retirement | `retirement.test.ts` (snapshot list authored = the post-deletion expected set) | deletions, manifest, verify-script rewrite | TC-6.1, TC-6.2 green; full default suite green; DoD coverage comparison recorded |\\\\\\\\n| 2: Seam | `inference-construction`, `inference-routing` | XOR + validation in `sdk.ts`, adapter construction wiring | TC-1.1, TC-1.2 green |\\\\\\\\n| 3: Adapter + prompts | `inference-adapter`, `inference-prompts` + goldens | seven operations, templates, bounding, empty-output | TC-2.1–2.3 green |\\\\\\\\n| 4: Classification | `inference-classification` | table, `safeCall`, handler provenance copies | TC-3.1, TC-3.2 green; full Epic 02 derivation suites still green (machinery untouched proof) |\\\\\\\\n| 5: Real suite | `inference-real` (guard testable unkeyed) | OpenRouter fixture, accounting guard, capstone leg | unkeyed: not-ran record asserted in CI; keyed: TC-4.1–4.3 green locally (run recorded in story DoD with model used) |\\\\\\\\n| 6: Boundary | `view-boundary-turn-end` + amendment ledger applied | gate, grouping, peek-ahead, config change | TC-5.1–5.3 green; amended Epic 03 suite green; red-manifest regenerated |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Story-completion accounting for Chunk 5:** the keyed run cannot gate CI (no secret in CI by design). The story's DoD requires a recorded keyed run — date, model, pass state — in the deviation/completion notes. A keyed run that has never happened blocks epic acceptance: the capstone is the integration-readiness gate, and an unexercised gate is the silent-skip failure mode this suite exists to prevent.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Architecture-Risk Tests\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Risk | Test |\\\\\\\\n|------|------|\\\\\\\\n| Adapter parses model output for mechanical facts | TC-2.1's adversarial canned text (claims wrong outcome; stamp must come from record) |\\\\\\\\n| Validation drifts from `FormKind` union | TC-1.1 parameterizes over the exported union; a new kind fails the matrix automatically |\\\\\\\\n| Queue machinery quietly forked for inference | Chunk 4 exit gate: Epic 02 derivation suites green untouched |\\\\\\\\n| Boundary decision and status sum diverge | shared-query invariant + TC-5.3's status leg after each advance |\\\\\\\\n| Silent real-suite skip | TC-4.1's unkeyed leg asserts the not-ran record exists and is not a pass |\\\\\\\\n| Behavior test deleted with process suites | TC-6.1 snapshot + full-suite green + DoD coverage comparison |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"numLines\\\\\\\":178,\\\\\\\"startLine\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\"totalLines\\\\\\\":178}}}\\\\ndocs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:59:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"stream_event\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"event\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"content_block_delta\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"index\\\\\\\":2,\\\\\\\"delta\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"input_json_delta\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"partial_json\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/ 2>&1\\\\\\\"}},\\\\\\\"session_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"7428c05c-eec6-4310-9738-8828ad5ff5b2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"uuid\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"167f7ff0-ec8e-4200-9339-13ad4739119d\\\\\\\"}\\\\ndocs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/streams/003-implementor.stdout.log:63:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"assistant\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"claude-fable-5\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"msg_01J5NY4cn936vdhk5eLsqqLW\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"assistant\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool_use\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"toolu_015AyEoSi5Qg5XwaA21HtepY\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Bash\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"input\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ls /Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/test/ /Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/test/fixtures/ /Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/artifacts/05-real-inference-suite-and-capstone/ 2>&1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"List test dir and story artifacts\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\"caller\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"direct\\